al angles.\n\nIt's important to realize that this is an identity, *not* an equation, which means that it is true for *all* values of $x$." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186060899 1064 "The derivatives of sine, cosine, and tangent are shown in the table below:\n\n| $f(x)$ | $f'(x)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\sin x$ | $\\cos x$ |\n| $\\cos x$ | $-\\sin x$ |\n| $\\tan x$ | $\\sec^2 x$ |\n\nNow that we know the derivatives of the standard trigonometric functions, we can differentiate any combination of them by using the properties of differentiation.\n\nFor example, let's find the derivative of $y = \\cos x + \\sin x.$\n\nHere, we can use the fact that the derivative of the sum is equal to the sum of the derivatives, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}x+\\operatorname{sin}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We'll derive the derivatives shown in the table at the end of this lesson." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186060899 1064 "The derivatives of sine, cosine, and tangent are shown in the table below:\n\n| $f(x)$ | $f'(x)$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $\\sin x$ | $\\cos x$ |\n| $\\cos x$ | $-\\sin x$ |\n| $\\tan x$ | $\\sec^2 x$ |\n\nNow that we know the derivatives of the standard trigonometric functions, we can differentiate any combination of them by using the properties of differentiation.\n\nFor example, let's find the derivative of\n\n$$\ny = \\cos x + \\sin x\n$$\n\nHere, we can use the fact that the derivative of the sum is equal to the sum of the derivatives, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\cos x + \\sin x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\cos x) + \\frac{d}{dx}(\\sin x) \\\\\n&=-\\sin x + \\cos x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We'll derive the derivatives shown in the table at the end of this lesson." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186060900 1064 "To understand why the derivative of sine is cosine, we need to use the definition of the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}} {\\textrm{d}x} (\\sin x) =  \\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0}\\dfrac{\\sin(x+h) - \\sin x}{h}\n$$\n\nNow, the so-called **addition formula for sine** states that\n\n$$\n\\sin(x+h) = \\sin(x)\\cos(h)+\\cos (x)  \\sin(h).\n$$\n\nBy using the addition formula for sine, we can rewrite our expression for the derivative as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}x) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(x)\\operatorname{cos}(h)+\\operatorname{sin}(h)\\operatorname{cos}(x)-\\operatorname{sin}(x)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(x)(\\operatorname{cos}(h)-1)+\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\operatorname{sin}(h)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\operatorname{sin}(x)\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}(h)-1)}{h}+\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(h)}{h}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}(x){{{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}(h)-1)}{h}}_{⏟}}_{0}+\\operatorname{cos}(x){{{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(h)}{h}}_{⏟}}_{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the last step, we used two important results for trigonometric limits:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{1 - \\cos(h)}{h}=0 \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad \\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{\\sin(h)}{h}=1.\n$$\n\nDeriving these results requires separate discussions, which we'll leave to future topics." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186060900 1064 "To understand why the derivative of sine is cosine, we need to use the definition of the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}} {\\textrm{d}x} (\\sin x) = \\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0}\\dfrac{\\sin(x+h) - \\sin x}{h}\n$$\n\nNow, the so-called **addition formula for sine** states that\n\n$$\n\\sin(x+h) = \\sin(x)\\cos(h)+\\cos (x) \\sin(h)\n$$\n\nBy using the addition formula for sine, we can rewrite our expression for the derivative as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\sin x) &= lim_(h → 0)(\\sin (x)\\cos (h) + \\sin (h)\\cos (x) - \\sin (x))/(h) \\\\\n&= lim_(h → 0)(\\sin (x)(\\cos (h) - 1) + \\cos (x)\\sin (h))/(h) \\\\\n&= lim_(h → 0)(\\sin (x)((\\cos (h) - 1))/(h) + \\cos (x)(\\sin (h))/(h)) \\\\\n&= \\sin (x)lim_(h → 0)((\\cos (h) - 1))/(h)_(⏟)_(0) + \\cos (x)lim_(h → 0)(\\sin (h))/(h)_(⏟)_(1) \\\\\n&= \\cos (x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the last step, we used two important results for trigonometric limits:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{1 - \\cos(h)}{h}=0 \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad \\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{\\sin(h)}{h}=1\n$$\n\nDeriving these results requires separate discussions, which we'll leave to future topics." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186060901 1064 "We can follow the same method for the cosine and use the definition of the derivative to show that\n\n$$\n(\\cos x)'=-\\sin x\n$$\n\nFirst, we start with the definition of the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}} {\\textrm{d}x} (\\cos x) = \\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0}\\dfrac{\\cos(x+h) - \\cos x}{h}\n$$\n\nNow, the so-called **addition formula for cosine** states that\n\n$$\n\\cos(x+h) = \\cos(x)\\cos(h)- \\sin(x)\\sin(h)\n$$\n\nUsing the addition formula for cosine, we can rewrite our expression for the derivative as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\cos x) &= lim_(h → 0)(\\cos (x)\\cos (h) - \\sin (x)\\sin (h) - \\cos (x))/(h) \\\\\n&= lim_(h → 0)(\\cos (x)(\\cos (h) - 1) - \\sin (x)\\sin (h))/(h) \\\\\n&= \\cos (x)lim_(h → 0)(\\cos (h) - 1)/(h)_(⏟)_(0) - \\sin (x)lim_(h → 0)(\\sin (h))/(h)_(⏟)_(1) \\\\\n&=-\\sin (x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnce again, in the last step, we used the special limits for sine and cosine." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186060901 1064 "We can follow the same method for the cosine and use the definition of the derivative to show that $(\\cos x)'=-\\sin x.$ First, we start with the definition of the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}} {\\textrm{d}x} (\\cos x) =  \\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0}\\dfrac{\\cos(x+h) - \\cos x}{h}\n$$\n\nNow, the so-called **addition formula for cosine** states that\n\n$$\n\\cos(x+h) = \\cos(x)\\cos(h)- \\sin(x)\\sin(h).\n$$\n\nUsing the addition formula for cosine, we can rewrite our expression for the derivative as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}x) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\operatorname{cos}(h)-\\operatorname{sin}(x)\\operatorname{sin}(h)-\\operatorname{cos}(x)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}(x)(\\operatorname{cos}(h)-1)-\\operatorname{sin}(x)\\operatorname{sin}(h)}{h} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}(x){{{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}(h)-1}{h}}_{⏟}}_{0}-\\operatorname{sin}(x){{{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(h)}{h}}_{⏟}}_{1} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnce again, in the last step, we used the special limits for sine and cosine." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186060902 1064 "We can also prove that\n\n$$\n(\\tan x)'= \\sec^2 x\n$$\n\nusing limits, though it's a bit cumbersome.\n\nA faster way is to use a rule of differentiation known as the **quotient rule**.\n\nYou'll learn about the quotient rule very soon!" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186060902 1064 "We can also prove that $(\\tan x)'= \\sec^2 x$ using limits, though it's a bit cumbersome. \n\nA faster way is to use a rule of differentiation known as the **quotient rule**. \n\nYou'll learn about the quotient rule very soon!" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186060903 1064 "The **second derivative**  is the derivative of a function's first derivative. It is written as $f''(x).$\n\nTo calculate the second derivative of a function $f(x)$, we first need to calculate the first derivative $f'(x).$ After that, we calculate the derivative of $f'(x).$ So using prime $\\left('\\right)$ notation, we can define the second derivative of a function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf''(x) = (f'(x))'.\n$$\n\nLet's try it out with the function $f(x) = 4x^2 + 3x.$ The first derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =(4{x}^{2}+3x)^{'} \\\\  & =4(2)x+3 \\\\  & =8x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now calculate the derivative of $f'(x)$ to give us $f''(x).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{''}(x) & =({f}^{'}(x))^{'} \\\\  & =(8x+3)^{'} \\\\  & =8+0 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the second derivative of $f(x)$ is $8.$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186060903 1064 "The **second derivative** is the derivative of a function's first derivative. It is written as $f''(x)$.\n\nTo calculate the second derivative of a function $f(x)$, we first need to calculate the first derivative $f'(x)$. After that, we calculate the derivative of $f'(x)$. So using prime\n\n$$\n\\left('\\right)\n$$\n\nnotation, we can define the second derivative of a function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf''(x) = (f'(x))'\n$$\n\nLet's try it out with the function\n\n$$\nf(x) = 4x^2 + 3x\n$$\n\nThe first derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= (4x^{2} + 3x)^{′} \\\\\n&= 4(2)x + 3 \\\\\n&= 8x + 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now calculate the derivative of $f'(x)$ to give us $f''(x)$. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{″}(x) &= (f^{′}(x))^{′} \\\\\n&= (8x + 3)^{′} \\\\\n&= 8 + 0 \\\\\n&= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second derivative of $f(x)$ is $8$." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186060904 1064 "We can also express the second derivative using\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d} x}\n$$\n\nnotation, also known as Leibnitz notation. If we have a function $f(x)$, then the second derivative $f''(x)$ can be written as\n\n$$\nf''(x) = \\dfrac {\\textrm d^2 f}{\\textrm d x^2} = \\dfrac {\\textrm d} {\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac {\\textrm df} {\\textrm d x}\\right)\n$$\n\nSometimes, we define a function as $y=f(x)$, in which case we could also express the second derivative as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186060904 1064 "We can also express the second derivative using $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d} x}$ notation, also known as Leibniz notation. If we have a function $f(x),$ then the second derivative $f''(x)$ can be written as\n\n$$\nf''(x) = \\dfrac {\\textrm d^2 f}{\\textrm d x^2} = \\dfrac {\\textrm d} {\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac {\\textrm df} {\\textrm d x}\\right).\n$$\n\nSometimes, we define a function as $y=f(x),$ in which case we could also express the second derivative as $\\dfrac {\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2}.$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186060904 1067 "Using Leibnitz Notation" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186060904 1067 "Using Leibniz Notation" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186060905 1064 "To calculate the third derivative of the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=2x^4+3x\n$$\n\nwe need to differentiate it three times.\n\nComputing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(2x^{4} + 3x) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 4x^{4 - 1} + 1 \\cdot 3x^{1 - 1} \\\\\n&= 8x^{3} + 3x^{0} \\\\\n&= 8x^{3} + 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{″}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(8x^{3} + 3) \\\\\n&= 8 \\cdot 3x^{3 - 1} + 0 \\\\\n&= 24x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, computing the third derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{‴}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(24x^{2}) \\\\\n&= 24 \\cdot 2x^{2 - 1} \\\\\n&= 48x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third derivative of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nf'''(x)=48x\n$$\n\nUsing Leibnitz notation, the third derivative of a function $f(x)$ can also be written as\n\n$$\nf'''(x)=\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(f(x))\\right)\\right)=\\dfrac {\\textrm d^3}{\\textrm d x^3}f(x)\n$$\n\nIn general, if we want to calculate the $n$-th order derivative of a function, we need to differentiate it $n$ times:\n\n$$\nf^{(n)}(x)=\\dfrac {\\textrm d^n}{\\textrm d x^n}f(x)\n$$\n\n**Note**: The parenthesis in the superscript of $f^{(n)}(x)$ means that it is the the $n$-th derivative of $f(x)$. This is to distinguish it from $f^n(x)$ with no parenthesis, which indicates the $n$-th power of $f(x)$." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186060905 1064 "To calculate the third derivative of the function $f(x)=2x^4+3x,$ we need to differentiate it three times.\n\nComputing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{4}+3x) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4{x}^{4-1}+1\\cdot 3{x}^{1-1} \\\\ \\text{ } & =8{x}^{3}+3{x}^{0} \\\\  & =8{x}^{3}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{''}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8{x}^{3}+3) \\\\  & =8\\cdot 3{x}^{3-1}+0 \\\\ \\text{ } & =24{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, computing the third derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(24{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =24\\cdot 2{x}^{2-1} \\\\ \\text{ } & =48x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third derivative of $f(x)$ is $f'''(x)=48x.$ \n\nUsing Leibniz notation, the third derivative of a function $f(x)$ can also be written as\n\n$$\nf'''(x)=\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(f(x))\\right)\\right)=\\dfrac {\\textrm d^3}{\\textrm d x^3}f(x).\n$$\n\nIn general, if we want to calculate the $n$-th order derivative of a function, we need to differentiate it $n$ times:\n\n$$\nf^{(n)}(x)=\\dfrac {\\textrm d^n}{\\textrm d x^n}f(x).\n$$\n\n**Note**: The parenthesis in the superscript of $f^{(n)}(x)$ means that it is the the $n$-th derivative of $f(x).$ This is to distinguish it from $f^n(x)$ with no parenthesis, which indicates the $n$-th power of $f(x).$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186060937 1064 "The **determinant** of a general $2 \\times 2$ matrix\n$[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]$\nis denoted $\\text {det} ({A})$ and computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, given the matrix\n$[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ \nthe determinant is\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = {\\color{blue}{2}} \\cdot {\\color{blue}{3}} - {\\color{red}{1}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{5}} = 1.\n$$\n\nTo remember how to compute the determinant of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, you can imagine taking the product of the diagonal of the matrix, and then subtracting the product of the off-diagonal terms, as follows:\n\n![](images/25/2594f99e0f02241dc6d01f85c82923103df1d6fb4dda5ca03d438637f5178a28.png)\n\nLater, we will see that the determinant is related to the \"volume\" spanned by the row vectors or column vectors in a matrix. We will also see that the determinant can tell us about many other properties of a matrix. \n\nHowever, in this lesson, we will just focus on computing the determinant." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186060937 1064 "The **determinant** of a general $2 \\times 2$ matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}$\nis denoted $\\text {det} ({A})$ and computed as\n$\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFor example, given the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nthe determinant is\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = {\\color{blue}{2}} \\cdot {\\color{blue}{3}} - {\\color{red}{1}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{5}} = 1\n$$\n\nTo remember how to compute the determinant of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, you can imagine taking the product of the diagonal of the matrix, and then subtracting the product of the off-diagonal terms, as follows:\n\n![](images/25/2594f99e0f02241dc6d01f85c82923103df1d6fb4dda5ca03d438637f5178a28.png)\n\nLater, we will see that the determinant is related to the \"volume\" spanned by the row vectors or column vectors in a matrix. We will also see that the determinant can tell us about many other properties of a matrix.\n\nHowever, in this lesson, we will just focus on computing the determinant." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186060959 1064 "To divide one complex number by another, we set up a fraction and then multiply both the numerator and the denominator by the complex conjugate of the denominator.\n\nFor example, to divide $2 - \\textrm{i}$ by $1 + 3\\textrm{i}$, we start by setting up a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {2 - \\textrm{i}} {1 + 3\\textrm{i}}\n$$\n\nThe denominator is $1 + 3\\textrm{i}$ and its conjugate is $1 - 3\\textrm{i}$.\n\nWe multiply both the numerator and denominator by $1 - 3\\textrm{i}$, so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac {2 - \\textrm{i}} {1 + 3\\textrm{i}} \\cdot \\dfrac {1 - 3\\textrm{i}} {1 - 3\\textrm{i}} &= \\dfrac {2 - 6\\textrm{i} -\\textrm{i} + 3\\textrm{i}^2} {1 - 3\\textrm{i} + 3\\textrm{i} - 9\\textrm{i}^2} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {2 - 7\\textrm{i} + 3(-1)} {1 - 9(-1)} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {2 - 7\\textrm{i} - 3} {1 + 9} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {-1 - 7\\textrm{i}} {10} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b \\textrm{i}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac {-1 - 7\\textrm{i}} {10} = -\\dfrac{1} {10} - \\dfrac {7} {10} \\textrm{i} \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186060959 1064 "To divide one complex number by another, we set up a fraction and then multiply both the numerator and the denominator by the complex conjugate of the denominator.\n\nFor example, to divide $2 - \\textrm{i}$ by $1 + 3\\textrm{i},$ we start by setting up a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {2 - \\textrm{i}} {1 + 3\\textrm{i}}\n$$\n\nThe denominator is $1 + 3\\textrm{i}$ and its conjugate is $1 - 3\\textrm{i}.$\n\nWe multiply both the numerator and denominator by $1 - 3\\textrm{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {2 - \\textrm{i}} {1 + 3\\textrm{i}} \\cdot \\dfrac {1 - 3\\textrm{i}} {1 - 3\\textrm{i}} \n&= \\dfrac {2 - 6\\textrm{i} -\\textrm{i}  + 3\\textrm{i}^2} {1 - 3\\textrm{i} + 3\\textrm{i} - 9\\textrm{i}^2}  \\[5pt]\n&=\t\t\\dfrac {2 - 7\\textrm{i}  + 3(-1)} {1 - 9(-1)}  \\[5pt]\n&= \t\t\\dfrac {2 - 7\\textrm{i}  - 3} {1 + 9}  \\[5pt]\n&= \t\t\\dfrac {-1 - 7\\textrm{i}} {10} \n\t}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b \\textrm{i}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {-1 - 7\\textrm{i}} {10} = \n\t\t-\\dfrac{1} {10} - \\dfrac {7} {10} \\textrm{i}\n\t}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186060960 1064 "We can combine vertical reflections, stretches, and shifts to get new transformed trigonometric curves. For example, let's draw the graph of the curve\n\n![](images/99/99f8d7bb60d08d48e8c2d757944053ac893d4f8848d8bd9cfa30daed617a742e.svg)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of ![](images/dc/dc5febeafd17e45544875b7479eaf66ff4f56d739a1794b204aac280765ab860.svg)\n\n![](images/6d/6d3c6289759e7b34c6ec8490ea1add8af6b7d4cb02fb37b7669bd5d96e7d0ffd.png)\n\n- Then, stretch it parallel to the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis by a stretch factor of ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) to give ![](images/a5/a5cc13d6ecde342df38cd8e3c27f9078a1aa9922fe0630ce7e8bb91293303d48.svg)\n\n![](images/7c/7c35f875500803e5fb9ea094831a7535c0ca367125bb006b3999dc113988785d.png)\n\n- Next, reflect it over the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis to give ![](images/ca/caf66236d26007222af32f9c7a74de04b8abfdd0c5e85ebae3b2263dc2b76de9.svg)\n\n![](images/7d/7d1184b7bab8eb3dbd2c99c5e16094809d4cef09a73522c4fcdce4941df4307d.png)\n\n- Finally, shift it **down** by ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) unit to give ![](images/99/99f8d7bb60d08d48e8c2d757944053ac893d4f8848d8bd9cfa30daed617a742e.svg)\n\n![](images/aa/aaa0f414eff732b89a1c9c74ec0fe286210e3e1aea12063635e235d1b0c658a1.png)" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186060960 1064 "We can combine vertical reflections, stretches, and shifts to get new transformed trigonometric curves. For example, let's draw the graph of the curve\n\n$$\ny = -2 \\cos x - 1\n$$\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\cos{x}$.\n\n![](images/6d/6d3c6289759e7b34c6ec8490ea1add8af6b7d4cb02fb37b7669bd5d96e7d0ffd.png)\n\n- Then, stretch it parallel to the $y$-axis by a stretch factor of $2$ to give $y=2\\cos{x}$.\n\n![](images/cc/cc24b259222f447a6ba50886b6df7e808725e485082421f164b2ff95ef25c60f.png)\n\n- Next, reflect it over the $x$-axis to give $y=-2\\cos x$.\n\n![](images/f1/f130b3688a98f155e65178a716101596b93802bad10d57142d690404aa004ba3.png)\n\n- Finally, shift it **down** by $1$ unit to give $y=-2\\cos x - 1$.\n\n![](images/7a/7ad2238407708ac5bff793590dbffa16ed80ff270b22bba2e83b4efcd6fe7c16.png)" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186061002 1064 "Suppose we know that the point $( - 1 , 2 )$ lies on the graph of $y = f ( x ).$ What point *must* lie on the graph of $y = - f ( x ) + 3?$ This problem can be solved using one of two methods.\n\n**Method 1 - Transform the Point Using Graph Transformations**\n\nTo plot $y = -f(x) + 3,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the graph across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,2)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1,-(2)) = (-1,-2).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result by $3$ units up and obtain the graph of $y = -f(x) + 3.$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,-2)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1,-2+3)={\\color{blue}(-1,1)}.\n$$\n\n**Method 2 - Evaluate the Transformed Function**\n\nSince the point $(-1, 2)$ lies on the graph of $y = f(x),$ we have that $f(-1) = 2.$\n\nNow, consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+3.$ Substituting $x = {\\color{blue}-1}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(-1)+3 \\\\  & =-(2)+3 \\\\  & =-2+3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $\\color{blue}(-1, 1)$ must lie on $y = -f(x) + 3.$\n\nSo, both methods give the same result." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186061002 1064 "Suppose we know that the point $(-1, 2)$ lies on the graph of $y = f (x)$. What point *must* lie on the graph of\n\n$$\ny =-f (x) + 3\n$$\n\nThis problem can be solved using one of two methods.\n\n**Method 1 - Transform the Point Using Graph Transformations**\n\nTo plot\n\n$$\ny = -f(x) + 3\n$$\n\nwe apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$.\n- Then, we reflect the graph across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x)$. After this transformation, the point $(-1,2)$ will be mapped to the point\n$(-1,-(2)) = (-1,-2)$.\n- Finally, we shift the result by $3$ units up and obtain the graph of $y = -f(x) + 3$. After this transformation, the point $(-1,-2)$ will be mapped to the point\n$(-1,-2+3)={\\color{blue}(-1,1)}$.\n\n**Method 2 - Evaluate the Transformed Function**\n\nSince the point $(-1, 2)$ lies on the graph of $y = f(x)$, we have that\n\n$$\nf(-1) = 2\n$$\n\nNow, consider the graph of\n\n$$\ny=-f(x)+3\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nx = {\\color{blue}-1}\n$$\n\n, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= -f(-1) + 3 \\\\\n&= - (2) + 3 \\\\\n&=-2 + 3 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $\\color{blue}(-1, 1)$ must lie on\n\n$$\ny = -f(x) + 3\n$$\n\nSo, both methods give the same result." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186061004 1064 "Consider the function $y=f(x)$ shown below.\n\n![](images/af/afe8baa8375ceaf4974b62549e4f073d0e6cecfcfc1a50a91200421296141ef1.png)\n\nThis function has a local minimum point, and a local maximum point, as shown.\n\nNotice that if we perform the graph transformation $y=f(2x)-1$, then\n\n- the local *maximum* point on the original graph is mapped to a local *maximum* point on the transformed curve, and\n- the local *minimum* point on the original graph is mapped to a local *minimum* point on the transformed curve.\n\n![](images/cd/cda6d2c00de091df0f4df03d4b63019fd7c5681159cff65ea25e7518f5e263cd.png)\n\nSo, this transformation maps maximums (local and global) to maximums and minimums (local and global) to minimums.\n\nIs this always the case? In other words, does *any* graph transformation map maximums to maximums and minimums to minimums?\n\nTo answer this, let's consider another example." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186061004 1064 "Consider the function $y=f(x)$ shown below. \n\n![](images/af/afe8baa8375ceaf4974b62549e4f073d0e6cecfcfc1a50a91200421296141ef1.png)\n\nThis function has a local minimum point, and a local maximum point, as shown. \n\nNotice that if we perform the graph transformation $y=f(2x)-1,$ then \n\n- the local *maximum* point on the original graph is mapped to a local *maximum* point on the transformed curve, and\n\n- the local *minimum* point on the original graph is mapped to a local *minimum* point on the transformed curve.\n\n![](images/cd/cda6d2c00de091df0f4df03d4b63019fd7c5681159cff65ea25e7518f5e263cd.png)\n\nSo, this transformation maps maximums (local and global) to maximums and minimums (local and global) to minimums. \n\nIs this always the case? In other words, does *any* graph transformation map maximums to maximums and minimums to minimums? \n\nTo answer this, let's consider another example." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186061005 1064 "Consider the transformation\n\n$$\ny=-f(x)+2\n$$\n\nwhich contains a reflection about the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/7f/7f4041c17b278b0e40e31abbd98e3c47a8462b14a6109123d600c2db0e5d6f36.png)\n\nNotice that\n\n- the local *maximum* point on the original graph is mapped to a local ***minimum*** point on the transformed curve, and\n- the local *minimum* point on the original graph is mapped to a local ***maximum*** point on the transformed curve.\n\nSo, due to the presence of vertical reflection, this transformation maps maximums (local and global) to minimums and minimums (local and global) to maximums." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186061005 1064 "Consider the transformation $y=-f(x)+2,$ which contains a reflection about the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/7f/7f4041c17b278b0e40e31abbd98e3c47a8462b14a6109123d600c2db0e5d6f36.png)\n\nNotice that \n\n- the local *maximum* point on the original graph is mapped to a local ** *minimum* ** point on the transformed curve, and\n\n- the local *minimum* point on the original graph is mapped to a local ** *maximum* ** point on the transformed curve.\n\nSo, due to the presence of vertical reflection, this transformation maps maximums (local and global) to minimums and minimums (local and global) to maximums." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186061006 1064 "We can summarize our findings by the following theorem:\n\n> *If a transformation does **not** contain a reflection across the $x$-axis, then the transformation maps local (or global) maximums to local (or global) maximums, and maps local (or global) minimums to local (or global) minimums. *\n\nIn the case where we do have a vertical reflection, we have the following theorem.\n\n> *If a transformation contains a reflection across the $x$-axis, then the transformation maps local (or global) maximums to local (or global) minimums, and maps local (or global) minimums to local (or global) maximums. *" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186061006 1064 "We can summarize our findings by the following theorem:\n\n> *If a transformation does **not** contain a reflection across the $x$-axis, then the transformation maps local (or global) maximums to local (or global) maximums, and maps local (or global) minimums to local (or global) minimums.*\n\nIn the case where we do have a vertical reflection, we have the following theorem.\n\n> *If a transformation contains a reflection across the $x$-axis, then the transformation maps local (or global) maximums to local (or global) minimums, and maps local (or global) minimums to local (or global) maximums.*" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186065341 1064 "The chain rule can be used to differentiate trigonometric functions like\n\n$$\ny = \\sin (2x)\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that this function can be written as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = \\sin u, \\qquad u = 2x\n$$\n\nWe can use the chain rule to compute the derivative of this composite function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{dy}{du} \\cdot \\frac{du}{dx} \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{du}(\\sin u) \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(2x) \\\\\n&= \\cos u \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 2\\cos u\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $u=2x$, we obtain the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= 2\\cos (2x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186065341 1064 "The chain rule can be used to differentiate trigonometric functions like \n\n$$\ny = \\sin (2x).\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that this function can be written as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = \\sin u, \\qquad u = 2x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the chain rule to compute the derivative of this composite function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\operatorname{sin}u)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}u\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $u=2x,$ we obtain the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2\\operatorname{cos}(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186065374 1064 "Remember that the derivative of a function ![](images/93/93f888a1ee0b7da5e29f442f803f1c0eaf73ad02f5eaa97e85bb8a35d607015b.svg) represents the slope of the tangent line to the curve ![](images/e2/e2ed4a9881134f510823ba710f835acf5720b866f0df86fa6cd24448524431d6.svg)\n\nFor example, consider the curve ![](images/2e/2eb046be7e52e5a72d29a1333f9cf483411f0a8d6d897fe53014cc1952c6f759.svg), shown below. If we want to compute the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point ![](images/3a/3a613cb22e7bfd4038442b830babc21756061f5b18d1b1969451c8403d2894a3.svg) then we compute ![](images/a5/a54bfe86ff259aa5b62b72de2f050ee0c2818056cbc4bd30385c5faab112f71d.svg) and evaluate ![](images/91/910d575ac485dde82a562406c34e5e80677b10d5fb0e38b3370558d22badc908.svg)\n\n![](images/63/63f675532755376a733f2f0cad2b40e55f19b3278c1bcc5e8cb355fc6bdac277.png)\n\nIf ![](images/01/01ced1892dd48d3cbba7806664f3a1d763ef692dd88e5bf031d84db220141410.svg) then ![](images/a9/a9460f8b0abe06f33ff168a2a1887572f1c282de78c982c3af65c1c0670b9818.svg) by the power rule. Substituting ![](images/df/df9822243c11ec9f0fabca5cc578976d0bdea2c521b4f61964f5f3850a970504.svg) into ![](images/a5/a54bfe86ff259aa5b62b72de2f050ee0c2818056cbc4bd30385c5faab112f71d.svg) gives\n![](images/e0/e046ab7a11915ca797bd74e8e304484fe46ecaf4d2f19429409a4a8c6a3b1456.svg)\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve ![](images/2e/2eb046be7e52e5a72d29a1333f9cf483411f0a8d6d897fe53014cc1952c6f759.svg) at the point ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) is ![](images/27/270d33581e0657a10d567c6cfaa529521b34e20a39c9a29550698cbf252fabfa.svg)" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186065374 1064 "Remember that the derivative of a function $f(x)$ represents the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=f(x)$.\n\nFor example, consider the curve $y=x^2$, shown below. If we want to compute the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $A(1,1)$, then we compute $f'(x)$ and evaluate $f'(1)$.\n\n![](images/a5/a56edd18eb6f6587108c44cf4e798f3544f7862f08b771288b48304a432f5c52.png)\n\nIf\n\n$$\nf(x)=x^2\n$$\n\nthen $f'(x) = 2 x$ by the power rule. Substituting $x = 1$ into $f'(x)$ gives\n\n$$\nf'(1) = 2 (1) = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=x^2$ at the point $A$ is $2$." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186065383 1064 "Why do we study derivatives? It turns out that we can use derivatives to understand many practical situations, like the motion of a particle.\n\nSuppose we're given the position $x$ of a particle as a function of time, so $x = x(t)$. The **velocity** $v(t)$ of the particle is the rate of change of its position with respect to time.\n\nSo, we can compute the velocity by taking the derivative of $x(t)$ with respect to time:\n\n$$\nv(t) = \\frac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nThe velocity of a particle represents how fast the particle is going at any moment in time, and the direction in which it is traveling (positive or negative).\n\n![](images/1d/1d6cfba60814e4e56a623888b59809318a984a075d90ac455c166f686de968d6.png)" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186065383 1064 "Why do we study derivatives? It turns out that we can use derivatives to understand many practical situations, like the motion of a particle.\n\nSuppose we're given the position $x$ of a particle as a function of time, so $x = x(t).$ The **velocity** $v(t)$ of the particle is the rate of change of its position with respect to time. \n\nSo, we can compute the velocity by taking the derivative of $x(t)$ with respect to time:\n\n$$\nv(t) = \\frac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nThe velocity of a particle represents how fast the particle is going at any moment in time, and the direction in which it is traveling (positive or negative).\n\n![](images/1d/1d6cfba60814e4e56a623888b59809318a984a075d90ac455c166f686de968d6.png)" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186065384 1064 "Let's build some more intuition for why velocity is defined as the derivative of position.\n\nIf a car is moving at a constant velocity, and it travels a distance of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x=100\\,\\textrm{m}\n$$\n\nin a time interval\n\n$$\n\\Delta t=10\\,\\textrm{s}\n$$\n\nthen we know that its velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv &= \\frac{Δx}{Δt} \\\\\n&= \\frac{100}{10} \\\\\n&= 10m/s\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBut what if the velocity itself changes with time? Perhaps the car accelerates for a couple of seconds in the beginning, thereby increasing its velocity. In that case, the formula above only gives us the average velocity.\n\nHowever, if we take a smaller time interval, we can get more precise information about the velocity at a given time $t$. To get the instantaneous velocity of the car, we need to take the limit as $\\Delta t\\to 0$. When we do this, we get the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= lim_(Δt → 0)\\frac{Δx}{Δt} \\\\\n&= \\frac{dx}{dt}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe instantaneous velocity is the number that you read on the speedometer of cars." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186065384 1064 "Let's build some more intuition for why velocity is defined as the derivative of position.\n\nIf a car is moving at a constant velocity, and it travels a distance of $\\Delta x=100\\,\\textrm{m}$ in a time interval $\\Delta t=10\\,\\textrm{s},$ then we know that its velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{Δx}{Δt} \\\\  & =\\frac{100}{10} \\\\  & =10\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBut what if the velocity itself changes with time? Perhaps the car accelerates for a couple of seconds in the beginning, thereby increasing its velocity. In that case, the formula above only gives us the average velocity. \n\nHowever, if we take a smaller time interval, we can get more precise information about the velocity at a given time $t.$ To get the instantaneous velocity of the car, we need to take the limit as $\\Delta t\\to 0.$ When we do this, we get the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & ={lim}_{Δt\\to 0}\\frac{Δx}{Δt} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe instantaneous velocity is the number that you read on the speedometer of cars." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186065385 1064 "**Velocity** is a vector. It has both magnitude and direction, and can be negative. For motion along a straight line (i.e. along the $x$-axis), negative velocity means that it's moving to the left, in the negative direction.\n\n**Speed**, however, is a scalar quantity with no direction. It is the magnitude of the velocity, given by $\\mid v \\mid$. The speed is always positive or zero, and is never negative.\n\nFor example, if a particle moving along the $x$-axis has a velocity of $-2 \\, \\textrm{m/s}$, then it is moving left (in the negative direction) with a speed of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid {-2} &= 2 \\, \\textrm{m/s}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reason why we take the absolute value when computing speed is that speed is directionless (unlike velocity, which can be positive or negative depending on the direction of motion).\n\n![](images/c9/c981522543f72ab3ed921d28d9b7f7f8a45bd7542b7ffc9931416456e9d27cdd.png)" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186065385 1064 "**Velocity** is a vector. It has both magnitude and direction, and can be negative. For motion along a straight line (i.e. along the $x$-axis), negative velocity means that it's moving to the left, in the negative direction. \n\n**Speed**, however, is a scalar quantity with no direction. It is the magnitude of the velocity, given by $|v|$. The speed is always positive or zero, and is never negative.\n\nFor example, if a particle moving along the $x$-axis has a velocity of $-2 \\, \\textrm{m/s},$ then it is moving left (in the negative direction) with a speed of $|{-2}|=2 \\, \\textrm{m/s}.$\n\nThe reason why we take the absolute value when computing speed is that speed is directionless (unlike velocity, which can be positive or negative depending on the direction of motion).\n\n![](images/c9/c981522543f72ab3ed921d28d9b7f7f8a45bd7542b7ffc9931416456e9d27cdd.png)" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186065400 1064 "Derivatives naturally occur in many real-world situations, so it's essential to know their units of measurement. The key is to remember that the derivative represents a rate of change.\n\nFor instance, suppose that the temperature of a pizza $t$ minutes after coming out of a hot oven is $T(t)$ degrees Fahrenheit. What are the units of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}T}{\\textrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we have that the temperature $T(t)$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit and the time $t$ is measured in minutes.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}T(t): & degrees Fahrenheit \\\\ t: & minutes\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe derivative\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\color{black}T}{\\textrm{d}\\color{black}t}\n$$\n\nmeasures a change in ${\\color{black}T}$ over a change in ${\\color{black}t}$. So, it represents a ratio of the units:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}: \\mid \\frac{degrees Fahrenheit}{minutes}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\color{black}T}{\\textrm{d}\\color{black}t}\n$$\n\nis measured in \"degrees Fahrenheit per minute\", or $^\\circ \\textrm F / \\textrm {min}$." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186065400 1064 "Derivatives naturally occur in many real-world situations, so it's essential to know their units of measurement. The key is to remember that the derivative represents a rate of change.\n\nFor instance, suppose that the temperature of a pizza $t$ minutes after coming out of a hot oven is $T(t)$ degrees Fahrenheit. What are the units of $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}T}{\\textrm{d}t}?$\n\nFirst, we have that the temperature $T(t)$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit and the time $t$ is measured in minutes.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t): & \\text{degrees Fahrenheit} \\\\ t: & \\text{minutes}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\color{black}T}{\\textrm{d}\\color{black}t}$ measures a change in ${\\color{black}T}$ over a change in ${\\color{black}t}.$ So, it represents a ratio of the units:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}: & \\frac{\\text{degrees Fahrenheit}}{\\text{minutes}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\color{black}T}{\\textrm{d}\\color{black}t}$ is measured in \"degrees Fahrenheit per minute\", or $^\\circ \\textrm F / \\textrm {min}.$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186065407 1064 "Intuitively, a function $f(x)$ is **continuous** if we can draw $y=f(x)$ without taking our pen off the paper.\n\nWe can give a precise mathematical definition of continuity using limits:\n\n> *A function $f(x)$ is **continuous at the point** $\\boldsymbol{x=c}$ if*\n> $\\lim_{x\\to c}f(x) = f(c)$.\n\nIn other words, a function is continuous at a point if the limit at the point is equal to the function value at the point.\n\nTo test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n1. Does $f(c)$ exist? In other words, we must check that $f(x)$ is defined at $x=c$.\n2. Does $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist? In other words, we have to check that $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to c^{-}}f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to c^{+}}f(x)$.\n3. Does $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x) = f(c)$?\n\nIf the answer to all three questions is yes, then the function is continuous at $x=c$. If the function is not continuous at $x=c$, we say that it has a **discontinuity** at $x=c$.\n\nThe diagram below shows a function that's continuous at $x=c$.\n\n![](images/54/54ebdb8faa4b1ece5620b817b813780e8abd5cc89cf858eed4cb9ba03f24a046.png)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(c)$ exists$\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exists $\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to c}f(x) = f(c)$ $\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\nLet's now see how to use these continuity conditions to check for continuity in a piecewise function." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186065407 1064 "Intuitively, a function $f(x)$ is **continuous** if we can draw $y=f(x)$ without taking our pen off the paper. \n\nWe can give a precise mathematical definition of continuity using limits:\n\n> *A function $f(x)$ is **continuous at the point** $\\boldsymbol{x=c}$ if*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x) = f(c).\n> $$\n\nIn other words, a function is continuous at a point if the limit at the point is equal to the function value at the point.\n\nTo test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n- Does $f(c)$ exist? In other words, we must check that $f(x)$ is defined at $x=c.$\n\n- Does $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist? In other words, we have to check that $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to c^{-}}f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to c^{+}}f(x).$\n\n- Does $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x) = f(c)?$\n\nIf the answer to all three questions is yes, then the function is continuous at $x=c.$ If the function is not continuous at $x=c,$ we say that it has a **discontinuity** at $x=c.$\n\nThe diagram below shows a function that's continuous at $x=c.$\n\n![](images/54/54ebdb8faa4b1ece5620b817b813780e8abd5cc89cf858eed4cb9ba03f24a046.png)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(c)$ exists$\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exists $\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to c}f(x) = f(c)$ $\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\nLet's now see how to use these continuity conditions to check for continuity in a piecewise function." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186065408 1064 "Remember that to test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n- Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n- Does $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n- Does $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x) = f(c)?$\n\nLet's use these three conditions to show that the following function is continuous at $x=1.$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}1+x, & x\\le 1 \\\\ 3-x & x>1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe check all three conditions in turn.\n\n- Using the expression for the first branch of the function, we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(1)= 1+1= 2.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1.$ $\\:\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(1+x) \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(3-x) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1^-} f(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1^+} f(x)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ exists: \n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 1} f(x) = 2 \\:\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\n- We have that $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 1} f(x) = 2 = f(1).$ $\\:\\: {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1.$ The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/c5/c55aa89d279d94b7561f6c24ebdb37bafcf46dbc20edf4f93d515aca7ae4cde6.png)" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186065408 1064 "Remember that to test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n1. Does $f(c)$ exist?\n2. Does $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n3. Does $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x) = f(c)$?\n\nLet's use these three conditions to show that the following function is continuous at $x=1$.\n\n$$\n{1 + x,\\begin{vmatrix}x \\le 1 \\\\ 3 - x\\end{vmatrix}x > 1\n$$\n\nWe check all three conditions in turn.\n\n1. Using the expression for the first branch of the function, we obtain that\n$f(1)= 1+1= 2$.\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1$. $\\:\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n2. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n$lim_(x → 1^{-})f(x)|= lim_(x → 1^{-})(1 + x); = 1 + 1; = 2; lim_(x → 1^{+})f(x)|= lim_(x → 1^{+})(3 - x); = 3 - 1; = 2$\nAs a result:\n$\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1^-} f(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1^+} f(x)$\nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ exists:\n$\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 1} f(x) = 2 \\:\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n3. We have that $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 1} f(x) = 2 = f(1)$. $\\:\\: {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/c5/c55aa89d279d94b7561f6c24ebdb37bafcf46dbc20edf4f93d515aca7ae4cde6.png)" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186065413 1064 "Remember that the exponential function $e^x$ has the special property that the derivative of the exponential function is the exponential function itself. In other words, taking the derivative doesn't change anything, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm d x} \\left(e^ x\\right)  = e^ x.\n$$\n\nSince integration is the opposite of differentiation, and differentiating the exponential function leaves it unchanged, integrating the exponential function also leaves it unchanged. So, the integral of the exponential function is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int e^x \\, \\textrm d x = e^x +C.\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186065413 1064 "Remember that the exponential function $e^x$ has the special property that the derivative of the exponential function is the exponential function itself. In other words, taking the derivative doesn't change anything, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm d x} \\left(e^ x\\right) = e^ x\n$$\n\nSince integration is the opposite of differentiation, and differentiating the exponential function leaves it unchanged, integrating the exponential function also leaves it unchanged. So, the integral of the exponential function is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int e^x \\, \\textrm d x = e^x +C\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186065414 1064 "Remember that if $a$ is a positive real number, then the derivative of $a^x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm d x}\\left( a^ x\\right)\t=\ta^ x \\ln a.\n$$\n\nSo, by \"reversing\" this process, we obtain the following formula for the integral of a general exponential function:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int  a^x \\, \\textrm d x  = \\dfrac{a^ x }{\\ln a} +C.\n$$\n\nFor instance, suppose that we wish to calculate the integral \n\n$$\n\\int 2^x  \\, \\textrm d x\n$$\n\nTo compute the integral of a general exponential function, we just need to divide by the natural logarithm of the base of the exponential function:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\int 2^x  \\, \\textrm d x   &= \\dfrac{2^x}{\\ln 2} + C\n}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186065414 1064 "Remember that if $a$ is a positive real number, then the derivative of $a^x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm d x}\\left(a^ x\\right) = a^ x \\ln a\n$$\n\nSo, by \"reversing\" this process, we obtain the following formula for the integral of a general exponential function:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int a^x \\, \\textrm d x = \\dfrac{a^ x}{\\ln a} +C\n$$\n\nFor instance, suppose that we wish to calculate the integral\n\n$$\n\\int 2^x \\, \\textrm d x\n$$\n\nTo compute the integral of a general exponential function, we just need to divide by the natural logarithm of the base of the exponential function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\int 2^x \\, \\textrm d x &= \\dfrac{2^x}{\\ln 2} + C \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186065415 1064 "We know that the derivative of sine is cosine:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}(\\sin{x}) = \\cos{x}\n$$\n\nSince integration is the reverse of differentiation, we see that the integral of cosine is sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\cos{x}\\,\\textrm d x = \\sin{x} + C\n$$\n\nWe can discover two other integrals by considering the derivatives of cosine and tangent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sin}x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{cos}x+C \\\\ \\int {sec}^{2}x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{tan}x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, by considering the derivatives of secant, cosecant, and cotangent, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{sec}x+C \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{csc}x\\operatorname{cot}x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{csc}x+C \\\\ \\int {csc}^{2}x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{cot}x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith these six formulas, we can integrate many different trigonometric functions." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186065415 1064 "We know that the derivative of sine is cosine:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(\\sin{x}) = \\cos{x}\n$$\n\nSince integration is the reverse of differentiation, we see that the integral of cosine is sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\cos{x}\\,\\textrm d x = \\sin{x} + C\n$$\n\nWe can discover two other integrals by considering the derivatives of cosine and tangent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\sin xdx &= -\\cos x + C \\\\\n\\int\\sec^{2} xdx &= \\tan x + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, by considering the derivatives of secant, cosecant, and cotangent, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\sec x\\tan xdx &= \\sec x + C \\\\\n\\int\\csc x\\cot xdx &= -\\csc x + C \\\\\n\\int\\csc^{2} xdx &= -\\cot x + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith these six formulas, we can integrate many different trigonometric functions." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186065430 1064 "We know how to calculate $\\displaystyle\\int x^5 \\, \\textrm{d}x$. But suppose that we want to calculate an integral like\n\n$$\n\\int (2x+1)^5 \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThe integral above is different from what we've seen previously because now it's a whole function $(2x+1)$ that is being raised to the power of $5$. So what do we do?\n\nIn this case, the answer is to introduce a new variable. Our goal is to replace all of the $x$'s in the integral, *including* the $\\textrm d x$, with this new variable which we'll call $u$.\n\nFirst, we let\n\n$$\nu=2x+1\n$$\n\nDifferentiating both sides with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2\n$$\n\nand if we treat\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\n$$\n\nlike a fraction, we can get the $\\textrm d x$ on its own as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{d} x = \\dfrac 1 2 \\textrm d u\n$$\n\nWe're now in a position to write our integral in terms of the new variable $u$, which we do as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int(2x + 1)^{5}dx &= \\intu^{5} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\intu^{5}du\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now calculate our integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}\\intu^{5}du &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{u^{6}}{6} + C \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{12}u^{6} + C \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{12}(2x + 1)^{6} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** After solving an integral using substitution, we should always double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}[\\frac{1}{12}(2x + 1)^{6} + C] &= \\frac{1}{12} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}[(2x + 1)^{6}] + \\frac{d}{dx}(C) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{12} \\cdot 6(2x + 1)^{5} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(2x + 1) + 0 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(2x + 1)^{5} \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= (2x + 1)^{5}✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186065430 1064 "We know how to calculate $\\displaystyle\\int x^5 \\, \\textrm{d}x.$ But suppose that we want to calculate an integral like\n\n$$\n\\int (2x+1)^5 \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThe integral above is different from what we've seen previously because now it's a whole function $(2x+1)$ that is being raised to the power of $5.$ So what do we do? \n\nIn this case, the answer is to introduce a new variable. Our goal is to replace all of the $x$'s in the integral, *including* the $\\textrm d x$, with this new variable which we'll call $u.$\n\nFirst, we let $u=2x+1.$ Differentiating both sides with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2,\n$$\n\nand if we treat $\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}$ like a fraction, we can get the $\\textrm d x$ on its own as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{d} x = \\dfrac 1 2 \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nWe're now in a position to write our integral in terms of the new variable $u,$ which we do as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (2x+1)^{5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {u}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int {u}^{5}\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now calculate our integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\int {u}^{5}\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{6}}{6}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{u}^{6}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(2x+1)^{6}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** After solving an integral using substitution, we should always double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{12}(2x+1)^{6}+C] & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(2x+1)^{6}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 6(2x+1)^{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x+1)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2x+1)^{5}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =(2x+1)^{5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186065433 1064 "By using the substitution\n\n$$\nu=ax+b\n$$\n\n, it's straightforward to show that\n\n$$\n\\int (ax+b)^n\\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac{1}{a(n+1)}(ax+b)^{n+1} + C, \\qquad n\\neq -1\n$$\n\nIf you can remember the formula, great! You'll save yourself some time. If you don't like memorizing formulas, you can always solve these integrals using the substitution method described in this lesson." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186065433 1064 "By using the substitution $u=ax+b$, it's straightforward to show that\n\n$$\n\\int (ax+b)^n\\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac{1}{a(n+1)}(ax+b)^{n+1} + C, \\qquad n\\neq -1.\n$$\n\nIf you can remember the formula, great! You'll save yourself some time. If you don't like memorizing formulas, you can always solve these integrals using the substitution method described in this lesson." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186065434 1064 "Suppose that we want to calculate an integral like\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{2x+1} \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nWe know that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{x} \\, \\textrm dx = \\ln \\mid x \\mid\n$$\n\nbut our case is different because now it's a whole function $(2x+1)$ that is in the denominator.\n\nFor integrals like the one above, we can use a substitution. First, let\n\n$$\nu=2x+1\n$$\n\nThen differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x = \\dfrac 1 2 \\, \\textrm d u\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ and solve it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{1}{2x + 1}dx &= \\int\\frac{1}{u} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int\\frac{1}{u}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\ln \\mid u \\mid + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we write the final answer in terms of $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{1}{2x + 1}dx &= \\frac{1}{2}\\ln \\mid 2x + 1 \\mid + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** After solving an integral using substitution, we should always double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}\\ln & 2x + 1 & + C\\end{bmatrix} &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}[\\ln \\mid 2x + 1 \\mid ] + \\frac{d}{dx}(C) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2x + 1} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(2x + 1) + 0 \\\\\n&= (1)/(2(2x + 1)) \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2x + 1}✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186065434 1064 "Suppose that we want to calculate an integral like\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{2x+1} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe know that $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{x} \\, \\textrm dx = \\ln |x|,$ but our case is different because now it's a whole function $(2x+1)$ that is in the denominator.\n\nFor integrals like the one above, we can use a substitution. First, let $u=2x+1.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x = \\dfrac 1 2 \\,  \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ and solve it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}|u|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we write the final answer in terms of $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}|2x+1|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** After solving an integral using substitution, we should always double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}|2x+1|+C] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\operatorname{ln}|2x+1|]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2x+1}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x+1)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2(2x+1)}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2x+1}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186065435 1064 "In general,\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{ax+b}\\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac 1 a \\ln \\mid ax+b \\mid + C\n$$\n\nThese types of integrals come up quite often in calculus, so it's worth remembering this formula." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186065435 1064 "In general,\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{ax+b}\\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac 1 a \\ln|ax+b| + C.\n$$\n\nThese types of integrals come up quite often in calculus, so it's worth remembering this formula." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186069928 1064 "For a parametrically defined function\n\n$$\nx = x(t), y = y(t)\n$$\n\nwe know that the derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx} = (y^{′}(t))/(x^{′}(t))\n$$\n\nTo calculate the second derivative\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2}\n$$\n\nwe differentiate\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$ and use the chain rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{dy}{dx}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx}) \\cdot \\frac{dt}{dx} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx}))/((\\frac{dx}{dt}))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, let's calculate\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2}\n$$\n\nfor the curve defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx= t^2, \\qquad y= 2 t^3, \\qquad t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty)\n$$\n\nTo calculate\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nwe first need to compute the derivatives of $x$ and $y$. We get\n\n$$\nx'(t) = 2t, \\qquad y'(t) = 6t^2\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= (y^{′}(t))/(x^{′}(t)) = \\frac{6t^{2}}{2t} = 3t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} &= (\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx}))/((\\frac{dx}{dt})) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{d}{dt}(3t))/(2t) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186069928 1064 "For a parametrically defined function ![](images/e1/e159a3e09b9558743ad31676b2b24ddc5693567fa5f14a0db26b59ed056e218e.svg) we know that the derivative is given by\n\n![](images/b1/b19bbbdffd7fc7704f2f449afaa51a736b8dfd4cec0947e2e890cac9b990c390.svg)\nwhere ![](images/37/37108c872e8d79d8cfbccc54ca92b4cc53c2c31952db5169866b625862ba03ed.svg) denotes ![](images/7f/7f4ab03a7abb27a7d1c7ec192739835b48a83499d29e980b3114c77644a445da.svg) and ![](images/f1/f174e41615895d39c258b8ce122e1aa12f028de8f0da695c7449df90d29795b0.svg) denotes ![](images/44/4472311979bb886e78126f5a764202c0059808f2f02f5255d6b8ecd053a62edb.svg)\n\nTo calculate the second derivative ![](images/bd/bd5e402ed5bf31cdb9a7ead27e347b1a4df672818e8735051c778920439d13ef.svg) we differentiate ![](images/b9/b9e4e8135d478f59edec90e8937fb24436bc2054a40d9394ba589549e2c381ee.svg) with respect to ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) and use the chain rule, as follows:\n![](images/6d/6d67811848f344c1c55cc088ee3245f26c0481c596215be2637a40d57da24332.svg)\n\nFor example, let's calculate ![](ima