t( \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)^x+3$ has the same shape as $y=\\left( \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)^x,$ but it's shifted $3$ units up.\n\n![](images/f7/f76f7a276392996811bd70c707107c3b8e3e764c38d188ae3e31e4414ad92ff2.png)\n\nNote the following points:\n\n- The $y$-intercept $(0,1)$ has moved up $3$ units to the point $(0,4).$\n\n- The horizontal asymptote $y=0$ has moved up $3$ units to $y=3.$\n\nNow, consider the graph of $y=\\left( \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)^x-3.$ In this case, the graph of $y=\\left( \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)^x$ is shifted $3$ units *down*.\n\n![](images/7e/7e5afd3739b8db7488041a8c90fc0ca7db1e61908ffd7bf7ca4d484fa356f5eb.png)" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186069945 1064 "When an exponential decay function is vertically shifted *and* stretched, we apply the stretch first and the shift second.\n\nFor example, to plot the function\n\n$$\ny=2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4} \\right)^x+3\n$$\n\nwe start by plotting the graph of\n\n$$\ny = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{4} \\right)^x{:}\n$$\n\n![](images/84/842f4b6dfd9a45f4d187f4b448048879761cfbf4d880a113c9f3fcabc4988543.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of\n\n$$\ny = 2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^x\n$$\n\nTo do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $2$. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,2)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0$.\n\n![](images/04/0439fee0ab5e8e993cd5eda5dbebc72e5b79209c60aa547c036940ced6a970eb.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of\n\n$$\ny = 2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^x + 3\n$$\n\nTo do this, we shift the above graph $3$ units above. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,2)$ to $(0,5)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=3$.\n\n![](images/22/220210f1b1ece359ea2ba4b41a1176ba02c0e34c370ad09772d03a5afeacbe7d.png)" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186069945 1064 "When an exponential decay function is vertically shifted *and* stretched, we apply the stretch first and the shift second.\n\nFor example, to plot the function $y=2\\left( \\dfrac{1}{4} \\right)^x+3,$ we start by plotting the graph of $y = \\left( \\dfrac{1}{4} \\right)^x{:}$\n\n![](images/84/842f4b6dfd9a45f4d187f4b448048879761cfbf4d880a113c9f3fcabc4988543.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y = 2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^x.$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $2.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,2)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/04/0439fee0ab5e8e993cd5eda5dbebc72e5b79209c60aa547c036940ced6a970eb.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y = 2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^x + 3.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $3$ units above. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,2)$ to $(0,5)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=3.$\n\n![](images/22/220210f1b1ece359ea2ba4b41a1176ba02c0e34c370ad09772d03a5afeacbe7d.png)" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186069952 1064 "Up to now, we've completed the square on quadratic expressions whose perfect square is already isolated.\n\nIn general, isolating the perfect square in a quadratic expression requires a trick. We'll now learn how this works.\n\nAs an example, let's complete the square on the following expression:\n\n$$\nx^2+10x+{\\color{purple}{15}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term ($10$) as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2+{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{5}}x+{\\color{purple}{15}}\n$$\n\nSo, the linear coefficient is now decomposed into two factors, $\\color{red}2$ and ${\\color{blue}{5}}$.\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and then *subtract* the square of the second factor ${\\color{blue}{5}}$, writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}{15}}$ last:\n\n$$\nx^2+{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{5}}x + {\\color{blue}{5}}^2 - {\\color{blue}{5}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{15}}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first three terms now form a perfect square!\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2+{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{5}}x + {\\color{blue}{5}}^2} - {\\color{blue}{5}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{15}}\n$$\n\nFactoring the perfect square, we get\n\n$$\n(x+{\\color{blue}{5}})^2 - {\\color{blue}{5}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{15}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the last two terms as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 5)^{2} - 5^{2} + 15 &=  \\\\\n(x + 5)^{2} - 25 + 15 &=  \\\\\n&= (x + 5)^{2} - 10 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done!\n\nWe can easily check that our answer is correct by expanding the parentheses and simplifying:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 5)^{2} - 10 &=  \\\\\nx^{2} + 2 \\cdot 5 \\cdot x + 5^{2} - 10 &=  \\\\\nx^{2} + 10x + 25 - 10 &=  \\\\\n&= x^{2} + 10x + 15 \\mid ✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186069952 1064 "Up to now, we've completed the square on quadratic expressions whose perfect square is already isolated. \n\nIn general, isolating the perfect square in a quadratic expression requires a trick. We'll now learn how this works.\n\nAs an example, let's complete the square on the following expression:\n\n$$\nx^2+10x+{\\color{purple}{15}}\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term ($10$) as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2+{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{5}}x+{\\color{purple}{15}}\n$$\n\nSo, the linear coefficient is now decomposed into two factors, $\\color{red}2$ and ${\\color{blue}{5}}.$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and then *subtract* the square of the second factor ${\\color{blue}{5}},$ writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}{15}}$ last:\n\n$$\nx^2+{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{5}}x + {\\color{blue}{5}}^2 - {\\color{blue}{5}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{15}}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first three terms now form a perfect square!\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2+{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{5}}x + {\\color{blue}{5}}^2} - {\\color{blue}{5}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{15}}\n$$\n\nFactoring the perfect square, we get\n\n$$\n(x+{\\color{blue}{5}})^2 - {\\color{blue}{5}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{15}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the last two terms as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)^{2}-{5}^{2}+15 & = \\\\ (x+5)^{2}-25+15 & = \\\\ (x+5)^{2}-10 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done!\n\nWe can easily check that our answer is correct by expanding the parentheses and simplifying:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)^{2}-10 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 5\\cdot x+{5}^{2}-10 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+10x+25-10 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+10x+15 & \\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186069953 1064 "We can use the method of completing the square to solve *any* quadratic equation, even if the equation does not factor.\n\nTo illustrate, suppose we want to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\nx^2+2x-8=0\n$$\n\nWe can do so by following the five steps below:\n\n**Step 1.** Move the constant term to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 2x - 8 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} + 2x &= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 2x &= 8 \\\\\nx^{2} + 2 \\cdot 1 \\cdot x &= 8 \\\\\nx^{2} + 2 \\cdot 1 \\cdot x + 1^{2} - 1^{2} &= 8 \\\\\n(x^{2} + 2 \\cdot 1 \\cdot x + 1^{2}) - 1 &= 8 \\\\\n(x + 1)^{2} - 1 &= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Isolate the perfect square by adding $1$ to both sides. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 1)^{2} &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4.** We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 1)^{2} &= 9 \\\\\n\\sqrt{(x + 1)^{2}} &= \\sqrt{9} \\\\\n\\mid x + 1 \\mid &= 3 \\\\\nx + 1 &= ± 3 \\\\\nx &= -1 ± 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5.** Finally, we simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= -1 - 3 =-4 \\\\\nx_{2} &= -1 + 3 = 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-4$ and $x=2$." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186069953 1064 "We can use the method of completing the square to solve *any* quadratic equation, even if the equation does not factor.\n\nTo illustrate, suppose we want to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\nx^2+2x-8=0\n$$\n\nWe can do so by following the five steps below:\n\n**Step 1.** Move the constant term to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x-8 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot x & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot x+{1}^{2}-{1}^{2} & =8 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot x+{1}^{2})-1 & =8 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}-1 & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Isolate the perfect square by adding $1$ to both sides. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4.** We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =9 \\\\ \\sqrt{(x+1)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\ |x+1| & =3 \\\\ x+1 & =\\pm 3 \\\\ x & =-1\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5.** Finally, we simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-1-3=-4 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =-1+3=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-4$ and $x=2.$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186069964 1064 "A function $f(x)$ is **continuous** over its entire domain if the graph of $y=f(x)$ can be drawn using a pencil and a piece of paper without taking the pencil off of the paper.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the function $y=x^2$ whose graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/67/67ffd0b78845693b522100f4f0ba460cae6fedd796964b24708e320eab04ce29.png)\n\nWe can draw as much of the function $y=x^2$ as we want without taking our pencil off of the paper. Therefore, we say that $y=x^2$ is **continuous** at every point in its domain.\n\nLet's compare this to the function $y=g(x)$ below:\n\n![](images/a0/a07ccd15d14fa3eeb00a9c4ab07674c1c169e2a0d0a3e9fcb64798e9929e2ff9.png)\n\nNotice that this function has a jump in its value at $x=0$.\n\nIt is *impossible* to draw $y=g(x)$ without taking the pencil off the paper to fill in the jump at $x=0$. Therefore, we say that $y=g(x)$ has a **discontinuity** at $x=0$." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186069964 1064 "A function $f(x)$ is **continuous** over its entire domain if the graph of $y=f(x)$ can be drawn using a pencil and a piece of paper without taking the pencil off of the paper.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the function $y=x^2$ whose graph is shown below. \n\n![](images/67/67ffd0b78845693b522100f4f0ba460cae6fedd796964b24708e320eab04ce29.png)\n\nWe can draw as much of the function $y=x^2$ as we want without taking our pencil off of the paper. Therefore, we say that $y=x^2$ is **continuous** at every point in its domain.\n\nLet's compare this to the function $y=g(x)$ below:\n\n![](images/a0/a07ccd15d14fa3eeb00a9c4ab07674c1c169e2a0d0a3e9fcb64798e9929e2ff9.png)\n\nNotice that this function has a jump in its value at $x=0.$\n\nIt is *impossible* to draw $y=g(x)$ without taking the pencil off the paper to fill in the jump at $x=0.$ Therefore, we say that $y=g(x)$ has a **discontinuity** at $x=0.$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186069965 1064 "A function $f(x)$ is said to be **left-continuous** at the point $x=c$ if the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to c^-} f(x) = f(c)\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the function $y=f(x)$ defined on the interval $(-\\infty,2]$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/14/14b92b0e5a0f482b5e58da975f6f8d369890af97ddc562a4876e41c496f5dfef.png)\n\nNotice that at $x=2$, the left limit exists, while right limit does not exist because $f(x)$ is not defined for $x>2$.\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2^-}f(x) = 4, \\qquad \\lim_{x\\to 2^+}f(x) = \\text{DNE}\n$$\n\nBecause the right-sided limit does not exist, the overall limit does not exist either:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x) = \\text{DNE}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=2$. However, because the left-sided limit exists and is equal to the function value at $x=2$, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2^-}f(x) = 4 = f(2)\n$$\n\nwe can say that the function is **left-continuous** at $x=2$." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186069965 1064 "A function $f(x)$ is said to be **left-continuous** at the point $x=c$ if the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to c^-} f(x) = f(c).\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the function $y=f(x)$ defined on the interval $(-\\infty,2]$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/14/14b92b0e5a0f482b5e58da975f6f8d369890af97ddc562a4876e41c496f5dfef.png)\n\nNotice that at $x=2,$ the left limit exists, while right limit does not exist because $f(x)$ is not defined for $x>2.$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2^-}f(x) = 4, \\qquad \\lim_{x\\to 2^+}f(x) = \\text{DNE}\n$$\n\nBecause the right-sided limit does not exist, the overall limit does not exist either:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x) = \\text{DNE}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=2.$ However, because the left-sided limit exists and is equal to the function value at $x=2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2^-}f(x) = 4 = f(2),\n$$\n\nwe can say that the function is **left-continuous** at $x=2.$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186069966 1064 "A function $f(x)$ is said to be **right-continuous** at the point $x=c$ if the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to c^+} f(x) = f(c)\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the function $y=f(x)$ defined on the interval $[-2,\\infty)$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/0b/0b625f271c8889466c2a19ed8a333b05e70c2e852058cc1d4469f428d761c799.png)\n\nThe function is **right-continuous** at $x=-2$ because\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -2^+}f(x) = 4 = f(-2)\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186069966 1064 "A function $f(x)$ is said to be **right-continuous** at the point $x=c$ if the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to c^+} f(x) = f(c).\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the function $y=f(x)$ defined on the interval $[-2,\\infty)$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/0b/0b625f271c8889466c2a19ed8a333b05e70c2e852058cc1d4469f428d761c799.png)\n\nThe function is **right-continuous** at $x=-2$ because\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -2^+}f(x) = 4 = f(-2).\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186069967 1064 "Now consider the function $y=h(x)$ below. This function has a discontinuity at $x=0$, so it is not continuous over its entire domain.\n\n![](images/4e/4e41ca41e4befba33c62da68666a8c4a2e2c7a19c79331360ae4bf124edafd50.png)\n\nHowever, the function $h(x)$ may be continuous at other points, such as $x=1$.\n\nTo determine whether $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$, we have to consider points in the **neighborhood** of $x=1$. Roughly speaking, the *neighborhood of $x=1$* means the points on the curve that have an $x$-coordinate that's close to $1$.\n\nIndeed, in the *neighborhood* of $x=1$, we can draw the function $h(x)$ without taking the pencil off the paper. So, $h(x)$ is indeed continuous at $x=1$." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186069967 1064 "Now consider the function $y=h(x)$ below. This function has a discontinuity at $x=0,$ so it is not continuous over its entire domain.\n\n![](images/4e/4e41ca41e4befba33c62da68666a8c4a2e2c7a19c79331360ae4bf124edafd50.png)\n\nHowever, the function $h(x)$ may be continuous at other points, such as $x=1.$ \n\nTo determine whether $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=1,$ we have to consider points in the **neighborhood** of $x=1.$ Roughly speaking, the *neighborhood of $x=1$* means the points on the curve that have an $x$-coordinate that's close to $1.$\n\nIndeed, in the *neighborhood* of $x=1,$ we can draw the function $h(x)$ without taking the pencil off the paper. So, $h(x)$ is indeed continuous at $x=1.$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186069968 1064 "Recall that a function $f(x)$ is said to be **continuous** at a point $x=c$ if\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to c} f(x) = f(c)\n$$\n\nIn order for the above statement to be true, we require both\n\n$$\nlim_(x → c^{-})f(x) = f(c)andlim_(x → c^{+})f(x) = f(c)\n$$\n\nThe above equalities represent left-continuity and right-continuity. So, we have the following fact:\n\n> A function is **continuous** at a point if and only if it is *both* **left-continuous** *and* **right-continuous** at that point." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186069968 1064 "Recall that a function $f(x)$ is said to be **continuous** at a point $x=c$ if \n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to c} f(x) = f(c) .\n$$\n\nIn order for the above statement to be true, we require both\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {c}^{-}}f(x)=f(c)\\,\\text{and}\\,{lim}_{x\\to {c}^{+}}f(x)=f(c).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equalities represent left-continuity and right-continuity. So, we have the following fact:\n\n> A function is **continuous** at a point if and only if it is *both* **left-continuous** *and* **right-continuous** at that point." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186069983 1064 "To solve an equation that contains multiple rational terms, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the least common denominator.\n\nFor example, consider the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2x^2}= \\dfrac{1}{2x}-\\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nFirst, note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution because it would make the denominators equal zero.\n\nNow, let's multiply both sides of the equation by the least common denominator. The equation has the denominators $2x^2$, $2x$, and $x$, so the least common multiple (or $\\textrm{LCM}$) of the denominators is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(2x^2, 2x, x) = {\\color{black}2x^2}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by ${\\color{black}2x^2}$ and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2x^{2}} &= 2x^{2} \\cdot (\\frac{1}{2x} - \\frac{1}{x}) \\\\\n2x^{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2x^{2}} &= \\frac{2x^{2}}{2x} - \\frac{2x^{2}}{x} \\\\\n1 &= \\frac{2x \\cdot x}{2x} - \\frac{x \\cdot 2x}{x} \\\\\n1 &= x - 2x \\\\\n1 &= -x \\\\\nx &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. Since $x = -1$ is different from $x=0$, we can accept it as the solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-1$." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186069983 1064 "To solve an equation that contains multiple rational terms, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the least common denominator. \n\nFor example, consider the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2x^2}= \\dfrac{1}{2x}-\\dfrac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution because it would make the denominators equal zero.\n\nNow, let's multiply both sides of the equation by the least common denominator. The equation has the denominators $2x^2,$ $2x,$ and $x,$ so the least common multiple (or $\\textrm{LCM}$) of the denominators is \n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(2x^2, 2x, x) = {\\color{black}2x^2}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by ${\\color{black}2x^2}$ and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}} & =2{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2x}-\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\ \\cancel{2{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\cancel{2{x}^{2}}} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2x}-\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{x} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{\\cancel{2x}\\cdot x}{\\cancel{2x}}-\\frac{\\cancel{x}\\cdot 2x}{\\cancel{x}} \\\\ 1 & =x-2x \\\\ 1 & =-x \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. Since $x = -1$ is different from $x=0,$ we can accept it as the solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-1.$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186069984 1064 "To sketch the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{2}{x}\n$$\n\nwe start with the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac1x\n$$\n\nand stretch it by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis, as shown below.\n\n![](images/6d/6de3b0c1a61952f51f2a681337e72af171aaf0b2be91f588b7d4b8f2a303f951.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The point $(1,1)$ on $y=\\dfrac1x$ is mapped to the point $(1,2)$ on $y=\\dfrac2x$.\n- The point $(-1,-1)$ on $y=\\dfrac1x$ is mapped to the point $(-1,-2)$ on $y=\\dfrac2x$." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186069984 1064 "To sketch the graph of ![](images/0d/0d56960f9390e8a7fe5c1fe17b9fa8b62fe9f33e052eb295161ce7a368047111.svg) we sta