 $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to a} \\frac {f'(x)}{g'(x)}$ exists.\n- If $a$ is $\\pm \\infty$, and $f$ and $g$ are both differentiable for all sufficiently large $x$, then the limit $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to \\pm\\infty} \\frac {f'(x)}{g'(x)}$ exists.\n\nWhen working with familiar functions like polynomials, trigonometric functions, exponential functions, logarithmic functions, etc, it's safe to assume that these conditions are met." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186069988 1064 "In this lesson, we started out with a special case of L'Hopital's rule (the case of $\\dfrac{0}{0}$ as $x \\to a$) and gradually introduced more cases. In closing, we will state the full version of L'Hopital's rule.\n\nSuppose that\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to a} \\dfrac{f(x)}{g(x)} = \\dfrac{0}{0} \\quad \\textrm{or} \\quad \\lim\\limits_{x \\to a} \\dfrac{f(x)}{g(x)} = \\dfrac{\\pm \\infty}{\\pm \\infty},\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is any real number, or $\\pm \\infty.$ Then we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a}  \\dfrac {f(x)}{g(x)} = \\lim_{x\\to a}  \\frac {f'(x)}{g'(x)},\n$$\n\nprovided that the following conditions are met:\n\n- If $a$ is a real number, and $f$ and $g$ are both differentiable at $x=a,$ then the limit $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to a}  \\frac {f'(x)}{g'(x)}$ exists.\n\n- If $a$ is $\\pm \\infty,$ and $f$ and $g$ are both differentiable for all sufficiently large $x,$ then the limit $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to \\pm\\infty}  \\frac {f'(x)}{g'(x)}$ exists.\n\nWhen working with familiar functions like polynomials, trigonometric functions, exponential functions, logarithmic functions, etc, it's safe to assume that these conditions are met." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186069992 1064 "Similarly, to sketch the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{2x}\n$$\n\nwe take the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac1x\n$$\n\nand stretch it by a scale factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nparallel to the $y$-axis.\n\n![](images/08/08c02826d4c2dff684e00c01e9229e5ef7767f4122d99fcfa8e5d63c254cbd94.png)\n\