rst condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}(kx)+(ky) \\\\ 2(ky)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}k(x+y) \\\\ k(2y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k[\\begin{aligned}x+y \\\\ 2y\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})+({y}_{1}+{y}_{2}) \\\\ 2({y}_{1}+{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1}+{y}_{1})+({x}_{2}+{y}_{2}) \\\\ 2{y}_{1}+2{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{y}_{1} \\\\ 2{y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}+{y}_{2} \\\\ 2{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth conditions are satisfied, so this transformation is linear." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186088946 1064 "A transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ is called a **linear transformation** (or **linear map**) if the following two properties are satisfied:\n\n1. $\\mathbf{T}(k\\mathbf{v})=k\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v})$ for any vector $\\mathbf{v}$ and any scalar $k$\n2. $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_1+\\mathbf{v}_2)=\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_1)+\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_2)$ for any vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$\n\nIf we let $\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$ $\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ y_{1}\\end{bmatrix}$ $\\begin{bmatrix}x_{2} \\\\ y_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ then the conditions above can be written as follows:\n\n1. $\\begin{bmatrix}kx \\\\ ky\\end{bmatrix}$\n2. $\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} + x_{2} \\\\ y_{1} + y_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe can determine whether the transformation $\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$ is linear by checking if these two conditions are true.\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n$T\\begin{bmatrix}kx \\\\ 