ithin the given domain, we simply subtract ![](images/3c/3c00bafca99d6e3b871cfc92c1c18f0689457bdbd47d5fafeadcfddfd24691a2.svg)\n\n![](images/e3/e30c675c887c49206b4b10ffd2db3f4adadaf994faa621783590ceb97eaa7f15.svg)\n\nSo, the solutions are ![](images/79/79cbf4b44767be3ca02472a95e45ef8966e290e95dce26e11abd070bc225e217.svg)" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186093391 1064 "Can we find solutions to the equation\n\n$$\n0^{∘} \\le x < 360^{∘}\n$$\n\nRemember that the sine function always satisfies\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\sin{x} \\leq 1\n$$\n\nThe number $2$ is outside of this range. So, there is no solution to the equation above.\n\nIn general, when a number $a$ is not in $[-1,1]$, there are no values of $x$ such that $\\sin x = a$." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186093391 1064 "Can we find solutions to the equation\n![](images/93/933c55527c58604b70aaf835d0a2888cc674b27829c686d387ba2cbbb8105387.svg)\n\nRemember that the sine function always satisfies ![](images/e4/e488fa3d55f6cef0ef1cb7fac0dc91b7b0e064263c00ea21e7914fced75b03da.svg) The number ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) is outside of this range. So, there is no solution to the equation above.\n\nIn general, when a number ![](images/34/349015631ec44ef653936c85586d2c5ef56ba64392b3f92b4b92cbef3e731bc5.svg) is not in ![](images/c8/c8f028686e053833b4bc9544f31d327fdcfc1b755e90d523407d1084b3871a18.svg) there are no values of ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) such that ![](images/58/58cdd1494b26c6aaf02cf636e3f7cd23b48dff6be4e9270eb0fb23d3d3adce37.svg)" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186093392 1064 "Suppose we want to find all of the solutions to the equation\n\n$$\n\\cos x = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},  \\qquad 0^\\circ \\leq x < 360^\\circ.\n$$\n\nWe can immediately calculate a solution, called the **principal value**, as follows:\n\n$$\nx = \\arccos\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) = 30^\\circ\n$$\n\nSince $30^\\circ$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is $x_1=30^\\circ.$ This angle lies in the first quadrant and has reference angle $\\theta_R=30^\\circ.$\n\nBut there is another solution. Since $\\cos x=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} >0,$ the second solution to the equation will lie in the fourth quadrant, and its reference angle is also $\\theta_R=30^\\circ.$ To find the second solution, we use a CAST diagram.\n\n![](images/8c/8c6e3ae6bdbfb1c66aee719f77225453992fffe6f3c6e3dfdc3f1ff92c59863a.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{30}^{∘} \\\\  & ={330}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=30^\\circ, 330^\\circ.$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186093392 1064 "Suppose we want to find all of the solutions to the equation\n\n$$\n\\cos x = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}, \\qquad 0^\\circ \\leq x < 360^\\circ\n$$\n\nWe can immediately calculate a solution, called the **principal value**, as follows:\n\n$$\nx = \\arccos\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) = 30^\\circ\n$$\n\nSince $30^\\circ$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is\n\n$$\nx_1=30^\\circ\n$$\n\nThis angle lies in the first quadrant and has reference angle\n\n$$\n\\theta_R=30^\\circ\n$$\n\nBut there is another solution. Since\n\n$$\n\\cos x=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} >0\n$$\n\nthe second solution to the equation will lie in the fourth quadrant, and its reference angle is also\n\n$$\n\\theta_R=30^\\circ\n$$\n\nTo find the second solution, we use a CAST diagram.\n\n![](images/8c/8c6e3ae6bdbfb1c66aee719f77225453992fffe6f3c6e3dfdc3f1ff92c59863a.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= 360^{∘} - θ_{R} \\\\\n&= 360^{∘} - 30^{∘} \\\\\n&= 330^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx=30^\\circ, 330^\\circ\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186093393 1064 "Suppose we want to find all of the solutions to\n\n$$\n-180^{∘} < x \\le 180^{∘}\n$$\n\nThis time, we are looking for solutions in the domain\n\n$$\n-180^\\circ < x \\leq 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arccos\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) = 30^\\circ\n$$\n\nSince $30^\\circ$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is\n\n$$\nx_1=30^\\circ\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of\n\n$$\n\\theta_R=30^\\circ\n$$\n\nSince $\\cos x>0$, the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle\n\n$$\n\\theta_R=30^\\circ\n$$\n\n![](images/8c/8c6e3ae6bdbfb1c66aee719f77225453992fffe6f3c6e3dfdc3f1ff92c59863a.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nX &= 360^{∘} - θ_{R} \\\\\n&= 360^{∘} - 30^{∘} \\\\\n&= 330^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the domain\n\n$$\n-180^\\circ < x \\leq 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nTo get a second solution within the required domain, we need to find an angle that's coterminal with $X$. To do this, we subtract $360^\\circ$ from $X$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= X - 360^{∘} \\\\\n&= 330^{∘} - 360^{∘} \\\\\n&=-30^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx=-30^\\circ, 30^\\circ\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186093393 1064 "Suppose we want to find all of the solutions to\n\n$$\n-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nThis time, we are looking for solutions in the domain $-180^\\circ < x \\leq 180^\\circ.$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arccos\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) = 30^\\circ\n$$\n\nSince $30^\\circ$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is $x_1=30^\\circ.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of $\\theta_R=30^\\circ.$ Since $\\cos x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle $\\theta_R=30^\\circ.$\n\n![](images/8c/8c6e3ae6bdbfb1c66aee719f77225453992fffe6f3c6e3dfdc3f1ff92c59863a.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{30}^{∘} \\\\  & ={330}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the domain $-180^\\circ < x \\leq 180^\\circ.$ To get a second solution within the required domain, we need to find an angle that's coterminal with $X.$ To do this, we subtract $360^\\circ$ from $X\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =X-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & ={330}^{∘}-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & =-{30}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=-30^\\circ, 30^\\circ.$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186093407 1064 "Can we solve the following equation?\n\n$$\n0^{∘} \\le x < 360^{∘}\n$$\n\nRemember that the range of the cosine function is given by\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\cos{x} \\leq 1\n$$\n\nSince the number $3$ lies outside of this range, the equation $\\cos x = 3$ has no solution for any value of $x$.\n\nIn general, the equation\n\n$$\n\\cos x = a\n$$\n\nhas no solutions for $\\mid a \\mid > 1$." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186093407 1064 "Can we solve the following equation?\n\n$$\n{0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}\n$$\n\nRemember that the range of  the cosine function is given by \n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\cos{x} \\leq 1.\n$$\n\nSince the number $3$ lies outside of this range, the equation $\\cos x = 3$ has no solution for any value of $x.$\n\nIn general, the equation\n\n$$\n\\cos x = a\n$$\n\nhas no solutions for $|a| > 1.$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186093412 1064 "To specify the position of a point $P$ in the Cartesian plane, we normally use its $x$- and $y$- coordinates, also called the **Cartesian coordinates** of $P$. We then write the position of the point as $P(x,y)$.\n\nFor example, the point below has coordinates $x=3$ and $y=4$, so we write it as $P(3,4)$.\n\n![](images/eb/eba0b986f45ba9a3e4e5184cf9ee83f37eb8985d026ec2bd97c5faa9d1ed1d32.png)\n\nHowever, this is not the only way of specifying the position of a point.\n\nWe could identify a point by writing down its distance to the origin $(r)$ and the angle that it makes with the positive $x$-axis $(\\theta)$, also called the **polar angle**, defined such that\n\n$$\n0\\leq \\theta \\lt 2\\pi\n$$\n\nThe coordinates $r$ and $\\theta$ are called **polar coordinates**.\n\n![](images/ae/aed825a215063a0c6b1182692479da0ef1c9d9a76cf7256fb8878544db072ca9.png)\n\nLet's see how to express a point $P(x,y)$ in polar coordinates $P(r,\\theta)$." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186093412 1064 "To specify the position of a point $P$ in the Cartesian plane, we normally use its $x$- and $y$- coordinates, also called the **Cartesian coordinates** of $P.$ We then write the position of the point as $P(x,y).$\n\nFor example, the point below has coordinates $x=3$ and $y=4,$ so we write it as $P(3,4).$\n\n![](images/eb/eba0b986f45ba9a3e4e5184cf9ee83f37eb8985d026ec2bd97c5faa9d1ed1d32.png)\n\nHowever, this is not the only way of specifying the position of a point.\n\nWe could identify a point by writing down its distance to the origin $(r)$ and the angle that it makes with the positive $x$-axis $(\\theta),$ also called the **polar angle**, defined such that $0\\leq \\theta \\lt 2\\pi .$\nThe coordinates $r$ and $\\theta$ are called **polar coordinates**.\n\n![](images/ae/aed825a215063a0c6b1182692479da0ef1c9d9a76cf7256fb8878544db072ca9.png)\n\nLet's see how to express a point $P(x,y)$ in polar coordinates $P(r,\\theta).$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186093436 1064 "The equation of the tangent line to the curve $y = f(x)$ at the point $P(x_1,y_1)$, in point-slope form, is given by\n\n$$\ny - y_1 = m (x - x_1), \\hspace{.75cm} m=f'(x_1)\n$$\n\nHere, $m = f'(x_1)$ because $f'(x_1)$ represents the slope of the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ at the point $(x_1,y_1)$.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to compute the equation of the tangent line to the curve $y=x^2$ at the point $P(1,1)$.\n\n![](images/48/487d21289b468fb82efa2097e425dd560811dc3165890509dacbab8093e0ecdb.png)\n\nSince $y=x^2$, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=x^2\n$$\n\nWe begin by calculating the derivative of $f(x)$:\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(x^2)= 2x\n$$\n\nThen, we determine the slope of the tangent at $P(1,1)$:\n\n$$\nm = f'(1) = 2(1) = 2\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the slope $m=2$ and the coordinates of $P(1,1)$ into the point-slope form, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - 1 &= 2(x - 1) \\\\\ny &= 2x - 2 + 1 \\\\\ny &= 2x - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the tangent is $y=2x-1$." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186093436 1064 "The equation of the tangent line to the curve $y = f(x)$ at the point $P(x_1,y_1),$ in point-slope form, is given by\n\n$$\ny - y_1 = m (x - x_1), \\hspace{.75cm} m=f'(x_1).\n$$\n\nHere, $m = f'(x_1)$ because $f'(x_1)$ represents the slope of the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ at the point $(x_1,y_1).$\n\nFor example, suppose we want to compute the equation of the tangent line to the curve $y=x^2$ at the point $P(1,1).$\n\n![](images/48/487d21289b468fb82efa2097e425dd560811dc3165890509dacbab8093e0ecdb.png)\n\nSince $y=x^2,$ we have $f(x)=x^2.$ We begin by calculating the derivative of $f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf'(x) \t\t = \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(x^2)= 2x\n$$\n\nThen, we determine the slope of the tangent at $P(1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nm = f'(1)  = 2(1) = 2\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we substitute the slope $m=2$ and the coordinates of $P(1,1)$ into the point-slope form, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =2(x-1) \\\\ y & =2x-2+1 \\\\ y & =2x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the tangent is $y=2x-1.$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186093448 1064 "The equation of the normal line to the curve $y = f(x)$ at the point $P(x_1,y_1)$, in point-slope form, is given by\n\n$$\ny - y_1 = m (x - x_1), \\hspace{1cm} m=-\\dfrac{1}{f'(x_1)}\n$$\n\nHere,\n\n$$\nm=-\\dfrac{1}{f'(x_1)}\n$$\n\nbecause the slope of the normal line is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the tangent line, and $f'(x_1)$ represents the slope of the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ at the point $(x_1,y_1)$.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to compute the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=x^2$ at the point $A(1,1)$. This normal line is the dashed line shown below, which is perpendicular to the solid tangent line.\n\n![](images/31/31ba09ff14e5bd485794f283c5d6a5b087b6c21fdb9242af01b75ec8e8744e0d.png)\n\nSince $y=x^2$, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=x^2\n$$\n\nWe begin by calculating the derivative of $f(x)$:\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(x^2)= 2x\n$$\n\nAt $A(1,1)$, the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\nf'(1) = 2(1) = 2\n$$\n\nHowever, we want the normal line, and the slope of the normal line is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the tangent line:\n\n$$\nm = -\\dfrac{1}{f'(1)} = -\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we work out the equation of the normal line using the point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - 1 &= -\\frac{1}{2}(x - 1) \\\\\ny &= -\\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{1}{2} + 1 \\\\\ny &= -\\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\ny=-\\dfrac x 2 + \\dfrac 3 2\n$$\n\nWe could also write this in standard form as\n\n$$\nx+2y=3\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186093448 1064 "The equation of the normal line to the curve $y = f(x)$ at the point $P(x_1,y_1),$ in point-slope form, is given by\n\n$$\ny - y_1 = m (x - x_1), \\hspace{1cm} m=-\\dfrac{1}{f'(x_1)}.\n$$\n\nHere, $m=-\\dfrac{1}{f'(x_1)}$ because the slope of the normal line is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the tangent line, and $f'(x_1)$ represents the slope of the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ at the point $(x_1,y_1).$\n\nFor example, suppose we want to compute the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=x^2$ at the point $A(1,1).$ This normal line is the dashed line shown below, which is perpendicular to the solid tangent line. \n\n![](images/31/31ba09ff14e5bd485794f283c5d6a5b087b6c21fdb9242af01b75ec8e8744e0d.png)\n\nSince $y=x^2,$ we have $f(x)=x^2.$ We begin by calculating the derivative of $f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf'(x) \t\t = \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(x^2)= 2x\n$$\n\nAt $A(1,1),$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\nf'(1) \t= 2(1) = 2.\n$$\n\nHowever, we want the normal line, and the slope of the normal line is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the tangent line:\n\n$$\nm = -\\dfrac{1}{f'(1)} = -\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we work out the equation of the normal line using the point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}+1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the normal line is $y=-\\dfrac x 2 + \\dfrac 3 2.$ We could also write this in standard form as $x+2y=3.$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186097812 1064 "Now, let's derive the rule\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}\\left(a^x\\right) = a^x \\ln a\n$$\n\nThe trick here is to write this in terms of $e^x$.\n\nFirst, note that since the natural logarithm is the inverse of the exponential function, we have\n\n$$\na^x = e^{\\ln (a^x)}\n$$\n\nand by the power rule for logarithms, we can write this as\n\n$$\na^x = e^{x\\ln a}\n$$\n\nNow, it can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(e^{x\\ln a}) &= e^{x\\ln a} \\cdot \\ln a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(a^{x}) &= e^{x\\ln a} \\cdot \\ln a \\\\\n&= e^{\\ln a^{x}} \\cdot \\ln a \\\\\n&= a^{x}\\ln a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186097812 1064 "Now, let's derive the rule\n![](images/77/77334bed6aafc3383552d90bcfc8da614d56493edce4e7ab89a2f28e01d3dbfa.svg)\n\nThe trick here is to write this in terms of ![](images/5b/5ba79af546498192229d0cdd5a4a922d1d015802402b8eec5910fe00f999820d.svg) \n\nFirst, note that since the natural logarithm is the inverse of the exponential function, we have\n![](images/09/091c69568c8472aacf8229a756a835e0edc26700677e4fa67de3d2f6ac0d96a2.svg)\n\nand by the power rule for logarithms, we can write this as\n\n![](images/c9/c9b146d550ef068823a4ee4b9f63eea89f46d6ceecedf0ad4baa6462f11d9127.svg)\n\nNow, the following result can be used (we'll prove it in the subsequent topics):\n\n![](images/ce/cedfc7ab127ac8b34cd72b21e8c3e527541e054721324d9d948d6df123cd61e0.svg)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n![](images/58/5880fb665abda7f3cef4ad7425c99c43b16e84c448c7b9e22ff32f76e36df87c.svg)\n\nas required." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186097813 1064 "Let's now prove the chain rule in the following form:\n\n$$\n(f \\circ g)'(a) = f'(g(a)) \\cdot g'(a)\n$$\n\nLet $f$ and $g$ be functions such that\n\n- $g$ is differentiable at $a$,\n\n- $f$ is differentiable at $g(a)$.\n\nThen $f \\circ g$ is differentiable at $a$, and we compute its derivative as follows.\n\nWe start with the definition of the derivative:\n\n$$\n(f \\circ g)'(a) = \\lim_{x \\to a} \\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(a))}{x - a}\n$$\n\nSince $g$ is differentiable at $a$, it is also continuous there. Hence $g(x) \\to g(a)$ as $x \\to a$. For $x$ sufficiently close to $a$, we have $g(x) \\ne g(a)$, so we may write\n\n$$\n\\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(a))}{x - a} \n= \\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(a))}{g(x) - g(a)} \\cdot \\frac{g(x) - g(a)}{x - a}.\n$$\n\nTaking the limit as $x \\to a$, and using the product rule for limits (which applies since both limits exist), we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f∘g)^{'}(a) & ={lim}_{x\\to a}(\\frac{f(g(x))-f(g(a))}{g(x)-g(a)}\\cdot \\frac{g(x)-g(a)}{x-a}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(g(x))-f(g(a))}{g(x)-g(a)}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{g(x)-g(a)}{x-a} \\\\  & ={f}^{'}(g(a))\\cdot {g}^{'}(a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186097813 1064 "Let's now prove the chain rule in the following form:\n\n$$\n(f \\circ g)'(a) = f'(g(a)) \\cdot g'(a)\n$$\n\nLet $f$ and $g$ be functions such that\n\n- $g$ is differentiable at $a$,\n- $f$ is differentiable at $g(a)$.\n\nThen $f \\circ g$ is differentiable at $a$, and we compute its derivative as follows.\n\nWe start with the definition of the derivative:\n\n$$\n(f \\circ g)'(a) = \\lim_{x \\to a} \\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(a))}{x - a}\n$$\n\nSince $g$ is differentiable at $a$, it is also continuous there. Hence $g(x) \\to g(a)$ as $x \\to a$. For $x$ sufficiently close to $a$, we have\n\n$$\ng(x) \\ne g(a)\n$$\n\n, so we may write\n\n$$\n\\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(a))}{x - a} = \\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(a))}{g(x) - g(a)} \\cdot \\frac{g(x) - g(a)}{x - a}\n$$\n\nTaking the limit as $x \\to a$, and using the product rule for limits (which applies since both limits exist), we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f∘g)^{′}(a) &= lim_(x → a)((f(g(x)) - f(g(a)))/(g(x) - g(a)) \\cdot (g(x) - g(a))/(x - a)) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → a)(f(g(x)) - f(g(a)))/(g(x) - g(a)) \\cdot lim_(x → a)(g(x) - g(a))/(x - a) \\\\\n&= f^{′}(g(a)) \\cdot g^{′}(a)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186097816 1064 "Sometimes, we might have a system of equations with decimal coefficients, such as the following:\n\n$$\n{0.2x + y = 1; 0.15x - 0.04y = 0.2\n$$\n\nTo make the system easier to solve, we turn it into a system involving integer coefficients only.\n\nIn general, we multiply each equation by $10$, or $100$, or $1000$, etc. depending on how many decimal places we need to eliminate. In this particular instance:\n\n- We need to eliminate one decimal place in the top equation, so we multiply by $10$.\n- We need to eliminate two decimal places in the bottom equation, so we multiply by $100$.\n\nSo, we multiply the first equation by $10$ and the second equation by $100$, as follows:\n\n$$\n{0.2x + y = 1 \\mid \\mid \\cdot 10; 0.15x - 0.04y = 0.2 \\mid \\mid \\cdot 100\n$$\n\nFrom here, we can solve the system of equations using either substitution or elimination." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186097816 1064 "Sometimes, we might have a system of equations with decimal coefficients, such as the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0.2x+y=1 \\\\ 0.15x-0.04y=0.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo make the system easier to solve, we turn it into a system involving integer coefficients only.\n\nIn general, we multiply each equation by $10,$ or $100,$ or $1000,$ etc. depending on how many decimal places we need to eliminate. In this particular instance:\n\n- We need to eliminate one decimal place in the top equation, so we multiply by $10.$\n\n- We need to eliminate two decimal places in the bottom equation, so we multiply by $100.$\n\nSo, we multiply the first equation by $10$ and the second equation by $100,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0.2x+y=1 & |\\times 10 \\\\ 0.15x-0.04y=0.2 & |\\times 100\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we can solve the system of equations using either substitution or elimination." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186097819 1064 "The exponential function\n\n$$\nf(x) = e^x\n$$\n\nhas the special property that its derivative is the function itself:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d} x}\\left(e^x \\right)= e^x\n$$\n\nSo, taking the derivative of an exponential function does nothing to the exponential function. The exponential function remains exactly the same.\n\nNote that the exponential function is the *only* function with this special property.\n\nWe'll justify this result at the end of the lesson. But for now, let's get some practice working with it." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186097819 1064 "The exponential function  $f(x) = e^x$ has the special property that its derivative is the function itself:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{ \\textrm{d} x}\\left( e^x \\right)= e^x\n$$\n\nSo, taking the derivative of an exponential function does nothing to the exponential function. The exponential function remains exactly the same. \n\nNote that the exponential function is the *only* function with this special property.\n\nWe'll justify this result at the end of the lesson. But for now, let's get some practice working with it." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186097820 1064 "If we replace  the base $e$ of an exponential function by any other positive number $a$, then the derivative is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{ \\textrm{d} x}\\left( a^x \\right)= a^x \\ln a\n$$\n\nWe just multiply the given exponential function by the natural logarithm of the base.\n\nNote that since $\\ln e =1$, our first formula is a particular case of this one!\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{ \\textrm{d} x}\\left( e^x \\right)= e^x \\ln e = e^x \\cdot 1 = e^x\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186097820 1064 "If we replace the base $e$ of an exponential function by any other positive number $a$, then the derivative is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d} x}\\left(a^x \\right)= a^x \\ln a\n$$\n\nWe just multiply the given exponential function by the natural logarithm of the base.\n\nNote that since $\\ln e =1$, our first formula is a particular case of this one!\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d} x}\\left(e^x \\right)= e^x \\ln e = e^x \\cdot 1 = e^x\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186097821 1064 "To derive the identity\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(e^x) = e^x\n$$\n\nwe begin with the definition of the derivative:\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nf(x) = e^x\n$$\n\n.\n\nSubstituting the function $f(x)$ into the definition of $f'(x)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= lim_(h → 0)\\frac{e^{x + h} - e^{x}}{h} \\\\\n&= lim_(h → 0)\\frac{e^{x} \\cdot e^{h} - e^{x}}{h} \\\\\n&= lim_(h → 0)(e^{x} \\cdot (e^{h} - 1))/(h) \\\\\n&= e^{x} \\cdot lim_(h → 0)\\frac{e^{h} - 1}{h}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, it can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h \\to 0} \\frac{e^h - 1}{h} = 1\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\nf'(x) = e^x \\cdot 1 = e^x\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(e^x) = e^x\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186097821 1064 "To derive the identity\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(e^x) = e^x,\n$$\n\nwe begin with the definition of the derivative:\n\nwhere $f(x) = e^x$.\n\nSubstituting the function $f(x)$ into the definition of $f'(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{x+h}-{e}^{x}}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{h}-{e}^{x}}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{x}\\cdot ({e}^{h}-1)}{h} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot {lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{h}-1}{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, it can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h \\to 0} \\frac{e^h - 1}{h} = 1,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\nf'(x) = e^x \\cdot 1 = e^x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(e^x) = e^x.\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186097822 1064 "The derivative of the natural logarithm is the reciprocal function:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{ \\textrm{d} x}\\left( \\ln x \\right)= \\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nWe'll prove this result in a future lesson." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186097822 1064 "The derivative of the natural logarithm is the reciprocal function:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d} x}\\left(\\ln x \\right)= \\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nWe'll prove this result in a future lesson." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186097823 1064 "For a general logarithm of base $a$, the derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d} x}\\left(\\log_a x \\right)= \\dfrac{1}{x\\ln a}\n$$\n\nAlso, recall that whenever $\\log$ is written without a base, we can assume that it represents the base $10$. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\log x = \\log_{10} x\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\log x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\log_{10} x) = \\frac{1}{x\\ln 10}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** To see where this rule comes from, we can use the change-of-base formula as shown below.\n\n$$\n\\log_a x = \\dfrac{\\ln x}{\\ln a}\n$$\n\nTaking the derivative and using the fact that $\\ln a$ is a constant, then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\log_{a} x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{\\ln x}{\\ln a}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\ln a} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(\\ln x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\ln a} \\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{x\\ln a}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186097823 1064 "For a general logarithm of base $a,$ the derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{ \\textrm{d} x}\\left( \\log_a x \\right)= \\dfrac{1}{x\\ln a}.\n$$\n\nAlso, recall that whenever $\\log$ is written without a base, we can assume that it represents the base $10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\log x =  \\log_{10} x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}(\\operatorname{log}x) & =\\frac{d}{dx}({log}_{10}x)=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** To see where this rule comes from, we can use the change-of-base formula as shown below.\n\n$$\n\\log_a x = \\dfrac{\\ln x}{\\ln a}\n$$\n\nTaking the derivative and using the fact that $\\ln a$ is a constant, then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{a}x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}x}{\\operatorname{ln}a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}a}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}a}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186097824 1064 "Suppose that we have the two functions $u(x)=2x^3$ and $v(x)=x^2$. How do we calculate the derivative of their product $(u(x) \\cdot v(x))'$?\n\nTo do that, we can use the **product rule**, which states that the derivative of the product of two functions is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\left(u(x) \\cdot v(x)\\right)' = u'(x) \\cdot v(x) + u(x) \\cdot v'(x)\n$$\n\nIn our case, the derivatives of the two functions are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu^{′}(x) &= (2x^{3})^{′} = 6x^{2}, \\\\\nv^{′}(x) &= (x^{2})^{′} = 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we plug all of this into the product rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(u(x) \\cdot v(x))^{′} &= (6x^{2})(x^{2}) + (2x^{3})(2x) \\\\\n&= 6x^{4} + 4x^{4} \\\\\n&= 10x^{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis example was quite simple, and we could have solved it by calculating the product first and then taking the derivative. We can do it now to double check our result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(u(x) \\cdot v(x))^{′} &= (2x^{3} \\cdot x^{2})^{′} \\\\\n&= (2x^{5})^{′} \\\\\n&= 10x^{4}✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSometimes, however, we cannot calculate the product algebraically, or it can be tedious to do it. In those cases, the product rule will turn out to be very useful!" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186097824 1064 "Suppose that we have the two functions $u(x)=2x^3$ and $v(x)=x^2.$ How do we calculate the derivative of their product $(u(x) \\cdot v(x))'?$\n\nTo do that, we can use the **product rule**, which states that the derivative of the product of two functions is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\left(u(x) \\cdot  v(x)\\right)' = u'(x) \\cdot v(x) + u(x) \\cdot v'(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the derivatives of the two functions are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{'}(x) & =(2{x}^{3})^{'}=6{x}^{2}, \\\\ {v}^{'}(x) & =({x}^{2})^{'}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we plug all of this into the product rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u(x)\\cdot v(x))}^{'} & =(6{x}^{2})({x}^{2})+(2{x}^{3})(2x) \\\\  & =6{x}^{4}+4{x}^{4} \\\\  & =10{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis example was quite simple, and we could have solved it by calculating the product first and then taking the derivative. We can do it now to double check our result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u(x)\\cdot v(x))}^{'} & =(2{x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{2})^{'} \\\\  & =(2{x}^{5})^{'} \\\\  & =10{x}^{4}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSometimes, however, we cannot calculate the product algebraically, or it can be tedious to do it. In those cases, the product rule will turn out to be very useful!" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186097825 1064 "The product rule can also be written using *Leibniz notation*:\n\nIf\n\n$$\ny = u(x) \\cdot v(x)\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} \\cdot v + u \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186097825 1064 "The product rule can also be written using *Leibniz notation*:\n\nIf $y = u(x) \\cdot v(x),$ then \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} \\cdot  v + u \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186097826 1064 "We know how to calculate the derivative of the product of two functions, but what if we have a quotient?\n\nFor that, we can use the **quotient rule**. The quotient rule is a formula to calculate the derivative of the ratio of two functions like\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac {u(x)}{v(x)}\n$$\n\nThe quotient rule for differentiation states that the derivative of the function above is given by\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\dfrac {u'(x)\\cdot v(x) - u(x) \\cdot v'(x)} {[v(x)]^2}\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose that we have the function $f(x)$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)= \\dfrac{x}{x^2+1}\n$$\n\nand we want to find $f'(x)$.\n\nHere $u(x)=x$ and\n\n$$\nv(x)=x^2+1\n$$\n\nTheir derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu^{′}(x) &= (x)^{′} = 1, \\\\\nv^{′}(x) &= (x^{2} + 1)^{′} = 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we plug them in the formula, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= (u^{′}(x) \\cdot v(x) - u(x) \\cdot v^{′}(x))/([v(x)]^{2}) \\\\\n&= (1 \\cdot (x^{2} + 1) - x \\cdot 2x)/((x^{2} + 1)^{2}) \\\\\n&= (x^{2} + 1 - 2x^{2})/((x^{2} + 1)^{2}) \\\\\n&= (1 - x^{2})/((x^{2} + 1)^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186097826 1064 "We know how to calculate the derivative of the product of two functions, but what if we have a quotient?\n\nFor that, we can use the **quotient rule**. The quotient rule is a formula to calculate the derivative of the ratio of two functions like \n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac {u(x)}{v(x)}.\n$$\n\nThe quotient rule for differentiation states that the derivative of the function above is given by\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\dfrac {u'(x)\\cdot v(x) - u(x) \\cdot v'(x)} {[v(x)]^2}.\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose that we have the function $f(x)$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)= \\dfrac{x}{x^2+1},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $f'(x).$ \n\nHere $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)=x^2+1.$  Their derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{'}(x) & =(x)^{'}=1, \\\\ {v}^{'}(x) & =({x}^{2}+1)^{'}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we plug them in the formula, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot ({x}^{2}+1)-x\\cdot 2x}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+1-2{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186097827 1064 "Using Leibniz notation, the quotient rule is expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left (\\dfrac u v\\right) = \\dfrac {v \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}u} {\\textrm{d}x}\n- u \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x}}{v^2}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186097827 1064 "Using Leibniz notation, the quotient rule is expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left (\\dfrac u v\\right) = \\dfrac {v \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}u} {\\textrm{d}x} - u \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x}}{v^2}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186097828 1064 "We can now prove that\n\n$$\n(\\tan x)' = \\sec^2{x}\n$$\n\nusing the quotient rule. Here's how!\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\tan x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}) \\\\\n&= ((\\sin x)^{′} \\cdot (\\cos x) - (\\sin x) \\cdot (\\cos x)^{′})/(\\cos^{2} x) \\\\\n&= ((\\cos x) \\cdot (\\cos x) - (\\sin x) \\cdot (-\\sin x))/(\\cos^{2} x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\cos^{2} x + \\sin^{2} x}{\\cos^{2} x} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\cos^{2} x} \\\\\n&= \\sec^{2} x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186097828 1064 "We can now prove that $(\\tan x)' = \\sec^2{x}$ using the quotient rule. Here's how! \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}x)^{'}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}x)-(\\operatorname{sin}x)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}x)^{'}}{{cos}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}x)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}x)-(\\operatorname{sin}x)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}x)}{{cos}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}x+{sin}^{2}x}{{cos}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}x} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186097843 1064 "Given a fixed **origin** in the plane (often denoted ![](images/75/7502527c010624a805906c12d7a6e94363988e41b1c40771a4cc4fc82900e083.svg)), and another point ![](images/42/42193b6382254991fb9c053ead9edd0631767155ea51d8a8551cfc4fc734340e.svg) the vector ![](images/20/20edf7e26e0b35148bf8ff1e9b52c38e5b6672ae0462a8c2e218e1c0456bae07.svg) is called the **position vector** of the point ![](images/ed/ed6dc1fd057e3dd2207c735a562bf4db22b72d1a9380651607439f00c0440725.svg)\n\nSo, the position vector tells us how to move from the origin to the point.\n\nFor example, suppose ![](images/4f/4f9204dcee76e03f03dc64827bf810327c9b2b981a0aee5a10c7f80ca98f4d5d.svg) and ![](images/41/412bd151cdf5602575028591e2f87d9a7e0328aa6b18dccb6459e7041a5d9b9c.svg) How can we find the position vector ![](images/20/20edf7e26e0b35148bf8ff1e9b52c38e5b6672ae0462a8c2e218e1c0456bae07.svg) in terms of ![](images/52/527e0abeb684dd24d8c92e45425542080c4c9493b9460bb89753fef3226c1f92.svg) and ![](images/c5/c5d2e1827ba6a11504655381514c15ce7eba142e62bf8dca3a10d12eef9d7861.svg)\n\n![](images/e6/e61003fb62534b04cfbbac07a64e61f6c16e2d464eb35bc5b98654b0603b24db.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we can trace a path from ![](images/75/7502527c010624a805906c12d7a6e94363988e41b1c40771a4cc4fc82900e083.svg) to ![](images/46/46833dd7644e5c60bb4525fe4e7649a99752b26096331755a4b44c2abe183f11.svg) by first moving from ![](images/75/7502527c010624a805906c12d7a6e94363988e41b1c40771a4cc4fc82900e083.svg) to ![](images/c2/c2c85081d1f89d74b8ebb2761496d211d7052193809b7864e7983437f5d0b80f.svg) and then from ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) to ![](images/ed/ed6dc1fd057e3dd2207c735a562bf4db22b72d1a9380651607439f00c0440725.svg) Therefore,\n![](images/ac/ac96d55fd0d74cf5fd24f1f1a96beb3865bc70bc45d17be5aad209c5adb1be6d.svg)\n\nSince ![](images/4f/4f9204dcee76e03f03dc64827bf810327c9b2b981a0aee5a10c7f80ca98f4d5d.svg) and ![](images/e2/e283cb6e7ab325c89dd652abd93ff1382b714462887d8839dfed8769693cc7a7.svg) we get\n![](images/e1/e1bde038ef63f77c3ecc5357b49999c96dd6d331aa7455fcdd1935b1f3eb8261.svg)\n\nSo, to find a position vector, we can trace a convenient path from the origin to the point and add the vectors along that path." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186097843 1064 "Given a fixed **origin** in the plane (often denoted $O$), and another point $X$, the vector $\\overrightarrow{OX}$ is called the **position vector** of the point $X$. The vector $\\mathbf{x}$ defines the position of $X$ on the plane in relation to $O$.\n\nFor example, suppose we have two vectors\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nand a parallelogram $OAXB_1$, where\n\n$$\n{OB_1}=2{OB}\n$$\n\nHow can we find the vector $\\mathbf{x}$, the diagonal of the parallelogram, expressed in terms of $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$?\n\n![](images/70/707f54e69e47d391432fe6a15e92fab108d4f698dc13c0b04c8c392b72a5386b.png)\n\nFrom the $\\triangle OB_1X$ in the above figure, we can see that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = \\overrightarrow{OB}_1+\\overrightarrow{B_1X}\n$$\n\nWe know that\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OB}_1=2\\overrightarrow{OB}=2\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nAlso, since $OAXB_1$ is a parallelogram, we have $OA \\parallel B_1X$ and\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{B_1X}=\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the original equality, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= OB^(→)_{1} + B_{1}X^(→) \\\\\n&= 2b + a \\\\\n&= a + 2b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186097878 1064 "The **chain rule** for differentiation states that the derivative of a composite function $f(g(x))$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}g} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}g} {\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nThere is some intuition behind this rule: if we think of the derivatives as fractions, the ${\\textrm{d}g}$'s would appear to cancel out.\n\n$$\n\\frac{df}{dx} = \\frac{df}{dg} \\cdot \\frac{dg}{dx}\n$$\n\nTo see how the chain rule can be used to differentiate a function like\n\n$$\nf(x) = (1+2x)^3\n$$\n\nfirst, notice that the function can be written as a composite function\n\n$$\nf(g) = g^3, \\qquad g(x) = 1+2x\n$$\n\nWe can use the chain rule to compute the derivative of this composite function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{df}{dx} &= \\frac{df}{dg} \\cdot \\frac{dg}{dx} \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dg}(g^{3}) \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(1 + 2x) \\\\\n&= 3g^{2} \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 6g^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\ng(x) = 1+2x\n$$\n\nfor $g$, we obtain the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{df}{dx} &= 6(1 + 2x)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We'll provide a derivation of the chain rule at the end of the lesson." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186097878 1064 "The **chain rule** for differentiation states that the derivative of a composite function $f(g(x))$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}g} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}g} {\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nThere is some intuition behind this rule: if we think of the derivatives as fractions, the ${\\textrm{d}g}$'s would appear to cancel out.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\cancel{\\text{d}g}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{d}g}}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo see how the chain rule can be used to differentiate a function like \n\n$$\nf(x) = (1+2x)^3,\n$$\n\nfirst, notice that the function can be written as a composite function\n\n$$\nf(g) = g^3, \\qquad g(x) = 1+2x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the chain rule to compute the derivative of this composite function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}g}({g}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1+2x) \\\\  & =3{g}^{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6{g}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(x) = 1+2x$ for $g,$ we obtain the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =6(1+2x)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We'll provide a derivation of the chain rule at the end of the lesson." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186097879 1064 "The chain rule can also be expressed using primes instead of Leibniz notation.\n\nThe chain rule, in prime notation, states that\n\n$$\n(f(g(x)))' = f'(g(x)) \\cdot g'(x)\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186097879 1064 "The chain rule can also be expressed using primes instead of Leibniz notation.\n\nThe chain rule, in prime notation, states that\n\n$$\n(f(g(x)))' = f'(g(x)) \\cdot g'(x).\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186097880 1064 "Sometimes, we need to combine multiple rules of differentiation to differentiate a given function. As a reminder, the product rule, quotient rule, and chain rule are stated below.\n\n- Product rule: $\\hspace{.5cm} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} }{\\mathrm{d}x } \\left[ u(x) \\cdot v(x) \\right] = u'(x) \\cdot v(x) + u(x) \\cdot v'(x)$\n\n- Quotient rule: $\\hspace{.5cm} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} }{\\mathrm{d}x } \\left[ \\dfrac{u(x)}{v(x)} \\right] = \\dfrac{u'(x)\\cdot v(x) - u(x)\\cdot v'(x)}{[v(x)]^2}$\n\n- Chain rule: $\\hspace{.5cm} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} }{\\mathrm{d}x } \\left[ u(v(x)) \\right] = u'(v(x))\\cdot v'(x)$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186097880 1064 "Sometimes, we need to combine multiple rules of differentiation to differentiate a given function. As a reminder, the product rule, quotient rule, and chain rule are stated below.\n\n- Product rule: $\\hspace{.5cm} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\left[u(x) \\cdot v(x) \\right] = u'(x) \\cdot v(x) + u(x) \\cdot v'(x)$\n- Quotient rule: $\\hspace{.5cm} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\left[\\dfrac{u(x)}{v(x)} \\right] = \\dfrac{u'(x)\\cdot v(x) - u(x)\\cdot v'(x)}{[v(x)]^2}$\n- Chain rule: $\\hspace{.5cm} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\left[u(v(x)) \\right] = u'(v(x))\\cdot v'(x)$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186097912 1064 "Consider the graph of the function $y = f(x)$ shown below.\n\n![](images/5b/5b7ca737255a0af8a8ea0412d74a5ef63d04fe5a6b441eae103e98d3c00bad1a.png)\n\nLet's use our knowledge of derivatives to find $f'(0)$, the derivative of $f$ at $x=0$.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $f'(0)$ represents the slope of the tangent to $y=f(x)$ at the point $x=0$.\n- The function $y = f(x)$ is made up of line segments.\n- So, to find $f'(0)$, we simply need to find the slope of $y = f(x)$ over an interval that contains $x=0$, and where $f$ is represented by a single line segment.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ over $x\\in [-2,1]$.\n\n![](images/80/80dd0f95c4bd8030bae693e17185d4becee29c7681d134d3ee50a3621a184b94.png)\n\nThis interval contains $x=0$, and $f$ is represented by a single line segment over this interval.\n\nThe slope of $y=f(x)$ over this interval is equal to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx} &= (2 - 1)/(1 - (-2)) = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf'(0)=\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186097912 1064 "Consider the graph of the function $y = f(x)$ shown below. \n\n![](images/5b/5b7ca737255a0af8a8ea0412d74a5ef63d04fe5a6b441eae103e98d3c00bad1a.png)\n\nLet's use our knowledge of derivatives to find $f'(0),$ the derivative of $f$ at $x=0.$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $f'(0)$ represents the slope of the tangent to $y=f(x)$ at the point $x=0.$\n\n- The function $y = f(x)$ is made up of line segments.\n\n- So, to find $f'(0),$ we simply need to find the slope of $y = f(x)$ over an interval that contains $x=0,$ and where $f$ is represented by a single line segment.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ over $x\\in [-2,1].$ \n\n![](images/80/80dd0f95c4bd8030bae693e17185d4becee29c7681d134d3ee50a3621a184b94.png)\n\nThis interval contains $x=0,$ and $f$ is represented by a single line segment over this interval.\n\nThe slope of $y=f(x)$ over this interval is equal to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{2-1}{1-(-2)}=\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f'(0)=\\dfrac{1}{3}.$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186097925 1064 "Sometimes, we might have a system of equations with fractional coefficients, such as the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{5}{4}y=-4 \\\\ 7x-5y=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can turn the system into an equivalent system involving only integer coefficients. This will make it easier to solve.\n\nIn general, we multiply each equation by its lowest common denominator. In this instance, multiplying the first equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions $(4)$ will do the job:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{5}{4}y=-4 & |\\,\\times 4 \\\\ 7x-5y=-2 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the system using, for example, the elimination method. Notice that the $5y$ term in the first equation can cancel with the $-5y$ term in the second equation.\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable, and allows us to solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+\\cancel{5y} & =-16 \\\\ 7x-\\cancel{5y} & =-2 \\\\ 9x & =-18 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=-2$ into the equation $7x - 5y= -2$ to solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x-5y & =-2 \\\\ 7(-2)-5y & =-2 \\\\ -14-5y & =-2 \\\\ -5y & =12 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{12}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-2, \\,y=-\\dfrac{12}{5}.$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186097925 1064 "Sometimes, we might have a system of equations with fractional coefficients, such as the following:\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{2}x + \\frac{5}{4}y =-4; 7x - 5y =-2\n$$\n\nWe can turn the system into an equivalent system involving only integer coefficients. This will make it easier to solve.\n\nIn general, we multiply each equation by its lowest common denominator. In this instance, multiplying the first equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions $(4)$ will do the job:\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{2}x + \\frac{5}{4}y =-4 \\mid \\mid \\cdot 4; 7x - 5y =-2\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the system using, for example, the elimination method. Notice that the $5y$ term in the first equation can cancel with the $-5y$ term in the second equation.\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable, and allows us to solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + 5y &= -16 \\\\\n7x - 5y &= -2 \\\\\n9x &= -18 \\\\\nx &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=-2$ into the equation $7x - 5y= -2$ to solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7x - 5y &= -2 \\\\\n7(-2) - 5y &= -2 \\\\\n- 14 - 5y &= -2 \\\\\n- 5y &= 12 \\\\\ny &= -\\frac{12}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nx=-2, \\,y=-\\dfrac{12}{5}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186097935 1064 "Adding and subtracting fractions is usually straightforward. For example, suppose that we wanted to calculate\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{x-1}-\\dfrac{3}{3x-2}\n$$\n\nWe can do this easily by putting each fraction over a common denominator, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{x - 1} - \\frac{3}{3x - 2} &= ((3x - 2) - 3(x - 1))/((x - 1)(3x - 2)) \\\\\n&= (3x - 2 - 3x + 3)/((x - 1)(3x - 2)) \\\\\n&= (1)/((x - 1)(3x - 2))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBut what about if we wanted to go the other way? How do we get from\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{(x-1)(3x-2)}\n$$\n\nto\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{x-1}-\\dfrac{3}{3x-2}\\,\n$$\n\nRephrasing the problem, our task is to find $A$ and $B$ so that\n\n$$\n(1)/((x - 1)(3x - 2)) = \\frac{A}{x - 1} + \\frac{B}{3x - 2}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the process. First, we multiply the expression above by $(x-1)(3x-2)$ to clear out the fractions.\n\n$$\n1={A(3x-2)+B(x-1)}\n$$\n\nThe equation above should be true for all $x$. So let's plug in $x=1$, which gets rid of the $B$ term and allows us to solve for $A$. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 &= A(3 - 2) + B(1 - 1) \\\\\n1 &= A\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, to solve for $B$, we substitute\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac 2 3\n$$\n\n, which gets rid of the $A$ term. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 &= A(3 \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} - 2) + B(\\frac{2}{3} - 1) \\\\\n1 &= A(2 - 2) + B(-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\\n1 &= -\\frac{1}{3}B \\\\\nB &= -3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nKnowing that $A=1$ and $B=-3$, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{(x-1)(3x-2)} = \\dfrac{1}{x-1}-\\dfrac{3}{3x-2}\n$$\n\nand we're done! This process is called **partial fractions decomposition**. In this case, the **partial fractions** are\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{x-1}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{3}{3x-2}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186097935 1064 "Adding and subtracting fractions is usually straightforward. For example, suppose that we wanted to calculate\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{x-1}-\\dfrac{3}{3x-2}.\n$$\n\nWe can do this easily by putting each fraction over a common denominator, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x-1}-\\frac{3}{3x-2} & =\\frac{(3x-2)-3(x-1)}{(x-1)(3x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3x-2-3x+3}{(x-1)(3x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(x-1)(3x-2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBut what about if we wanted to go the other way? How do we get from\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{(x-1)(3x-2)}\n$$\n\nto\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{x-1}-\\dfrac{3}{3x-2}\\,?\n$$\n\nRephrasing the problem, our task is to find $A$ and $B$ so that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(x-1)(3x-2)}=\\frac{A}{x-1}+\\frac{B}{3x-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the process. First, we multiply the expression above by $(x-1)(3x-2)$ to clear out the fractions. \n\n$$\n1={A(3x-2)+B(x-1)}\n$$\n\nThe equation above should be true for all $x.$ So let's plug in $x=1,$ which gets rid of the $B$ term and allows us to solve for $A.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =A(3-2)+B(1-1) \\\\ 1 & =A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, to solve for $B,$ we substitute $x=\\dfrac 2 3$, which gets rid of the $A$ term. We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =A(3\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}-2)+B(\\frac{2}{3}-1) \\\\ 1 & =A(2-2)+B(-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ 1 & =-\\frac{1}{3}B \\\\ B & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nKnowing that $A=1$ and $B=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{(x-1)(3x-2)} = \\dfrac{1}{x-1}-\\dfrac{3}{3x-2},\n$$\n\nand we're done! This process is called **partial fractions decomposition**. In this case, the **partial fractions** are $\\dfrac{1}{x-1}$ and $-\\dfrac{3}{3x-2}.$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186097936 1064 "We can also use the substitution method to solve systems of equations containing fractional coefficients.\n\nWe proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we multiply both equations by the lowest common denominator of the fractions in the system.\n\n- Then, we apply the substitution method as usual.\n\nLet's see an example." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186097936 1064 "We can also use the substitution method to solve systems of equations containing fractional coefficients.\n\nWe proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we multiply both equations by the lowest common denominator of the fractions in the system.\n- Then, we apply the substitution method as usual.\n\nLet's see an example." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186101937 1064 "Suppose we want to compute $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 1} f(x)$ for the piecewise function $f(x)$ given below.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<1 \\\\ 2\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x=1 \\\\ -(x-1)^{3}+3\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by sketching a graph of the function:\n\n![](images/01/01e1704152432a01a1491f6d7575ada56907272e53f5eeee8a90b8bc96f3eb51.png)\n\nTo compute $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 1} f(x),$ we must compute the left and right-sided limits separately.\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x<1,$ so we use the expression $f(x) = x^3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}{x}^{3} \\\\  & ={1}^{3} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=-(x-1)^3+3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}-(x-1)^{3}+3 \\\\  & =-(1-1)^{3}+3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the left and right-sided limits are not equal, the overall limit does not exist: \n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 1} f(x) = \\text{DNE}.\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186101937 1064 "Suppose we want to compute $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 1} f(x)$ for the piecewise function $f(x)$ given below.\n\n$$\n{x^{3}\\begin{vmatrix}forx < 1 \\\\ 2\\end{vmatrix}forx = 1; - (x - 1)^{3} + 3 \\mid forx > 1\n$$\n\nWe begin by sketching a graph of the function:\n\n![](images/01/01e1704152432a01a1491f6d7575ada56907272e53f5eeee8a90b8bc96f3eb51.png)\n\nTo compute $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 1} f(x)$, we must compute the left and right-sided limits separately.\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x<1$, so we use the expression $f(x) = x^3$. We get\n$lim_(x → 1^{-})f(x)|= lim_(x → 1^{-})x^{3}; = 1^{3}; = 1$.\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>1$, so we use the expression $f(x)=-(x-1)^3+3$. We get\n$lim_(x → 1^{+})f(x)|= lim_(x → 1^{+}) - (x - 1)^{3} + 3; = - (1 - 1)^{3} + 3; = 3$.\n\nBecause the left and right-sided limits are not equal, the overall limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 1} f(x) = \\text{DNE}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186101938 1064 "Suppose we want to find $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 0}f(x)$ for the piecewise function given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)={\\begin{aligned}{3}^{x+1}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<0 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get an idea of the behavior of the function around $x=0,$ we can plot the function.\n\n![](images/78/78a832c65a4bb5862694717f52704a61660bd81a34eb7a72cfeeb98a52bb50f1.png)\n\nTo compute $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 0} f(x),$ we must compute the left and right-sided limits separately.\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x < 0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=3^{x+1}.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}{3}^{x+1} \\\\  & ={3}^{0+1} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt {x^2+9}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe left and right-sided limits both exist and are equal. So, the overall limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 0} f(x) =3.\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186101938 1064 "Suppose we want to find $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 0}f(x)$ for the piecewise function given by\n\n$$\nf(x) = {3^{x + 1}\\begin{vmatrix}forx < 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{x^{2} + 9}\\end{vmatrix}forx \\ge 0\n$$\n\nTo get an idea of the behavior of the function around $x=0$, we can plot the function.\n\n![](images/78/78a832c65a4bb5862694717f52704a61660bd81a34eb7a72cfeeb98a52bb50f1.png)\n\nTo compute $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 0} f(x)$, we must compute the left and right-sided limits separately.\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x < 0$, so we use the expression $f(x)=3^{x+1}$. We get\n$lim_(x → 0^{-})f(x)|= lim_(x → 0^{-})3^{x + 1}; = 3^{0 + 1}; = 3$.\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>0$, so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt {x^2+9}$. We get\n$lim_(x → 0^{+})f(x)|= lim_(x → 0^{+})\\sqrt{x^{2} + 9}; = \\sqrt{0^{2} + 9}; = 3$.\n\nThe left and right-sided limits both exist and are equal. So, the overall limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 0} f(x) =3\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186101948 1064 "Let's suppose we only know the values of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ at $x=0$ along with their derivatives, as given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | $g(x)$ | $f'(x)$ | $g'(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n\nCan we calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\Big( f(x)g(x)\\Big )$ at $x=0?$\n\nUsing the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\Big( f(x)g(x)\\Big) = f'(x)g(x)+f(x)g'(x).\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=0,$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\Big( f(x)g(x)\\Big) \\right|_{x=0}\t=\tf'(0)g(0)+f(0)g'(0).\n$$\n\nAll of these values are given in the table! So, substituting the values from the table into the formula, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))|}_{x=0} & ={f}^{'}(0)g(0)+f(0){g}^{'}(0) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =22.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186101948 1064 "Let's suppose we only know the values of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ at $x=0$ along with their derivatives, as given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | $g(x)$ | $f'(x)$ | $g'(x)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n\nCan we calculate\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\Big(f(x)g(x)\\Big)\n$$\n\nat $x=0$?\n\nUsing the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\Big(f(x)g(x)\\Big) = f'(x)g(x)+f(x)g'(x)\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=0$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\Big(f(x)g(x)\\Big) \\right|_{x=0} = f'(0)g(0)+f(0)g'(0)\n$$\n\nAll of these values are given in the table! So, substituting the values from the table into the formula, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left.\\frac{d}{dx}(f(x)g(x))\\right|_{x = 0} &= f^{′}(0)g(0) + f(0)g^{′}(0) \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot 3 + 2 \\cdot 5 \\\\\n&= 22\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186107033 1064 "Consider $\\triangle ABC$ with sides of lengths $a,b$, and $c$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/1d/1d30e12e2252b27a651bb02b718cf2214c7ddcf4f0e768c5bd952c11b2e4ecde.png)\n\nLet's now add the height of length $h$ perpendicular to $\\overline{BC}$, as shown.\n\n![](images/96/96817ab2168b9ec37777166a26001ee8bc7e7709f80fdf234ac6d70cdfbb5ddc.png)\n\nUsing the usual formula for the area $\\mathcal A$ of a triangle in terms of its base and height, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A = \\dfrac12 ah\n$$\n\nUsing elementary trigonometry, we have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{h}{b} = \\sin C\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nh = b\\sin C\n$$\n\nTherefore, by substituting this into our expression for $\\mathcal A$, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A = \\dfrac12ab\\sin C\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nWe can use a similar approach to prove the following formulas:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{1}{2}bc\\sin A \\\\\nA &= \\frac{1}{2}ac\\sin B\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186107033 1064 "Consider $\\triangle ABC$ with sides of lengths $a,b,$ and $c,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/1d/1d30e12e2252b27a651bb02b718cf2214c7ddcf4f0e768c5bd952c11b2e4ecde.png)\n\nLet's now add the height of length $h$ perpendicular to $\\overline{BC},$ as shown.\n\n![](images/96/96817ab2168b9ec37777166a26001ee8bc7e7709f80fdf234ac6d70cdfbb5ddc.png)\n\nUsing the usual formula for the area $\\mathcal A$ of a triangle in terms of its base and height, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A = \\dfrac12 ah.\n$$\n\nUsing elementary trigonometry, we have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{h}{b} = \\sin C\n$$\n\nwhich means that \n\n$$\nh = b\\sin C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by substituting this into our expression for $\\mathcal A,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A = \\dfrac12ab\\sin C\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nWe can use a similar approach to prove the following formulas:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}A \\\\ A & =\\frac{1}{2}ac\\operatorname{sin}B\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186107036 1064 "Multiplication of a power function by any *positive* real constant does not influence its limit at infinity. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → ∞)2x^{2} &= lim_(x → ∞)x^{2} = ∞\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbecause $y=2x^2$ is an upward parabola, just like $y=x^2$.\n\n![](images/47/47d8cef2d3d189b32ee1c37f9fa563f58670d0bdc876360b13753106df03a79d.png)\n\nOn the other hand, multiplication of a power function by a *negative* real constant changes the *sign* of the limit. For example,\n\n$$\nlim_(x → ∞) - 2x^{2} =-lim_(x → ∞)x^{2} = - ∞\n$$\n\nbecause $y=-2x^2$ is a downwards parabola that points in the opposite direction as $y=x^2$." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186107036 1064 "Multiplication of a power function by any *positive* real constant does not influence its limit at infinity. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }2{x}^{2} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{2}=\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbecause $y=2x^2$ is an upward parabola, just like $y=x^2.$\n\n![](images/47/47d8cef2d3d189b32ee1c37f9fa563f58670d0bdc876360b13753106df03a79d.png)\n\nOn the other hand, multiplication of a power function by a *negative* real constant changes the *sign* of the limit. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }-2{x}^{2}=-{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{2}=-\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbecause $y=-2x^2$ is a downwards parabola that points in the opposite direction as $y=x^2.$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186107044 1064 "Suppose we want to calculate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} x^2$ and $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} x^2$. To do this, we can use the graph of $y=x^2$, which takes the familiar shape of a parabola.\n\n![](images/58/58536c20f942b37e75a1ebb60fa6298af5fa94d1ba7b2b1cd770a0251796b436.png)\n\nOn the right side of the graph, we see that $y$ increases without bound as $x \\to \\infty$. On the left side of the graph, we have a similar situation: $y$ again increases without bound as $x \\to -\\infty$. Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} x^2=\\infty\\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} x^2=\\infty\n$$\n\nNotice that the functions $y=x^4$ and $y=x^6$ have a similar shape, and as such their limiting behavior is the same.\n\n![](images/5e/5e7386db15731b3486fd64484db69e7919c8e79821d916974f689d737cf23f8a.png)\n\nIn general, whenever $n$ is an *even natural number*, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} x^n=\\infty\\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} x^n=\\infty, \\qquad \\textrm{for}\\qquad n=2,4,6,\\ldots\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186107044 1064 "Suppose we want to calculate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} x^2$ and $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} x^2.$ To do this, we can use the graph of $y=x^2,$ which takes the familiar shape of a parabola.\n\n![](images/58/58536c20f942b37e75a1ebb60fa6298af5fa94d1ba7b2b1cd770a0251796b436.png)\n\nOn the right side of the graph, we see that $y$ increases without bound as $x \\to \\infty.$ On the left side of the graph, we have a similar situation: $y$ again increases without bound as $x \\to -\\infty.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} x^2=\\infty\\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} x^2=\\infty.\n$$\n\nNotice that the functions $y=x^4$ and $y=x^6$ have a similar shape, and as such their limiting behavior is the same.\n\n![](images/5e/5e7386db15731b3486fd64484db69e7919c8e79821d916974f689d737cf23f8a.png)\n\nIn general, whenever $n$ is an *even natural number*, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} x^n=\\infty\\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} x^n=\\infty, \\qquad \\textrm{for}\\qquad n=2,4,6,\\ldots.\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186107045 1064 "Now let's look at the graph of $y=x^3$.\n\n![](images/53/53d67c89fce03c6b949aecbe1e55fe8bf912ebadf1c3edc6d6c3601047886b5a.png)\n\nWe notice that the situation here is different:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to \\infty} x^3=\\infty \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\lim_{x \\to -\\infty} x^3=-\\infty\n$$\n\nThese limits are valid for any power function of the form $x^n$, where $n$ is an *odd natural number*.\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} x^n=\\infty\\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} x^n=-\\infty, \\qquad n=1,3,5,\\ldots\n$$\n\nAgain, multiplication of $x^n$ by a positive constant does not affect the limit at infinity. However, multiplication by a negative constant changes the sign of the limit." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186107045 1064 "Now let's look at the graph of $y=x^3.$\n\n![](images/53/53d67c89fce03c6b949aecbe1e55fe8bf912ebadf1c3edc6d6c3601047886b5a.png)\n\nWe notice that the situation here is different:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to \\infty} x^3=\\infty \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\lim_{x \\to -\\infty} x^3=-\\infty.\n$$\n\nThese limits are valid for any power function of the form $x^n,$ where $n$ is an *odd natural number*.\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} x^n=\\infty\\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} x^n=-\\infty, \\qquad n=1,3,5,\\ldots.\n$$\n\nAgain, multiplication of $x^n$ by a positive constant does not affect the limit at infinity. However, multiplication by a negative constant changes the sign of the limit." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186107046 1064 "To compute the limit at infinity of a general polynomial, we *only* need to consider the term with the largest exponent, called the **leading term**. All of the other terms can be disregarded!\n\nFor example, to compute\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to \\infty} \\left(6x + 2x^3-7x^2+12\\right) \\,\n$$\n\nwe identify the leading term and compute its limit. Here, the leading term is ${\\color{blue}{2x^3}}$, so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → ∞)(6x + 2x^{3} - 7x^{2} + 12) &= lim_(x → ∞)(2x^{3}) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → ∞)(x^{3}) \\\\\n&= ∞\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reason this works is that when $x$ becomes very large, the behavior of the polynomial is dominated by the term with the largest exponent.\n\n**Watch out!** The leading term is *not* always the first term that appears in the expression. Rather, it is the term *with the largest exponent*. This term may appear anywhere in the expression.\n\nThe reason why we call the term with the largest exponent the \"leading\" term is that when the polynomial is arranged in standard form (with exponents ordered from greatest to least), the term with the largest exponent appears first:\n$6x+ {\\color{blue}{2x^3}}-7x^2+12 \\quad \\to \\quad {\\color{blue}{2x^3}}-7x^2+6x+12$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186107046 1064 "To compute the limit at infinity of a general polynomial, we *only* need to consider the term with the largest exponent, called the **leading term**. All of the other terms can be disregarded!\n\nFor example, to compute \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to \\infty} \\left( 6x + 2x^3-7x^2+12\\right) \\, ,\n$$\n\n we identify the leading term and compute its limit. Here, the leading term is ${\\color{blue}{2x^3}},$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(6x+2{x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}+12) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2{x}^{3}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\infty \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reason this works is that when $x$ becomes very large, the behavior of the polynomial is dominated by the term with the largest exponent.\n\n**Watch out!** The leading term is *not* always the first term that appears in the expression. Rather, it is the term *with the largest exponent*. This term may appear anywhere in the expression.\n\nThe reason why we call the term with the largest exponent the \"leading\" term is that when the polynomial is arranged in standard form (with exponents ordered from greatest to least), the term with the largest exponent appears first:\n\n$$\n6x+ {\\color{blue}{2x^3}}-7x^2+12 \\quad \\to \\quad {\\color{blue}{2x^3}}-7x^2+6x+12\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186107059 1064 "The **area of a triangle** can be computed using the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\dfrac{b \\cdot h}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere $b$ is a *base* of the triangle and $h$ is the corresponding *height*.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's compute the area of the triangle with base\n$b = 4$\nand the corresponding height is\n$h = 8$,\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/84/84de11aac8e9f33a459f2a83647dac43eb2054445d842ac769511711366a15ec.png)\n\nThe area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{b \\cdot h}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 \\cdot 8}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{32}{2} \\\\\n&= 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** At the end of the lesson, we'll demonstrate where the formula comes from. But first, let's get some practice using it." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186107059 1064 "The **area of a triangle** can be computed using the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\dfrac{b \\cdot h}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $b$ is a *base* of the triangle and $h$ is the corresponding *height*.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's compute the area of the triangle with base \n$b = 4$\nand the corresponding height is\n$h = 8,$\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/84/84de11aac8e9f33a459f2a83647dac43eb2054445d842ac769511711366a15ec.png)\n\nThe area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 8}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{2} \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** At the end of the lesson, we'll demonstrate where the formula comes from. But first, let's get some practice using it." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186107061 1064 "We know that the area formula for a triangle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\dfrac{b \\cdot h}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere $b$ is a base and $h$ is the corresponding height.\n\nBut where does this formula come from?\n\nTo understand where the formula comes from, consider $\\triangle{ABC}$ below.\n\n![](images/02/02c1ace5f18e9204c1559f08f591ecd692f00691c48cbee81bc4426c3b34400e.png)\n\nLet's draw a rectangle around $\\triangle{ABC}$, as follows:\n\n![](images/89/893026324410235de9cb7ac286867006593a451556587c60219fe4375a4218a4.png)\n\nWe have two pairs of right triangles with equal areas (each pair is indicated with arrows).\n\nSo, the area of $\\triangle{ABC}$ is exactly half of the area of the rectangle. This gives\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}_{ABC} = \\dfrac{AB \\cdot CH}{2}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186107061 1064 "We know that the area formula for a triangle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\dfrac{b \\cdot h}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $b$ is a base and $h$ is the corresponding height.\n\nBut where does this formula come from?\n\nTo understand where the formula comes from, consider $\\triangle{ABC}$ below.\n\n![](images/02/02c1ace5f18e9204c1559f08f591ecd692f00691c48cbee81bc4426c3b34400e.png)\n\nLet's draw a rectangle around $\\triangle{ABC},$ as follows:\n\n![](images/89/893026324410235de9cb7ac286867006593a451556587c60219fe4375a4218a4.png)\n\nWe have two pairs of right triangles with equal areas (each pair is indicated with arrows).\n\nSo, the area of $\\triangle{ABC}$ is exactly half of the area of the rectangle. This gives\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}_{ABC} = \\dfrac{AB \\cdot CH}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186107069 1064 "The formula for the area of a general triangle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=\\frac{1}{2}{\\color{red}{a}}{\\color{blue}{b}}\\sin{C}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\color{red}a$ and $\\color{blue}b$ are two sides of the triangle and $C$ is the measure of the angle between those sides (called the **included angle**).\n\nWe'll prove this result at the end of the lesson.\n\nTo demonstrate how this works, suppose that we want to calculate the area of the non-right-angled triangle below:\n\n![](images/4b/4b0fb75f9b2eaf3d0237d8cfe2e31bc27b02425b6cffa8d15f27d6de03f60838.png)\n\nLet's call the lengths of the sides\n\n$$\nBC = \\color{red}{a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nAC ={\\color{blue}{b}}\n$$\n\nFor the triangle above we have\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}a=10}\n$$\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}b=12}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nm\\angle C=45^{\\circ}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of our triangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 10 \\cdot 12 \\cdot \\sin 45^{∘} \\\\\n&= 60 \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\\n&= 30\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186107069 1064 "The formula for the area of a general triangle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=\\frac{1}{2}{\\color{red}{a}}{\\color{blue}{b}}\\sin{C},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\color{red}a$ and $\\color{blue}b$ are two sides of the triangle and $C$ is the measure of the angle between those sides (called the **included angle**).\n\nWe'll prove this result at the end of the lesson.\n\nTo demonstrate how this works, suppose that we want to calculate the area of the non-right-angled triangle below:\n\n![](images/4b/4b0fb75f9b2eaf3d0237d8cfe2e31bc27b02425b6cffa8d15f27d6de03f60838.png)\n\nLet's call the lengths of the sides $BC = \\color{red}{a}$ and $AC ={\\color{blue}{b}}.$ For the triangle above we have\n${\\color{red}a=10},$ ${\\color{blue}b=12},$ and  $m\\angle C=45^{\\circ}.$\n\nTherefore, the area of our triangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 10\\cdot 12\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{45}^{∘} \\\\  & =60\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =30\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186107070 1064 "Constants do not vary with $x$. So, any limit of a constant evaluates to just the constant itself.\n\nFor example, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} 3=3\n$$\n\nLikewise,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} \\sqrt{\\pi}= \\sqrt{\\pi}\n$$\n\nIn general, for any constant $C$, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\pm\\infty} C=C\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186107070 1064 "Constants do not vary with $x.$ So, any limit of a constant evaluates to just the constant itself.\n\nFor example, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} 3=3.\n$$\n\nLikewise, \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} \\sqrt{ \\pi}= \\sqrt{\\pi} .\n$$\n\nIn general, for any constant $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\pm\\infty} C=C.\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186107152 1064 "Recall that for $x >0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(\\ln{x}) = \\dfrac 1 x\n$$\n\nWe can generalize this to all values\n\n$$\nx\\neq 0\n$$\n\nby including an absolute value inside the natural logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(\\ln{\\mid x \\mid}) = \\dfrac 1 x\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral of the reciprocal function is\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac 1 x \\,\\textrm{d}x = \\ln \\mid x \\mid + C\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186107152 1064 "Recall that for $x >0,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}(\\ln{x}) = \\dfrac 1 x.\n$$\n\nWe can generalize this to all values $x\\neq 0$ by including an absolute value inside the natural logarithm: \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}(\\ln{|x|}) = \\dfrac 1 x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral of the reciprocal function is\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac 1 x \\,\\textrm{d}x = \\ln |x| + C.\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186107153 1064 "For any constant $C$, we can always write $C = \\ln K$, where $K>0$ is some other constant. Then, using the laws of logarithms, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{1}{x}dx &= \\ln\\begin{vmatrix}x & + C \\\\ = \\ln & x\\end{vmatrix}+ \\ln K \\\\\n&= \\ln (K \\mid x \\mid)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186107153 1064 "For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C = \\ln K,$ where $K>0$ is some other constant. Then, using the laws of logarithms, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}|x|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}|x|+\\operatorname{ln}K \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186111820 1064 "Some linear equations have **infinitely many solutions.**\n\nFor example, consider the following equation:\n\n$$\nx+5 = x + 5\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to solve this equation by subtracting $x$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 5 &= x + 5 \\\\\nx + 5 - x &= x + 5 - x \\\\\n5 &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, what does $x$ equal? The statement $5=5$ is *always* true, irrespective of the value of $x$. This tells us that *any* value of $x$ is a solution to the equation!\n\nDon't believe it? If we substitute any value, say $x=3$, into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 5 &= x + 5 \\\\\n3 + 5 &= 3 + 5 \\\\\n8 &= 8.✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's important to note that substituting *any* value for $x$ into the equation will lead to a true statement. For example, the values\n\n$$\nx=42,\\qquad x=-\\dfrac 1 4, \\qquad x=1.2345\n$$\n\nare *all* solutions to the original equation.\n\nTo summarize:\n\n- Our original equation has *infinitely many solutions.*\n- The solution of the equation is \"*any (real) value of* $x$\"." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186111820 1064 "Some linear equations have **infinitely many solutions.** \n\nFor example, consider the following equation: \n\n$$\nx+5 = x + 5\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to solve this equation by subtracting $x$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5 & =x+5 \\\\ x+5-x & =x+5-x \\\\ 5 & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, what does $x$ equal? The statement $5=5$ is *always* true, irrespective of the value of $x.$ This tells us that *any* value of $x$ is a solution to the equation!\n\nDon't believe it? If we substitute any value, say $x=3,$ into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5 & =x+5 \\\\ 3+5 & =3+5 \\\\ 8 & =8.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's important to note that substituting *any* value for $x$ into the equation will lead to a true statement. For example, the values \n\n$$\nx=42,\\qquad x=-\\dfrac 1 4, \\qquad x=1.2345\n$$\n\nare *all* solutions to the original equation.\n\nTo summarize:\n\n- Our original equation has *infinitely many solutions.*\n\n- The solution of the equation is \"*any (real) value of* $x$\"." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186111821 1064 "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n2qx = 10x,\n$$\n\nwhere $q$ is a constant. If we're given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, how can we find the value of $q?$\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax = bx{:}$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{2q}_{\\large a} \\ x = \\underbrace{10}_{\\large b} \\ x\n$$\n\nIn this case, \n\n$$\na = 2q, \\qquad b = 10.\n$$\n\nWe know that an equation of the form $ax = bx$ has infinitely many solutions whenever $a=b.$ Therefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n2q = 10.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2q & =10 \\\\ \\frac{2q}{2} & =\\frac{10}{2} \\\\ q & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $2qx = 10x$ has infinitely many solutions if $q = 5.$\n\nWe can check that this answer is correct by substituting $q = 5$ into our original equation. Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2qx & =10x \\\\ 2(5)x & =10x \\\\ 10x & =10x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand simplifying this equation by subtracting $10x$ from both sides yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10x & =10x \\\\ 10x-10x & =10x-10x \\\\ 0 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is true for all values of $x.$\n\n**Note:** The constant $q$ in this problem is sometimes called a **parameter.**" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186111821 1064 "Consider the equation\n$2qx = 10x$,\n\nwhere $q$ is a constant. If we're given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, how can we find the value of $q$?\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax = bx{:}$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{2q}_{\\large a} \\ x = \\underbrace{10}_{\\large b} \\ x\n$$\n\nIn this case,\n\n$$\na = 2q, \\qquad b = 10\n$$\n\nWe know that an equation of the form $ax = bx$ has infinitely many solutions whenever $a=b$. Therefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n$2q = 10$.\n\nSolving this for $q$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2q &= 10 \\\\\n\\frac{2q}{2} &= \\frac{10}{2} \\\\\nq &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $2qx = 10x$ has infinitely many solutions if $q = 5$.\n\nWe can check that this answer is correct by substituting $q = 5$ into our original equation. Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2qx &= 10x \\\\\n2(5)x &= 10x \\\\\n10x &= 10x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand simplifying this equation by subtracting $10x$ from both sides yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10x &= 10x \\\\\n10x - 10x &= 10x - 10x \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is true for all values of $x$.\n\n**Note:** The constant $q$ in this problem is sometimes called a **parameter.**" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186111823 1064 "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(s - 2)x + t = 6x + 9\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ and $t$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, how can we find the values of $s$ and $t$?\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form\n\n$$\nax+b = cx+d{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(s-2)}_{\\large a} \\ x + \\underbrace{t}_{\\large b} = \\underbrace{6}_{\\large c} \\ x + \\underbrace{9}_{\\large d}\n$$\n\nWe know that an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b = cx+d\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions when $a=c$ and $b=d$.\n\nSo, to determine the values of $s$ and $t$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n({\\color{blue}{{s - 2}}})x + {\\color{red}{{t}}} = {\\color{blue}{{6}}}x + {\\color{red}{{9}}}\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we **equate the coefficients** of $x$, and we **equate the constants:**\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x$, we have\n${\\color{blue}{{s - 2}}} = {\\color{blue}{{6}}}$.\nSolving this equation for $s$, we get\n$s|= 8$.\n- Equating the constants, we get\n${\\color{red}{{t}}} = {\\color{red}{{9}}}$.\n\nTherefore, if $s = 8$ and $t=9$, our original equation has infinitely many solutions." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186111823 1064 "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(s - 2)x + t = 6x + 9,\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ and $t$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, how can we find the values of $s$ and $t?$\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax+b = cx+d{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(s-2)}_{\\large a} \\ x + \\underbrace{t}_{\\large b} = \\underbrace{6}_{\\large c} \\ x + \\underbrace{9}_{\\large d}\n$$\n\nWe know that an equation of the form $ax+b = cx+d$ has infinitely many solutions when $a=c$ and $b=d.$\n\nSo, to determine the values of $s$ and $t$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n({\\color{blue}{{s - 2}}})x + {\\color{red}{{t}}} = {\\color{blue}{{6}}}x + {\\color{red}{{9}}}\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we **equate the coefficients** of $x,$ and we **equate the constants:**\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{{s - 2}}} = {\\color{blue}{{6}}}.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $s,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{{t}}} = {\\color{red}{{9}}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $s = 8$ and $t=9,$ our original equation has infinitely many solutions." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186111824 1064 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax = bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a = b$, then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nFor example, each of the following equations has infinitely many solutions:\n\n$$\n2x = 2x, \\frac{1}{3}x = \\frac{1}{3}x - 2.16x =-2.16x\n$$\n\nTo show that the general equation $ax = bx$ has infinitely many solutions whenever $a=b$, we can substitute $a$ for $b$ in our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nax &= bx \\\\\nax &= (a)x \\\\\nax &= ax\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we simplify this equation by subtracting $ax$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nax &= ax \\\\\nax - ax &= ax - ax \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation, $0=0$, is always true, regardless of the value of $x$. Therefore, the equation $ax = bx$ has infinitely many solutions whenever $a=b$, and any value of $x$ is a solution to the equation." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186111824 1064 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax = bx,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a = b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nFor example, each of the following equations has infinitely many solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x=2x,\\,\\frac{1}{3}x=\\frac{1}{3}x\\,-2.16x=-2.16x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo show that the general equation $ax = bx$ has infinitely many solutions whenever $a=b,$ we can substitute $a$ for $b$ in our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax & =bx \\\\ ax & =(a)x \\\\ ax & =ax\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we simplify this equation by subtracting $ax$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax & =ax \\\\ ax-ax & =ax-ax \\\\ 0 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation, $0=0,$ is always true, regardless of the value of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $ax = bx$ has infinitely many solutions whenever $a=b,$ and any value of $x$ is a solution to the equation." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186111825 1064 "Each of the following equations has infinitely many solutions:\n\n$$\n2x + 1 = 2x + 1, \\qquad \\dfrac13x -2 = \\dfrac13x -2, \\qquad 1-x = 1 - x\n$$\n\nMore generally, suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax + b = cx + d\n$$\n\nwhere $a, b, c$, and $d$ are constants. Then, this equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\na = c\\quad \\text{and}\\quad b = d\n$$\n\nNote that if we substitute $c=a$ and $d=b$ into this equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nax + b &= cx + d \\\\\nax + b &= (a)x + (b) \\\\\nax + b &= ax + b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying this equation by subtracting $ax$ from both sides of the equation gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nax + b &= ax + b \\\\\nax + b - ax &= ax + b - ax \\\\\nb &= b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation, $b = b$, is always true. Therefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions, and any real value of $x$ is a solution." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186111825 1064 "Each of the following equations has infinitely many solutions:\n\n$$\n2x + 1 = 2x + 1, \\qquad \\dfrac13x -2 = \\dfrac13x -2, \\qquad 1-x = 1 - x\n$$\n\nMore generally, suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax + b = cx + d,\n$$\n\nwhere $a, b, c,$ and $d$ are constants. Then, this equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na = c\\quad \\text{and}\\quad b = d.\n$$\n\nNote that if we substitute $c=a$ and $d=b$ into this equation, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax+b & =cx+d \\\\ ax+b & =(a)x+(b) \\\\ ax+b & =ax+b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying this equation by subtracting $ax$ from both sides of the equation gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax+b & =ax+b \\\\ ax+b-ax & =ax+b-ax \\\\ b & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation, $b = b,$ is always true. Therefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions, and any real value of $x$ is a solution." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186111873 1064 "Recall that the coordinates of a point on the unit circle with a central angle $\\theta$ have the property\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nIf this point is in the first quadrant, we can substitute these values into the Pythagorean Identity from before to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + y^{2} &= 1 \\\\\n(\\cos θ)^{2} + (\\sin θ)^{2} &= 1 \\\\\n\\cos^{2} θ + \\sin^{2} θ &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last expression is known as the **Pythagorean trigonometric identity,** and is arguably the most important equation in trigonometry.\n\nThe Pythagorean trigonometric identity holds for *every* angle $\\theta$, not just if $\\theta$ is in the first quadrant. In this lesson, we're focusing on the first quadrant only. We'll learn how to deal with angles in other quadrants in a future lesson." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186111873 1064 "Recall that the coordinates of a point on the unit circle with a central angle $\\theta$ have the property\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf this point is in the first quadrant, we can substitute these values into the Pythagorean Identity from before to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2} & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}\\theta +{sin}^{2}\\theta  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last expression is known as the **Pythagorean trigonometric identity,** and is arguably the most important equation in trigonometry.\n\nThe Pythagorean trigonometric identity holds for *every* angle $\\theta,$ not just if $\\theta$ is in the first quadrant. In this lesson, we're focusing on the first quadrant only. We'll learn how to deal with angles in other quadrants in a future lesson." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186111878 1064 "Suppose that $P$ is a point in the first quadrant that lies on the unit circle. We can then draw a right triangle, where the hypotenuse is the line segment between the origin and the point.\n\n![](images/4b/4bae3a49b5557df9e5b50280d744aa72048b2d224df042e103a6b5d156467de7.png)\n\nSince the hypotenuse is the radius of the unit circle, its length must be equal to $1$. And, since the triangle is a right triangle, the $(x,y)$ coordinates of any point on the unit circle in the first quadrant must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2=1\n$$\n\nWe can use this to find the $y$-coordinate of a point given the $x$-coordinate, and vice-versa.\n\nFor example, suppose that the $x$-coordinate of a point $P$ in the first quadrant on the unit circle is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{7}\n$$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{3}{7}\n$$\n\ninto the above and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} x^2+y^2&=1 \\\\[5pt] \\left(\\dfrac{3}{7}\\right)^2+y^2&=1 \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac{9}{49}+y^2&=1 \\\\[5pt] y^2&= 1-\\dfrac{9}{49} \\\\[5pt] y^2&= \\dfrac{40}{49} \\\\[5pt] y&= \\pm\\sqrt{\\dfrac{40}{49}} \\\\[5pt] y &= \\pm \\dfrac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $P$ lies in the first quadrant, $y$ must be positive. So we select the positive answer.\n\nTherefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186111878 1064 "Suppose that $P$ is a point in the first quadrant that lies on the unit circle. We can then draw a right triangle, where the hypotenuse is the line segment between the origin and the point. \n\n![](images/4b/4bae3a49b5557df9e5b50280d744aa72048b2d224df042e103a6b5d156467de7.png)\n\nSince the hypotenuse is the radius of the unit circle, its length must be equal to $1.$ And, since the triangle is a right triangle, the $(x,y)$ coordinates of any point on the unit circle in the first quadrant must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2=1.\n$$\n\nWe can use this to find the $y$-coordinate of a point given the $x$-coordinate, and vice-versa.\n\nFor example, suppose that the $x$-coordinate of a point $P$ in the first quadrant on the unit circle is $\\dfrac{3}{7}.$ To find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute $x=\\dfrac{3}{7}$ into the above and solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tx^2+y^2&=1\\[5pt]\n\t          \\left(\\dfrac{3}{7}\\right)^2+y^2&=1\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac{9}{49}+y^2&=1\\[5pt]\n\t\ty^2&= 1-\\dfrac{9}{49}\\[5pt]\n\t\ty^2&= \\dfrac{40}{49}\\[5pt]\n\t\ty&= \\pm\\sqrt{\\dfrac{40}{49}}\\[5pt]\n\t        y &= \\pm \\dfrac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}\n\n}\n$$\n\nSince $P$ lies in the first quadrant, $y$ must be positive. So we select the positive answer. \n\nTherefore, our final answer is $y=\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}.$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186111921 1064 "Two lines are **perpendicular** if they intersect at a right angle. For example, the lines\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}y = 2x - 1}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}y=-\\dfrac{1}{2}x+5}\n$$\n\nshown below are perpendicular:\n\n![](images/1a/1a6eb1344ebbc9b41f58e7e68594d6913f4ce75a60ed0f0a3bd869168d088e08.png)\n\nWe can tell whether two lines are perpendicular by looking at their slopes. In general,\n\n> *If two lines are perpendicular, then the product of their slopes is equal to $-1$.*\n\nWe see this with the two lines graphed above: the lines\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}y = 2x - 1}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}y=-\\dfrac{1}{2}x+5}\n$$\n\nhave slopes $\\color{red}2$ and\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}-\\dfrac{1}{2}}\n$$\n\nrespectively. If we take the product of the slopes, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}2} \\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)}} = -1\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** The only exception is for horizontal and vertical lines. Horizontal and vertical lines are perpendicular, but the slope of a vertical line is undefined, so we cannot multiply it by anything." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186111921 1064 "Two lines are **perpendicular** if they intersect at a right angle. For example, the lines ${\\color{red}y = 2x - 1}$ and ${\\color{blue}y=-\\dfrac{1}{2}x+5}$ shown below are perpendicular:\n\n![](images/1a/1a6eb1344ebbc9b41f58e7e68594d6913f4ce75a60ed0f0a3bd869168d088e08.png)\n\nWe can tell whether two lines are perpendicular by looking at their slopes. In general,\n\n> *If two lines are perpendicular, then the product of their slopes is equal to $-1.$*\n\nWe see this with the two lines graphed above: the lines ${\\color{red}y = 2x - 1}$ and ${\\color{blue}y=-\\dfrac{1}{2}x+5}$ have slopes $\\color{red}2$ and ${\\color{blue}-\\dfrac{1}{2}},$ respectively. If we take the product of the slopes, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}2} \\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\left( -\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right) }} = -1.\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** The only exception is for horizontal and vertical lines. Horizontal and vertical lines are perpendicular, but the slope of a vertical line is undefined, so we cannot multiply it by anything." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186116422 1064 "To plot the graph of $y=-2(3^x)$, we take the curve $y=2(3^x)$ and reflect it in the $x$-axis, as shown below:\n\n![](images/61/6130842f788070c7e97fe82698b701293af681a6aba3acfce0f4dde269cddbcf.png)\n\nThis transformation is called a **reflection in the $\\boldsymbol{x}$-axis**, and it occurs because the output $y$-values of the function $y=2(3^x)$ are made negative by the transformation.\n\nIf we combine vertical translations with reflection, we get a general form\n\n$$\ny = -a(b^x) + k\n$$\n\nThis is the equation of a reflected exponential graph $y=-a(b^x)$ that is translated $k$ units up." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186116422 1064 "To plot the graph of $y=-2(3^x),$ we take the curve $y=2(3^x)$ and reflect it in the $x$-axis, as shown below:\n\n![](images/61/6130842f788070c7e97fe82698b701293af681a6aba3acfce0f4dde269cddbcf.png)\n\nThis transformation is called a **reflection in the $\\boldsymbol{x}$-axis**, and it occurs because the output $y$-values of the function $y=2(3^x)$ are made negative by the transformation.\n\nIf we combine vertical translations with reflection, we get a general form\n\n$$\ny = -a(b^x) + k.\n$$\n\nThis is the equation of a reflected exponential graph $y=-a(b^x)$ that is translated $k$ units up." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186116441 1064 "Consider the graph of $y=e^x$ below. We will use this graph to compute $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 1} e^x \\, .$\n\n![](images/9d/9d83670c0d8b06b6412c8ca22a73a91ca0c2e05290eb0dbe34418cdf8e8f242c.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $1,$ the value of $y$ approaches $e^1.$ So\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 1} e^x = e^1 = e.\n$$\n\nThe limit of the function at a point is equal to the value of the function at that point, and this is true for *any* point, not just $x=1.$ If $c$ is any real number, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to c} e^x = e^c.\n$$\n\nIn fact, *all* exponential functions have this property. For $a \\gt 0$ and any real number $c,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to c} a^x = a^c.\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186116441 1064 "Consider the graph of $y=e^x$ below. We will use this graph to compute $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 1} e^x \\,$.\n\n![](images/9d/9d83670c0d8b06b6412c8ca22a73a91ca0c2e05290eb0dbe34418cdf8e8f242c.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $1$, the value of $y$ approaches $e^1$. So\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 1} e^x = e^1 = e\n$$\n\nThe limit of the function at a point is equal to the value of the function at that point, and this is true for *any* point, not just $x=1$. If $c$ is any real number, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to c} e^x = e^c\n$$\n\nIn fact, *all* exponential functions have this property. For $a \\gt 0$ and any real number $c$, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to c} a^x = a^c\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186116442 1064 "Consider the graphs of $y = 2^x$ and $y = 2^{-x}$ shown below. We will use these graphs to compute the limits at infinity.\n\n![](images/a3/a3189dbd6f2eb17099aff5b4fd533ce19277f6aa0021239aa2fe812ac86c7a7a.png)\n\nConsidering $y = 2^x$ first, we note that as $x \\to \\infty$, the function increases without bound, and as $x \\to -\\infty$, the function approaches zero. So\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty} 2^x = \\infty\\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim_{x\\to -\\infty} 2^x =0\n$$\n\nThe same properties hold true for any exponential function $y=a^x$ where $a \\gt 1$.\n\nNow consider the curve $y=2^{-x}$. We have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty} 2^{-x} = 0\\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim_{x\\to -\\infty} 2^{-x} =\\infty\n$$\n\nAgain, the same properties are true for any exponential function $y=a^{-x}$ for $a \\gt 1$." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186116442 1064 "Consider the graphs of $y = 2^x$ and $y = 2^{-x}$ shown below. We will use these graphs to compute the limits at infinity.\n\n![](images/a3/a3189dbd6f2eb17099aff5b4fd533ce19277f6aa0021239aa2fe812ac86c7a7a.png)\n\nConsidering $y = 2^x$ first, we note that as $x \\to \\infty,$ the function increases without bound, and as $x \\to -\\infty,$ the function approaches zero. So\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty} 2^x = \\infty\\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim_{x\\to -\\infty} 2^x =0.\n$$\n\nThe same properties hold true for any exponential function $y=a^x$ where $a \\gt 1.$\n\nNow consider the curve $y=2^{-x}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty} 2^{-x} = 0\\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim_{x\\to -\\infty} 2^{-x} =\\infty.\n$$\n\nAgain, the same properties are true for any exponential function $y=a^{-x}$ for $a \\gt 1.$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186116455 1064 "Consider $\\triangle ABC$ shown below.\n\n![](images/6f/6fdb9a8337d16f1ef1910989b3df92844c5393c33108f98ae7993cc939bed0b3.png)\n\nNow, let's draw a line segment that\n\n- runs from $\\color{blue}C$ to the line ${\\color{red}\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB}}$, and\n- is *perpendicular* to ${\\color{red}\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB}}$.\n\nThis line segment is the dashed segment shown below:\n\n![](images/4a/4a7ee2771c9b0c60dbb07c43b0439e822ea9b2c261ac41af9b44fec72b9f2a91.png)\n\nFinally, let's label the other endpoint of our segment ${\\color{blue}{H}}$.\n\n![](images/75/758eab533719220515460b6677054438a9ac0e2701d1a5267b973e526f63bd90.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The line segment ${\\color{blue}\\overline{CH}}$ is called the **height** (or **altitude**) *corresponding to* ${\\color{red}\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB}}$.\n- The line $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB}$ is called the **base** of the altitude ${\\color{blue}\\overline{CH}}$.\n\nAs we'll soon discover, the heights and their corresponding bases are important attributes of triangles." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186116455 1064 "Consider $\\triangle ABC$ shown below. \n\n![](images/6f/6fdb9a8337d16f1ef1910989b3df92844c5393c33108f98ae7993cc939bed0b3.png)\n\nNow, let's draw a line segment that \n\n- runs from $\\color{blue}C$ to the line ${\\color{red}\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB}},$ and\n\n- is *perpendicular* to ${\\color{red}\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB}}.$\n\nThis line segment is the dashed segment shown below:\n\n![](images/4a/4a7ee2771c9b0c60dbb07c43b0439e822ea9b2c261ac41af9b44fec72b9f2a91.png)\n\nFinally, let's label the other endpoint of our segment ${\\color{blue}{H}}.$\n\n![](images/75/758eab533719220515460b6677054438a9ac0e2701d1a5267b973e526f63bd90.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The line segment ${\\color{blue}\\overline{CH}}$ is called the **height** (or **altitude**) *corresponding to* ${\\color{red}\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB}}.$\n\n- The line $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB}$ is called the **base** of the altitude ${\\color{blue}\\overline{CH}}.$\n\nAs we'll soon discover, the heights and their corresponding bases are important attributes of triangles." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186116456 1064 "We can draw exactly three heights (or altitudes) in any triangle. However, sometimes the height lies outside the triangle.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider $\\triangle{ABC}$ below.\n\n![](images/df/df1ac2c1074305f8ead506d9aeb8c3927ad51ae5949b45976f1971b52d402ee5.png)\n\nThe height corresponding to $\\overline{BC}$ is a line segment that\n\n- runs from the opposite vertex $A$ to the line $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{BC}$, and\n- is perpendicular to $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{BC}$.\n\nIn this case, it's impossible to draw a perpendicular from $A$ to the *segment* $\\overline{BC}$. However, we can first *extend* the line segment $\\overline{BC}$ as follows:\n\n![](images/26/26bf9eb8af1e9b71c263c08cae5797d5c911410985cdc01213ac2c295f7b8d46.png)\n\nNow, we draw the perpendicular from $A$ to $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{BC}$. This gives the height $\\overline{AH}{:}$\n\n![](images/89/894686fce1393ebcf998be6885020627c9f3cde5916adfec67234f15f8895826.png)" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186116456 1064 "We can draw exactly three heights (or altitudes) in any triangle. However, sometimes the height lies outside the triangle.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider $\\triangle{ABC}$ below.\n\n![](images/df/df1ac2c1074305f8ead506d9aeb8c3927ad51ae5949b45976f1971b52d402ee5.png)\n\nThe height corresponding to $\\overline{BC}$ is a line segment that \n\n- runs from the opposite vertex $A$ to the line $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{BC},$ and\n\n- is perpendicular to $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{BC}.$\n\nIn this case, it's impossible to draw a perpendicular from $A$ to the *segment* $\\overline{BC}.$ However, we can first *extend* the line segment $\\overline{BC}$ as follows:\n\n![](images/26/26bf9eb8af1e9b71c263c08cae5797d5c911410985cdc01213ac2c295f7b8d46.png)\n\nNow, we draw the perpendicular from $A$ to $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{BC}.$ This gives the height $\\overline{AH}{:}$\n\n![](images/89/894686fce1393ebcf998be6885020627c9f3cde5916adfec67234f15f8895826.png)" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186116459 1064 "Consider the right triangle $\\triangle ABC$ shown below.\n\n![](images/3f/3f7de9810f4acf33b504eb010d641e99811de82eed919e4340b5e68d414269ca.png)\n\nThe area of this right triangle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A = \\dfrac 1 2 ab\n$$\n\nBut how can we calculate the area if we only know one side and one angle, as in the triangle below?\n\n![](images/c1/c1e5331333639f4d8e4f7b4cb3fee30ed1a7a889968844ae2b10961c5ae8e3ef.png)\n\nWe can follow the two steps below.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the missing side using the trigonometric ratios.\n\nHere, we need the adjacent side, and we know the opposite side. So, we should use tangent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan (B) &= \\frac{AC}{BC} \\\\\n\\tan 30^{∘} &= \\frac{3}{BC} \\\\\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} &= \\frac{3}{BC} \\\\\nBC\\sqrt{3} &= 9 \\\\\nBC &= \\frac{9}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\\nBC &= 3\\sqrt{3}cm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Use the formula to calculate the area.\n\nNow that we know both sides of the triangle, we can just apply the formula as usual and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{1}{2}AC \\cdot BC \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3\\sqrt{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{9\\sqrt{3}}{2}cm^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186116459 1064 "Consider the right triangle $\\triangle ABC$ shown below.\n\n![](images/3f/3f7de9810f4acf33b504eb010d641e99811de82eed919e4340b5e68d414269ca.png)\n\nThe area of this right triangle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A = \\dfrac 1 2 ab.\n$$\n\nBut how can we calculate the area if we only know one side and one angle, as in the triangle below? \n\n![](images/c1/c1e5331333639f4d8e4f7b4cb3fee30ed1a7a889968844ae2b10961c5ae8e3ef.png)\n\nWe can follow the two steps below.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the missing side using the trigonometric ratios.\n\nHere, we need the adjacent side, and we know the opposite side. So, we should use tangent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}(B) & =\\frac{AC}{BC} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}{30}^{∘} & =\\frac{3}{BC} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} & =\\frac{3}{BC} \\\\ BC\\sqrt{3} & =9 \\\\ BC & =\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ BC & =3\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Use the formula to calculate the area.\n\nNow that we know both sides of the triangle, we can just apply the formula as usual and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}AC\\cdot BC \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 3\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186116479 1064 "The derivatives of the reciprocal trigonometric functions are shown in the table below:\n\n| $f(x)$ | $f'(x)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\sec x$ | $\\sec x\\tan x$ |\n| $\\csc x$ | $-\\csc x\\cot x$ |\n| $\\cot x$ | $-\\csc^2 x$ |\n\nAs with the standard trigonometric functions, we can now differentiate any combination of the above functions using the rules of differentiation.\n\nFor example, let's find the derivative of \n\n$$\ny= \\sec x + \\csc x.\n$$\n\nWe can take the derivative of each term and then add them, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sec}x+\\operatorname{csc}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sec}x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{csc}x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x-\\operatorname{csc}x\\operatorname{cot}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We could have computed the derivative of the above function by rewriting it as\n\n$$\ny= \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x} + \\dfrac{1}{\\sin x}\n$$\n\nand applying the appropriate derivative rules. However, this would have taken significantly more work! You can save a lot of time by memorizing the derivatives of the reciprocal trigonometric functions." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186116479 1064 "The derivatives of the reciprocal trigonometric functions are shown in the table below:\n\n| $f(x)$ | $f'(x)$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $\\sec x$ | $\\sec x\\tan x$ |\n| $\\csc x$ | $-\\csc x\\cot x$ |\n| $\\cot x$ | $-\\csc^2 x$ |\n\nAs with the standard trigonometric functions, we can now differentiate any combination of the above functions using the rules of differentiation.\n\nFor example, let's find the derivative of\n\n$$\ny= \\sec x + \\csc x\n$$\n\nWe can take the derivative of each term and then add them, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\sec x + \\csc x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\sec x) + \\frac{d}{dx}(\\csc x) \\\\\n&= \\sec x\\tan x - \\csc x\\cot x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We could have computed the derivative of the above function by rewriting it as\n\n$$\ny= \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x} + \\dfrac{1}{\\sin x}\n$$\n\nand applying the appropriate derivative rules. However, this would have taken significantly more work! You can save a lot of time by memorizing the derivatives of the reciprocal trigonometric functions." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186116480 1064 "To see why the derivative of $\\sec x$ is $\\sec x \\tan x$, we start by remembering that\n\n$$\n\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}\n$$\n\nIf we now differentiate the above relation using the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\sec x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{1}{\\cos x}) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{d}{dx}(1) \\cdot \\cos x - 1 \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(\\cos x))/([\\cos x]^{2}) \\\\\n&= ((0) \\cdot \\cos x - 1 \\cdot (-\\sin x))/(\\cos^{2} x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos^{2} x} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\cos x}_(⏟)_(\\sec x)\\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}_(⏟)_(\\tan x) \\\\\n&= \\sec x\\tan x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the same method to find the derivative of the other two reciprocal trigonometric functions,\n\n$$\n\\csc x = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin x} \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad \\cot x = \\dfrac{1}{\\tan x}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186116480 1064 "To see why the derivative of $\\sec x$ is $\\sec x \\tan x,$ we start by remembering that\n\n$$\n\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}.\n$$\n\nIf we now differentiate the above relation using the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sec}x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}x-1\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}x)}{{[\\operatorname{cos}x]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}x-1\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}x)}{{cos}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{{cos}^{2}x} \\\\  & ={{\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}x}}_{⏟}}_{\\operatorname{sec}x}{{\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x}}_{⏟}}_{\\operatorname{tan}x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the same method to find the derivative of the other two reciprocal trigonometric functions,\n\n$$\n\\csc x = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin x} \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad \\cot x = \\dfrac{1}{\\tan x}.\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186116484 1064 "Suppose we are given the equation\n\n$$\n3^{3x} = 81^{x-1}\n$$\n\nwhich has two exponential terms. How do we solve it?\n\nTo solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base. To do this, we rewrite the equation using the fact that\n\n$$\n81 = {\\color{red}3}^4\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}3}^{3x}=\\left({\\color{red}3}^4\\right)^{x-1}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3^{3x} &= 3^{4(x - 1)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $({\\color{red}3})$, we equate the exponents and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x &= 4(x - 1) \\\\\n3x &= 4x - 4 \\\\\nx &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhenever we can rewrite an equation so that both sides have a common base, we can solve the equation by equating the exponents." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186116484 1064 "Suppose we are given the equation $3^{3x} = 81^{x-1},$ which has two exponential terms. How do we solve it?\n\nTo solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base. To do this, we rewrite the equation using the fact that $81 = {\\color{red}3}^4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}3}^{3x}=\\left({\\color{red}3}^4\\right)^{x-1}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{3x} & ={3}^{4(x-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $({\\color{red}3}),$ we equate the exponents and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =4(x-1) \\\\ 3x & =4x-4 \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhenever we can rewrite an equation so that both sides have a common base, we can solve the equation by equating the exponents." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186116485 1064 "Consider the exponential function\n\n$$\nf(x)=2^x\n$$\n\nIts domain is given by\n\n$x \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$.\nBecause the domain consists of all real numbers, if we were to apply graph transformations to $y = f(x)$, the domain would remain unchanged.\n\nHowever, applying graph transformations could affect other properties of $f(x)$, such as its range, intercepts, end-behavior, and roots. In this lesson, we will learn how to calculate the properties of transformed exponential functions." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186116485 1064 "Consider the exponential function $f(x)=2^x.$ Its domain is given by \n\n$$\nx \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$\n\nBecause the domain consists of all real numbers, if we were to apply graph transformations to $y = f(x),$ the domain would remain unchanged.\n\nHowever, applying graph transformations could affect other properties of $f(x),$ such as its range, intercepts, end-behavior, and roots. In this lesson, we will learn how to calculate the properties of transformed exponential functions." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186116530 1064 "Trigonometric functions appear very frequently in calculus, so it's extremely important to commit their derivatives to memory.\n\nConveniently, there are some mnemonic tricks that we can use to more easily remember the derivatives of trigonometric functions.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{d}{dx}(\\sin x) &= \\cos x & \\frac{d}{dx}(\\cos x)|=-\\sin x \\\\ \\frac{d}{dx}(\\tan x)|= \\sec^{2} x & \\frac{d}{dx}(\\sec x)|= \\sec x\\tan x \\\\ \\frac{d}{dx}(\\cot x)|=-\\csc^{2} x & \\frac{d}{dx}(\\csc x)|=-\\csc x\\cot x\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's helpful to notice that the \"co\"-functions (cosine, cosecant, and cotangent) all have negative signs in their derivatives, while the other trigonometric functions (sine, secant, and tangent) do not.\n\nIt's also helpful to realize that functions come in pairs.\n\n- Sine and cosine are a pair because each appears in the other's derivative.\n- Tangent and secant are a pair because each appears in the other's derivative.\n- Cotangent and cosecant are a pair because each appears in the other's derivative.\n\nLastly, it's helpful to realize the following:\n\n- Ignoring the positive or negative sign, tangent and cotangent have a similar format for their derivatives: the square of the function they're paired with.\n- Ignoring the positive or negative sign, secant and cosecant have a similar format for their derivatives: the product of themself and the function they're paired with." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186116530 1064 "Trigonometric functions appear very frequently in calculus, so it's extremely important to commit their derivatives to memory.\n\nConveniently, there are some mnemonic tricks that we can use to more easily remember the derivatives of trigonometric functions.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}x) & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\, & \\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}x) & ={sec}^{2}x\\, & \\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sec}x) & =\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cot}x) & =-{csc}^{2}x\\, & \\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{csc}x) & =-\\operatorname{csc}x\\operatorname{cot}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's helpful to notice that the \"co\"-functions (cosine, cosecant, and cotangent) all have negative signs in their derivatives, while the other trigonometric functions (sine, secant, and tangent) do not.\n\nIt's also helpful to realize that functions come in pairs.\n\n- Sine and cosine are a pair because each appears in the other's derivative.\n\n- Tangent and secant are a pair because each appears in the other's derivative.\n\n- Cotangent and cosecant are a pair because each appears in the other's derivative.\n\nLastly, it's helpful to realize the following:\n\n- Ignoring the positive or negative sign, tangent and cotangent have a similar format for their derivatives: the square of the function they're paired with.\n\n- Ignoring the positive or negative sign, secant and cosecant have a similar format for their derivatives: the product of themself and the function they're paired with." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186116532 1064 "When analyzing data, we often want to know how \"spread out\" the data is. One way to represent the spread of the data is to use the **variance** of the data.\n\nIf we have a data set that consists of the points $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$, then the variance, denoted by $\\sigma^2$, is defined as\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n x_i\n$$\n\nis the mean of the data set.\n\nThere's some nice intuition behind the formula for the variance. The mean $\\overline{x}$ can be interpreted as the \"center\" of the data set, and the squared difference $(x_i - \\overline{x})^2$ represents the squared distance between $x_i$ and the center. So, the variance gives the average squared distance of the data points from the center.\n\nIn general,\n\n- a data set that is *spread out* over many possible values will have a *high* variance, while\n- a data set that is *concentrated* (or *clumped*) near a single value will have a *low* variance.\n\n**Note**: The formula for the variance given here assumes that the points $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ include every member of the population we're interested in. There is a slightly different formula for variance when these points represent a sample drawn from a population. However, we won't worry about the difference for now." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186116532 1064 "When analyzing data, we often want to know how \"spread out\" the data is. One way to represent the spread of the data is to use the **variance** of the data. \n\nIf we have a data set that consists of the points $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n,$ then the variance, denoted by $\\sigma^2,$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 =  \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{x} = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n x_i$ is the mean of the data set.\n\nThere's some nice intuition behind the formula for the variance. The mean $\\overline{x}$ can be interpreted as the \"center\" of the data set, and the squared difference $(x_i - \\overline{x} )^2$ represents the squared distance between $x_i$ and the center. So, the variance gives the average squared distance of the data points from the center.\n\nIn general,\n\n- a data set that is *spread out* over many possible values will have a *high* variance, while\n\n- a data set that is *concentrated* (or *clumped*) near a single value will have a *low* variance.\n\n**Note**: The formula for the variance given here assumes that the points $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ include every member of the population we're interested in. There is a slightly different formula for variance when these points represent a sample drawn from a population. However, we won't worry about the difference for now." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186116534 1064 "When summarizing a data set, we often want to produce a *single* number that best represents *all* numbers in the data set. One way to do this is to find the **mean** of the data set. The mean can be considered the \"center\" of the data set.\n\nIf we have a data set that consists of the points $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$, then the mean, denoted $\\overline{x}$, is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^n x_i\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's compute the mean of the data set below:\n\n$$\n1, \\: 7, \\: 3, \\: 9\n$$\n\nHere, there are $n=4$ data points. Adding up all the points in the data set, we get\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n x_i = 1 + 7 + 3 + 9 = 20\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^(―) &= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot 20 \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, to compute the mean of a set of numbers, all we need to do is add up all the numbers and divide the result by how many numbers we added." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186116534 1064 "When summarizing a data set, we often want to produce a *single* number that best represents *all* numbers in the data set. One way to do this is to find the **mean** of the data set. The mean can be considered the \"center\" of the data set.\n\nIf we have a data set that consists of the points $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n,$ then the mean, denoted $\\overline{x},$ is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^n x_i\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's compute the mean of the data set below:\n\n$$\n1, \\: 7, \\: 3, \\: 9\n$$\n\nHere, there are $n=4$ data points. Adding up all the points in the data set, we get\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n x_i = 1 + 7 + 3 + 9 = 20.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 20 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, to compute the mean of a set of numbers, all we need to do is add up all the numbers and divide the result by how many numbers we added." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186116535 1064 "The **standard deviation** of a data set $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is defined as the square root of the variance:\n\n$$\n\\sigma = \\sqrt{ \\sigma^2 } = \\sqrt{ \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2 } = \\sqrt{ \\,\\,\\, \\vphantom{\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n} \\overline{x^2} - \\left( \\overline{x} \\right)^2 }\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's again consider the data set below:\n\n$$\n1, \\: 7, \\: 3, \\: 9\n$$\n\nPreviously, we found that the variance of this data set was $\\sigma^2 = 10.$ So, the standard deviation is given by\n\n$$\n\\sigma = \\sqrt{ 10 }.\n$$\n\nAs we will see in the future, the standard deviation is often used in calculations where we want to quantify how \"surprising\" a particular observation is.\n\nIn those contexts, we often compute how many standard deviations the observed value was away from the mean: the further away, the more surprising the observation.\n\nFor now, though, let's get some practice computing the standard deviation." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186116535 1064 "The **standard deviation** of a data set $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is defined as the square root of the variance:\n\n$$\n\\sigma = \\sqrt{\\sigma^2} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2} = \\sqrt{\\,\\,\\, \\vphantom{\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n} \\overline{x^2} - \\left(\\overline{x} \\right)^2}\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's again consider the data set below:\n\n$$\n1, \\: 7, \\: 3, \\: 9\n$$\n\nPreviously, we found that the variance of this data set was\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 = 10\n$$\n\nSo, the standard deviation is given by\n\n$$\n\\sigma = \\sqrt{10}\n$$\n\nAs we will see in the future, the standard deviation is often used in calculations where we want to quantify how \"surprising\" a particular observation is.\n\nIn those contexts, we often compute how many standard deviations the observed value was away from the mean: the further away, the more surprising the observation.\n\nFor now, though, let's get some practice computing the standard deviation." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186116536 1064 "There is an alternative formula that we can use to compute the variance of a data set $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$. This formula gives the same result as the usual formula, and it is often faster to use.\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 = \\overline{x^2} - (\\overline{x})^2\n$$\n\nHere, $\\overline{x^2}$ refers to the mean of the squares of the points in the data set, that is,\n\n$$\n\\overline{x^2} = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n x_i^2\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's again compute the variance of the data set below:\n\n$$\n1, \\: 7, \\: 3, \\: 9\n$$\n\nEarlier, we found that\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} = 5\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overline{x^2}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2}^(―) &= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot (1^{2} + 7^{2} + 3^{2} + 9^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot (1 + 49 + 9 + 81) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot 140 \\\\\n&= 35\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the variance as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nσ^{2} &= x^{2}^(―) - (x^(―))^{2} \\\\\n&= 35 - 5^{2} \\\\\n&= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIndeed, we get the same answer as before!" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186116536 1064 "There is an alternative formula that we can use to compute the variance of a data set $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n.$ This formula gives the same result as the usual formula, and it is often faster to use.\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 = \\overline{x^2} - (\\overline{x})^2\n$$\n\nHere, $\\overline{x^2}$ refers to the mean of the squares of the points in the data set, that is,\n\n$$\n\\overline{x^2} = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n x_i^2.\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's again compute the variance of the data set below:\n\n$$\n1, \\: 7, \\: 3, \\: 9\n$$\n\nEarlier, we found that $\\overline{x} = 5.$ Now, let's compute $\\overline{x^2}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot ({1}^{2}+{7}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{9}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (1+49+9+81) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 140 \\\\  & =35.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the variance as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =35-{5}^{2} \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIndeed, we get the same answer as before!" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186116537 1064 "Recall the definition of variance:\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 =  \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2\n$$\n\nYou may wonder why the variance uses the *squared* difference, $(x_i - \\overline{x})^2,$ instead of just using the non-squared difference $x_i - \\overline{x}.$\n\nIt turns out that if we add up all the non-squared differences $x_i - \\overline{x},$ then the negatives will cancel out the positives, and the result will always come out to zero no matter how spread out or concentrated the distribution is.\n\nFor example, consider the dataset\n\n$$\n-2, \\: -2, \\: 0, \\: 2, \\: 2.\n$$\n\nWe have $\\overline{x}=0,$ and if we compute $\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x}),$ we get a result of $0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}) & =\\frac{1}{5}[(-2-0)+(-2-0)+(0-0)+(2-0)+(2-0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[-2-2+0+2+2] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis will happen every time, because the quantity $\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})$ always simplifies to $0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}) & ={{\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{―}{x}}-\\frac{1}{n}{{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\overset{―}{x}}_{⏟}}_{n\\overset{―}{x}} \\\\  & =\\overset{―}{x}-\\frac{1}{n}\\cdot n\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\overset{―}{x}-\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need to perform some operation to the distances $x_i - \\overline{x}$ to make them non-negative. Squaring the distances, $(x_i - \\overline{x})^2,$ will accomplish this.\n\nYou might also wonder why we square the differences instead of taking the absolute value. The reason is that the sum of absolute values of the distances isn't guaranteed to increase if the data points are more spread out.\n\nFor example, consider the data set\n\n\\[\n-3, \\: -1, \\: 0, \\: 1, \\: 3,\n\\]\n\nwhich is more spread out than the earlier data set.\n\nEven though this data set is more spread out, the sum of absolute values of the distances is the same, which is not what we want. We want the variance to increase whenever the data set becomes more spread out.\n\n\\[\\begin{align*}\n-2, \\: -2, \\: 0, \\: 2, \\: 2 &\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{1}{5}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^5 \\left| x_i - \\overline{x} \\right| = \\dfrac{1}{5} \\left( 2+2+0+2+2 \\right) = \\dfrac{8}{5} \\quad \\color{red}\\times \\\\[5pt]\n-3, \\: -1, \\: 0, \\: 1, \\: 3 &\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{1}{5}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^5 \\left| x_i - \\overline{x} \\right| = \\dfrac{1}{5} \\left( 3+1+0+1+3 \\right) = \\dfrac{8}{5} \\quad \\color{red}\\times \\\\[5pt]\n\\end{align*}\\]\n\nIf we square the distances, though, we'll get a quantity that increases whenever the data set becomes more spread out.\n\n\\[\\begin{align*}\n-2, \\: -2, \\: 0, \\: 2, \\: 2 &\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{1}{5}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^5 \\left( x_i - \\overline{x} \\right)^2 = \\dfrac{1}{5} \\left( 2^2+2^2+0^2+2^2+2^2 \\right) = \\dfrac{16}{5} \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark \\\\[5pt]\n-3, \\: -1, \\: 0, \\: 1, \\: 3 &\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{1}{5}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^5 \\left( x_i - \\overline{x} \\right)^2 = \\dfrac{1}{5} \\left( 3^2+1^2+0^2+1^2+3^2 \\right) = \\dfrac{20}{5} \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark \\\\[5pt]\n\\end{align*}\\]" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186116537 1064 "Recall the definition of variance:\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2\n$$\n\nYou may wonder why the variance uses the *squared* difference, $(x_i - \\overline{x})^2$, instead of just using the non-squared difference $x_i - \\overline{x}$.\n\nIt turns out that if we add up all the non-squared differences $x_i - \\overline{x}$, then the negatives will cancel out the positives, and the result will always come out to zero no matter how spread out or concentrated the distribution is.\n\nFor example, consider the dataset\n\n$$\n-2, \\: -2, \\: 0, \\: 2, \\: 2\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\overline{x}=0\n$$\n\nand if we compute\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})\n$$\n\nwe get a result of $0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―)) &= \\frac{1}{5}[(-2 - 0) + (-2 - 0) + (0 - 0) + (2 - 0) + (2 - 0)] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}[- 2 - 2 + 0 + 2 + 2] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5} \\cdot 0 \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis will happen every time, because the quantity\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})\n$$\n\nalways simplifies to $0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―)) &= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}_(⏟)_(x^(―)) - \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)x^(―)_(⏟)_(nx^(―)) \\\\\n&= x^(―) - \\frac{1}{n} \\cdot nx^(―) \\\\\n&= x^(―) - x^(―) \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need to perform some operation to the distances $x_i - \\overline{x}$ to make them non-negative. Squaring the distances, $(x_i - \\overline{x})^2$, will accomplish this." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186116553 1064 "The variance $\\sigma^2$ of a data set is given by\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 =  \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2.\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate how to use this formula, let's compute the variance of the data set below:\n\n$$\n1, \\: 7, \\: 3, \\: 9\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute the mean:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (1+7+3+9) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 20 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(1-5)^{2}+(7-5)^{2}+(3-5)^{2}+(9-5)^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[16+4+4+16] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[40] \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the variance is $\\sigma^2 = 10.$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186116553 1064 "The variance $\\sigma^2$ of a data set is given by\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate how to use this formula, let's compute the variance of the data set below:\n\n$$\n1, \\: 7, \\: 3, \\: 9\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute the mean:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^(―) &= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot (1 + 7 + 3 + 9) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot 20 \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nσ^{2} &= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}[(1 - 5)^{2} + (7 - 5)^{2} + (3 - 5)^{2} + (9 - 5)^{2}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}[16 + 4 + 4 + 16] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}[40] \\\\\n&= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 = 10\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186125758 1064 "A function is **invertible** on an interval if and only if it's one-to-one. In other words, it must pass the horizontal line test.\n\nNot all functions have inverses! For example, consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=x^2-1\n$$\n\nwhose graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/e9/e99832fa1867a8314dda20f392d0c897ebb2942ac91a652ab1cf474b5d2d9612.png)\n\nNotice that $f(x)$ is not a one-to-one since it does not satisfy the horizontal line test.\n\nTo find the graph of a function's inverse, we usually reflect it over the line $y=x$. However, if we reflect $f(x)$ over the line $y=x$, the result is not a function since it will not satisfy the vertical line test.\n\n![](images/7a/7a5a95f1cc3e2c8b40e865a24d22f4529e47c3c0137a6cb8d0369059f4a89a56.png)\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is *not* invertible.\n\nIf a function is not invertible, we can sometimes make it invertible by restricting its domain.\n\nHere, if we take\n\n$$\nf(x)=x^2-1\n$$\n\nover the restricted interval $x \\in (0,\\infty)$, then it passes the horizontal line test and we can find the corresponding inverse function by reflecting the graph of $y=f(x)$ over the line $y=x$.\n\n![](images/0a/0a7f98c2dce4599ee74cc13dc53c3a693b9c3716ffe54c708811f4c011f18b6d.png)\n\nTo determine if a function is invertible over a particular domain, we need to use the horizontal line test to check if it's a one-to-one function." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186125758 1064 "A function is **invertible** on an interval if and only if it's one-to-one. In other words, it must pass the horizontal line test.\n\nNot all functions have inverses! For example, consider the function $f(x)=x^2-1$ whose graph is shown below. \n\n![](images/e9/e99832fa1867a8314dda20f392d0c897ebb2942ac91a652ab1cf474b5d2d9612.png)\n\nNotice that $f(x)$ is not a one-to-one since it does not satisfy the horizontal line test.\n\nTo find the graph of a function's inverse, we usually reflect it over the line $y=x.$ However, if we reflect $f(x)$ over the line $y=x,$ the result is not a function since it will not satisfy the vertical line test.\n\n![](images/7a/7a5a95f1cc3e2c8b40e865a24d22f4529e47c3c0137a6cb8d0369059f4a89a56.png)\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is *not* invertible.\n\nIf a function is not invertible, we can sometimes make it invertible by restricting its domain.\n\nHere, if we take $f(x)=x^2-1$ over the restricted interval $x \\in (0,\\infty),$ then it passes the horizontal line test and we can find the corresponding inverse function by reflecting the graph of $y=f(x)$ over the line $y=x.$\n\n![](images/0a/0a7f98c2dce4599ee74cc13dc53c3a693b9c3716ffe54c708811f4c011f18b6d.png)\n\nTo determine if a function is invertible over a particular domain, we need to use the horizontal line test to check if it's a one-to-one function." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186130338 1064 "For any point on the unit circle, we can draw a right triangle whose hypotenuse is the line segment between the origin and the point.\n\n![](images/b1/b1ec08957fd0d09179853d6d56c20b7058c4531bdd7e766c5b3462ba246fce2b.png)\n\nEach hypotenuse is the radius of the unit circle and consequently has a length equal to $1$.\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of *any* point on the unit circle must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2=1\n$$\n\nWe can use this fact to find one coordinate of a point on the unit circle given the other.\n\nFor example, suppose that the $x$-coordinate of the point $A$ in the fourth quadrant on the unit circle is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{7}\n$$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{3}{7}\n$$\n\ninto the above and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} x^2+y^2&=1 \\\\[5pt] \\left(\\dfrac{3}{7}\\right)^2+y^2&=1 \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac{9}{49}+y^2&=1 \\\\[5pt] y^2&= 1-\\dfrac{9}{49} \\\\[5pt] y^2&= \\dfrac{40}{49} \\\\[5pt] y&= \\sqrt{\\dfrac{40}{49}} \\\\[5pt] y &= \\pm \\dfrac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ lies in the *fourth* quadrant, $y$ must be *negative*. So we select the negative answer. Therefore,\n\n$$\ny=-\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186130338 1064 "For any point on the unit circle, we can draw a right triangle whose hypotenuse is the line segment between the origin and the point.\n\n![](images/b1/b1ec08957fd0d09179853d6d56c20b7058c4531bdd7e766c5b3462ba246fce2b.png)\n\nEach hypotenuse is the radius of the unit circle and consequently has a length equal to $1.$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of *any* point on the unit circle must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2=1.\n$$\n\nWe can use this fact to find one coordinate of a point on the unit circle given the other.\n\nFor example, suppose that the $x$-coordinate of the point $A$ in the fourth quadrant on the unit circle is $\\dfrac{3}{7}.$ To find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute $x=\\dfrac{3}{7}$ into the above and solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tx^2+y^2&=1\\[5pt]\n\t          \\left(\\dfrac{3}{7}\\right)^2+y^2&=1\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac{9}{49}+y^2&=1\\[5pt]\n\t\ty^2&= 1-\\dfrac{9}{49}\\[5pt]\n\t\ty^2&= \\dfrac{40}{49}\\[5pt]\n\t\ty&= \\sqrt{\\dfrac{40}{49}}\\[5pt]\n\t        y &= \\pm \\dfrac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}\n\n}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ lies in the *fourth* quadrant, $y$ must be *negative*. So we select the negative answer. Therefore, $y=-\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}.$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186130339 1064 "By the Pythagorean theorem, we know that the equation\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2 = 1\n$$\n\nholds for any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle.\n\nNow, we also know that for any point on the unit circle, the coordinates $(x,y)$ are related to the central angle $\\theta$ via the formulas\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the Pythagorean Identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + y^{2} &= 1 \\\\\n(\\cos θ)^{2} + (\\sin θ)^{2} &= 1 \\\\\n\\cos^{2} θ + \\sin^{2} θ &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equation is called the **Pythagorean trigonometric identity**, and it holds for *every* angle $\\theta$. This includes negative angles and angles that are larger than one full rotation." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186130339 1064 "By the Pythagorean theorem, we know that the equation \n\n$$\nx^2+y^2 = 1\n$$\n\nholds for any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle. \n\nNow, we also know that for any point on the unit circle, the coordinates $(x,y)$ are related to the central angle $\\theta$ via the formulas\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the Pythagorean Identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2} & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}\\theta +{sin}^{2}\\theta  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equation is called the **Pythagorean trigonometric identity**, and it holds for *every* angle $\\theta.$ This includes negative angles and angles that are larger than one full rotation." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186130343 1064 "Given the equation of a general transformed tangent function\n\n$$\ny={\\color{red}A}\\tan\\left( {\\color{blue}B} x + C\\right) +{\\color{green}D},\n$$\n\nsome properties of the graph of the function are as follows:\n\n- The period is $\\dfrac{\\pi}{\\color{blue}B}.$\n\n- The vertical shift is ${\\color{green}D}.$\n\nWe obtain the horizontal shift by factoring the argument of the function, as follows:\n\n$$\ny={\\color{red}A}\\tan\\left( {\\color{blue}B}\\left[ x + \\dfrac{C}{{\\color{blue}B}} \\right]\\right) +{\\color{green}D}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we can see that the phase (horizontal shift) is $-\\dfrac{C}{{\\color{blue}B}}.$\n\nWe can apply the same ideas to work out the properties of a transformed cotangent function of the form\n\n$$\ny={\\color{red}A}\\cot\\left( {\\color{blue}B} x + C\\right) +{\\color{green}D}.\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186130343 1064 "Given the equation of a general transformed tangent function\n\n$$\ny={\\color{red}A}\\tan\\left({\\color{blue}B} x + C\\right) +{\\color{green}D}\n$$\n\nsome properties of the graph of the function are as follows:\n\n- The period is $\\dfrac{\\pi}{\\color{blue}B}$.\n- The vertical shift is ${\\color{green}D}$.\n\nWe obtain the horizontal shift by factoring the argument of the function, as follows:\n\n$$\ny={\\color{red}A}\\tan\\left({\\color{blue}B}\\left[ x + \\dfrac{C}{{\\color{blue}B}} \\right]\\right) +{\\color{green}D}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we can see that the phase (horizontal shift) is\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{C}{{\\color{blue}B}}\n$$\n\nWe can apply the same ideas to work out the properties of a transformed cotangent function of the form\n\n$$\ny={\\color{red}A}\\cot\\left({\\color{blue}B} x + C\\right) +{\\color{green}D}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186130368 1064 "The **end behavior of a function** refers to what the function does when the input value $x$ is very large (either positive or negative).\n\nFor example, consider the polynomial\n\n$$\np(x)=x^2+2x+1\n$$\n\nWe can understand its behavior for very large $x$ by looking at its graph.\n\n![](images/61/61c2c4cd7efd64af7474bb8854b6d18878f5f5d932103f477d3048f806f83890.png)\n\nLet's inspect the graph as $x$ becomes very large.\n\n- As $x$ becomes very large in the *positive* direction, the polynomial $p(x)$ grows very large in the positive direction as well. So, we say that \"the polynomial $p(x)$ approaches positive infinity as $x$ approaches positive infinity,\" and we write\n$p(x) \\rightarrow +\\infty \\quad \\text{as} \\quad x \\rightarrow + \\infty$.\n- As $x$ becomes very large in the *negative* direction, the polynomial $p(x)$ again grows very large in the positive direction. So, we say that \"the polynomial $p(x)$ approaches positive infinity as $x$ approaches negative infinity,\" and we write\n$p(x) \\rightarrow +\\infty \\quad \\text{as} \\quad x \\rightarrow - \\infty$.\n\nWe can confirm this by evaluating the function at large values of $x$.\n\n- For large *positive* inputs, the values of $p(x)$ get larger.\n$p(100) = 100^{2} + 2(100) + 1 = 10000 + 200 + 1 = 10201; p(1000) = 1000^{2} + 2(1000) + 1 = 1000000 + 2000 + 1 = 1002001$\n- For large *negative* inputs, the values of $p(x)$ again get larger.\n$p(-100) = (-100)^{2} + 2(-100) + 1 = 10000 - 200 + 1 = 9801; p(-1000) = (-1000)^{2} + 2(-1000) + 1 = 1000000 - 2000 + 1 = 998001$\n\nAs you can see from the above computations, it is the leading term that \"decides\" the behavior of the function at infinity.\n\nIn general, to determine the end behavior of a polynomial, all we have to do is look at the leading term. Let's see how to do this.\n\n**Note:** The \"$+$\" sign is often dropped from the notation for positive infinity. Whenever you see an infinity symbol without a sign in front of it $(\\infty)$, it means positive infinity $(+\\infty)$." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186130368 1064 "The **end behavior of a function** refers to what the function does when the input value $x$ is very large (either positive or negative).\n\nFor example, consider the polynomial $p(x)=x^2+2x+1.$ We can understand its behavior for very large $x$ by looking at its graph. \n\n![](images/61/61c2c4cd7efd64af7474bb8854b6d18878f5f5d932103f477d3048f806f83890.png)\n\nLet's inspect the graph as $x$ becomes very large.\n\n- As $x$ becomes very large in the *positive* direction, the polynomial $p(x)$ grows very large in the positive direction as well. So, we say that \"the polynomial $p(x)$ approaches positive infinity as $x$ approaches positive infinity,\" and we write\n\n$$\np(x) \\rightarrow +\\infty \\quad \\text{as} \\quad x \\rightarrow + \\infty.\n$$\n\n- As $x$ becomes very large in the *negative* direction, the polynomial $p(x)$ again grows very large in the positive direction. So, we say that \"the polynomial $p(x)$ approaches positive infinity as $x$ approaches negative infinity,\" and we write\n\n$$\np(x) \\rightarrow +\\infty \\quad \\text{as} \\quad x \\rightarrow - \\infty.\n$$\n\nWe can confirm this by evaluating the function at large values of $x.$\n\n- For large *positive* inputs, the values of $p(x)$ get larger.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & p(100)={100}^{2}+2(100)+1=10\\,000+200+1=10\\,201 \\\\  & p(1\\,000)=1\\,{000}^{2}+2(1\\,000)+1=1\\,000\\,000+2\\,000+1=1\\,002\\,001\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For large *negative* inputs, the values of $p(x)$ again get larger.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & p(-100)=(-100)^{2}+2(-100)+1=10\\,000-200+1=9\\,801 \\\\  & p(-1\\,000)=(-1\\,000)^{2}+2(-1\\,000)+1=1\\,000\\,000-2\\,000+1=998\\,001\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs you can see from the above computations, it is the leading term that \"decides\" the behavior of the function at infinity. \n\nIn general, to determine the end behavior of a polynomial, all we have to do is look at the leading term. Let's see how to do this.\n\n**Note:** The \"$+$\" sign is often dropped from the notation for positive infinity. Whenever you see an infinity symbol without a sign in front of it $(\\infty),$ it means positive infinity $(+\\infty).$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186130369 1064 "It's possible to determine information regarding the multiplicities of the roots of a polynomial from its graph. We summarize all possible cases in the table below:\n\n| Multiplicity | Case 1 | Case 2 |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ \n\n (Simple root) \n\n Odd | ![](images/5a/5a0221f93c65a1eeb4503604a558a6da81c4c7af81c2c52cc35ecf3d189fe99f.png) | ![](images/d5/d556b4edf39181eebb7ec87c344d6f657a6875ecd2745405eaf5351ccaff6f96.png) |\n| $>1$ \n\n (Multiple root) \n\n Even | ![](images/9d/9d14c2588da2ee9c9f5bbc32a70791146bed609ea0d6d5912aeaa2edab02e2d7.png) | ![](images/28/28ad9248a9a509e3a44937d0438fb2366f550a22360d01b9b6a29bcac41357e8.png) |\n| $>1$ \n\n (Multiple root) \n\n Odd | ![](images/3a/3ae81378cb3c918dd5b7730519eb5a91e949f88eef9f03f4910f4ab8fb3cd5a9.png) | ![](images/95/9506a7ce4031f1f6244c0110c3235f66ea5652bb53548a032732869cf21f5997.png) |\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The graph is **tangent** to the $x$-axis at multiple roots. This means that if $x=a$ is a multiple root, then the graph of the polynomial is parallel to the $x$-axis at this point.\n\n- If the multiplicity of a root $x=a$ is *even*, the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x=a.$\n\n- If the multiplicity of a root $x=a$ is *odd* (including a simple root of multiplicity $1$), the graph crosses the $x$-axis at $x=a.$\n\n- The graph of the polynomial is *never* tangent to the $x$-axis at simple roots." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186130369 1064 "It's possible to determine information regarding the multiplicities of the roots of a polynomial from its graph. We summarize all possible cases in the table below:\n\n| Multiplicity | Case 1 | Case 2 |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$<br><br>(Simple root) | ![](images/5a/5a0221f93c65a1eeb4503604a558a6da81c4c7af81c2c52cc35ecf3d189fe99f.png) | ![](images/d5/d556b4edf39181eebb7ec87c344d6f657a6875ecd2745405eaf5351ccaff6f96.png) |\n| $>1$<br><br>(Multiple root)<br><br>Even | ![](images/9d/9d14c2588da2ee9c9f5bbc32a70791146bed609ea0d6d5912aeaa2edab02e2d7.png) | ![](images/28/28ad9248a9a509e3a44937d0438fb2366f550a22360d01b9b6a29bcac41357e8.png) |\n| $>1$<br><br>(Multiple root)<br><br>Odd | ![](images/3a/3ae81378cb3c918dd5b7730519eb5a91e949f88eef9f03f4910f4ab8fb3cd5a9.png) | ![](images/95/9506a7ce4031f1f6244c0110c3235f66ea5652bb53548a032732869cf21f5997.png) |\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The graph is **tangent** to the $x$-axis at multiple roots. This means that if $x=a$ is a multiple root, then the graph of the polynomial is parallel to the $x$-axis at this point.\n- If the multiplicity of a root $x=a$ is *even*, the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x=a$.\n- If the multiplicity of a root $x=a$ is *odd* (including a simple root of multiplicity $1$), the graph crosses the $x$-axis at $x=a$.\n- The graph of the polynomial is *never* tangent to the $x$-axis at simple roots." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186130371 1064 "If you can't remember all the different cases, there's a trick you can use to determine the sign of infinity:\n\n- Substitute $+1$ and $-1$ into the leading term of your polynomial and check the sign of the result.\n\n- The sign of the result corresponds to the sign of infinity approached by the function for $x\\rightarrow +\\infty$ and $x\\rightarrow -\\infty,$ respectively.\n\nTo illustrate, consider the following polynomial:\n\n$$\nP(x) = 2x^3 -6 x^2 + 8\n$$\n\nHere, the leading term is $2x^3.$ Applying the trick, we have that\n\n- $2(+1)^3 = {\\color{blue}\\mathbf+2},$ which indicates that $P(x) \\rightarrow  {\\color{blue}\\mathbf +\\infty}$ as $x \\rightarrow +\\infty.$\n\n- $2(-1)^3 ={\\color{red}\\mathbf-2},$ which indicates that  $P(x) \\rightarrow {\\color{red}\\mathbf-\\infty}$ as $x \\rightarrow -\\infty.$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186130371 1064 "If you can't remember all the different cases, there's a trick you can use to determine the sign of infinity:\n\n- Substitute $+1$ and $-1$ into the leading term of your polynomial and check the sign of the result.\n- The sign of the result corresponds to the sign of infinity approached by the function for $x\\rightarrow +\\infty$ and $x\\rightarrow -\\infty$, respectively.\n\nTo illustrate, consider the following polynomial:\n\n$$\nP(x) = 2x^3 -6 x^2 + 8\n$$\n\nHere, the leading term is $2x^3$. Applying the trick, we have that\n\n- $2(+1)^3 = {\\color{blue}\\mathbf+2}$, which indicates that $P(x) \\rightarrow {\\color{blue}\\mathbf +\\infty}$ as $x \\rightarrow +\\infty$.\n- $2(-1)^3 ={\\color{red}\\mathbf-2}$, which indicates that $P(x) \\rightarrow {\\color{red}\\mathbf-\\infty}$ as $x \\rightarrow -\\infty$." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186130372 1064 "When we transform the reciprocal function\n\n$$\ny= \\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nits domain might be modified as well.\n\nFor example, the diagram below shows the graph of the transformed reciprocal function\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x+2}\n$$\n\n![](images/f7/f729214a66435995d8a20c48b237105f50e0521d2aac93f2d4ba958c55d3ca5a.png)\n\nA reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. To find the domain, we must exclude all values of $x$ that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator of the reciprocal function above equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 2 &= 0 \\\\\nx &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\in(-\\infty, -2) \\cup (-2, +\\infty)$." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186130372 1064 "When we transform the reciprocal function $y= \\dfrac{1}{x},$ its domain might be modified as well.\n\nFor example, the diagram below shows the graph of the transformed reciprocal function $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x+2}.$\n\n![](images/f7/f729214a66435995d8a20c48b237105f50e0521d2aac93f2d4ba958c55d3ca5a.png)\n\nA reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. To find the domain, we must exclude all values of $x$ that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator of the reciprocal function above equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & =0 \\\\ x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\in(-\\infty, -2) \\cup (-2, +\\infty).$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186130373 1064 "When we transform the reciprocal function\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac1x\n$$\n\nwe might also modify its range.\n\nFor example, consider the graph of the transformed reciprocal function\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac1x -2\n$$\n\nshown below.\n\n![](images/74/745bdf3daa3acfcb8a3505e1931cc31ab86c2e4e9e43d2e6266d3156f9cab4fb.png)\n\nNotice that the function has the horizontal asymptote $y=-2$.\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ can attain any $y$-value with the exception of the $y$-value of the horizontal asymptote. Therefore, to compute its range, we take the interval $(-\\infty,\\infty)$ and exclude the $y$-value of the asymptote.\n\nTherefore, the range of $y = f(x)$ is\n$f(x)\\in (-\\infty, -2) \\cup (-2,\\infty)$.\n\nIn general, a transformed reciprocal function of the form\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{a}{bx+c} + d\n$$\n\nhas the horizontal asymptote $y=d$, and therefore its range is\n\n$$\nf(x) \\in (-\\infty, d)\\cup (d,\\infty)\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186130373 1064 "When we transform the reciprocal function $y=\\dfrac1x,$ we might also modify its range. \n\nFor example, consider the graph of the transformed reciprocal function $f(x) = \\dfrac1x -2,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/74/745bdf3daa3acfcb8a3505e1931cc31ab86c2e4e9e43d2e6266d3156f9cab4fb.png)\n\nNotice that the function has the horizontal asymptote $y=-2.$\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ can attain any $y$-value with the exception of the $y$-value of the horizontal asymptote. Therefore, to compute its range, we take the interval $(-\\infty,\\infty)$ and exclude the $y$-value of the asymptote.\n\nTherefore, the range of $y = f(x)$ is \n\n$$\nf(x)\\in (-\\infty, -2) \\cup (-2,\\infty).\n$$\n\nIn general, a transformed reciprocal function of the form\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{a}{bx+c} + d\n$$\n\nhas the horizontal asymptote $y=d,$ and therefore its range is\n\n$$\nf(x) \\in (-\\infty, d)\\cup (d,\\infty).\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186130401 1064 "In general, if we have a polynomial\n\n$$\nf(x) = a_nx^n+a_{n-1}x^{n-1}+...+a_{1}x + a_0\n$$\n\nwith leading coefficient\n\n$$\na_n\\neq 0\n$$\n\nthe end behavior of the polynomial is always\n\n$$\nf(x) \\rightarrow +\\infty \\quad \\text{or} \\quad f(x) \\rightarrow -\\infty\n$$\n\nas $x \\rightarrow \\pm \\infty$. The sign of infinity depends on whether the leading exponent is even or odd, and on the sign of the leading coefficient.\n\n- If the leading coefficient is *positive*, then $f(x) \\to +\\infty$ as $x \\to +\\infty$. Otherwise, if the leading coefficient is *negative*, then $f(x) \\to -\\infty$ as $x \\to +\\infty$.\n- If the leading exponent is *even*, then the end behavior as $x \\to +\\infty$ is the *same* as the end behavior as $x \\to -\\infty$. Otherwise, if the leading exponent is *odd*, then the end behavior as $x \\to +\\infty$ is *opposite* the end behavior as $x \\to -\\infty$.\n\nThe different cases are summarized in the table below.\n\n| $n$ | $a_n$ | $x \\rightarrow -\\infty$ | $x \\rightarrow +\\infty$ | Sketch |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Even | $>0$ | $f(x) \\rightarrow +\\infty$ | $f(x) \\rightarrow +\\infty$ | ![](images/f4/f45aadee45a2a07e4cd050b4c9942118c250e73c011b7d00fff09d70a76c3319.png) |\n| $<0$ | $f(x) \\rightarrow -\\infty$ | $f(x) \\rightarrow -\\infty$ | ![](images/b6/b61d6aa8182bda07e62ff6b8cc433f10a41aaaad0bde2bf4c3e8783415327b22.png) |  |\n| Odd | $>0$ | $f(x) \\rightarrow -\\infty$ | $f(x) \\rightarrow +\\infty$ | ![](images/06/064b94a21052ed2e20c20a69fe7649d8e0015c20c5840ad76440dbf2b7867bf5.png) |\n| $<0$ | $f(x) \\rightarrow +\\infty$ | $f(x) \\rightarrow -\\infty$ | ![](images/c4/c4863580d9d7aa01a94cabb918d70203e57993bb7872a7abb186ac06760945ec.png) |  |" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186130401 1064 "In general, if we have a polynomial\n\n$$\nf(x) = a_nx^n+a_{n-1}x^{n-1}+...+a_{1}x + a_0\n$$\n\nwith leading coefficient $a_n\\neq 0,$ the end behavior of the polynomial is always\n\n$$\nf(x) \\rightarrow +\\infty \\quad \\text{or} \\quad f(x) \\rightarrow -\\infty\n$$\n\nas $x \\rightarrow \\pm \\infty.$ The sign of infinity depends on whether the leading exponent is even or odd, and on the sign of the leading coefficient. \n\n- If the leading coefficient is *positive*, then $f(x) \\to +\\infty$ as $x \\to +\\infty.$ Otherwise, if the leading coefficient is *negative*, then $f(x) \\to -\\infty$ as $x \\to +\\infty.$\n\n- If the leading exponent is *even*, then the end behavior as $x \\to +\\infty$ is the *same* as the end behavior as $x \\to -\\infty.$ Otherwise, if the leading exponent is *odd*, then the end behavior as $x \\to +\\infty$ is *opposite* the end behavior as $x \\to -\\infty.$\n\nThe different cases are summarized in the table below.\n\n| $n$ | $a_n$ | $x  \\rightarrow -\\infty$ | $x  \\rightarrow +\\infty$ | Sketch |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Even | $>0$ | $f(x)  \\rightarrow +\\infty$ | $f(x)  \\rightarrow +\\infty$ | ![](images/f4/f45aadee45a2a07e4cd050b4c9942118c250e73c011b7d00fff09d70a76c3319.png) |\n| $<0$ | $f(x)  \\rightarrow -\\infty$ | $f(x)  \\rightarrow -\\infty$ | ![](images/b6/b61d6aa8182bda07e62ff6b8cc433f10a41aaaad0bde2bf4c3e8783415327b22.png) |  |\n| Odd | $>0$ | $f(x)  \\rightarrow -\\infty$ | $f(x)  \\rightarrow +\\infty$ | ![](images/06/064b94a21052ed2e20c20a69fe7649d8e0015c20c5840ad76440dbf2b7867bf5.png) |\n| $<0$ | $f(x)  \\rightarrow +\\infty$ | $f(x)  \\rightarrow -\\infty$ | ![](images/c4/c4863580d9d7aa01a94cabb918d70203e57993bb7872a7abb186ac06760945ec.png) |  |" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186130490 1064 "Suppose we have a transformed *sine* function whose graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/e0/e0ccd47111e0b05a9e8eeba3e8098e6ff0775c183d4c4f95e1bd2fae2a60d949.png)\n\nA transformed sine function in its most general form can be expressed as\n\n$$\ny={\\color{red}A}\\sin\\left( {\\color{blue}B} x + C\\right) +{\\color{green}D},\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\color{red}A}, {\\color{blue}B}, C,$ and ${\\color{green}D}$ are all constants.\n\nWe can determine its properties from this general form as follows:\n\n- The amplitude is $\\vert {\\color{red}A}\\vert.$\n\n- The period is $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\color{blue}B}.$\n\n- The vertical shift is ${\\color{green}{D}}.$\n\nThe phase shift (horizontal shift) is slightly trickier to work out. First, we must factor the constant $\\color{blue}B$ in the argument of the function as follows:\n\n$$\ny={\\color{red}A}\\sin\\left( {\\color{blue}B}\\left[ x + \\dfrac {C}{ \\color{blue}B}\\right]\\right) +{\\color{green}D}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we can see that the phase shift (or **phase**) is $-\\dfrac{C}{\\color{blue}B}.$\n\nThe **midline** is the horizontal line that lies halfway between the horizontal lines $y = y_{\\textrm{max}}$ and $y = y_{\\textrm{min}},$ where $y_{\\textrm{max}}$ and $y_{\\textrm{min}}$ are the maximum and minimum values of our trigonometric function, respectively. Its equation is given by\n\n$$\ny = {\\color{green}{D}}.\n$$\n\nWe can also think of the midline as representing the average of the maximum and minimum values. Thus, an alternative form for the midline equation is\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{ y_{\\textrm{max}} +  y_{\\textrm{min}}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can apply the same methods to determine the properties of a transformed cosine function of the form \n\n$$\ny={\\color{red}A}\\cos\\left( {\\color{blue}B} x + C\\right) +{\\color{green}D}.\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186130490 1064 "Suppose we have a transformed *sine* function whose graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/e0/e0ccd47111e0b05a9e8eeba3e8098e6ff0775c183d4c4f95e1bd2fae2a60d949.png)\n\nA transformed sine function in its most general form can be expressed as\n\n$$\ny={\\color{red}A}\\sin\\left({\\color{blue}B} x + C\\right) +{\\color{green}D}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\color{red}A}, {\\color{blue}B}, C$, and ${\\color{green}D}$ are all constants.\n\nWe can determine its properties from this general form as follows:\n\n- The amplitude is $\\vert {\\color{red}A}\\vert$.\n- The period is $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\color{blue}B}$.\n- The vertical shift is ${\\color{green}{D}}$.\n\nThe phase shift (horizontal shift) is slightly trickier to work out. First, we must factor the constant $\\color{blue}B$ in the argument of the function as follows:\n\n$$\ny={\\color{red}A}\\sin\\left({\\color{blue}B}\\left[ x + \\dfrac {C}{\\color{blue}B}\\right]\\right) +{\\color{green}D}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we can see that the phase shift (or **phase**) is\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{C}{\\color{blue}B}\n$$\n\nThe **midline** is the horizontal line that lies halfway between the horizontal lines\n\n$$\ny = y_{\\textrm{max}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny = y_{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nwhere $y_{\\textrm{max}}$ and $y_{\\textrm{min}}$ are the maximum and minimum values of our trigonometric function, respectively. Its equation is given by\n\n$$\ny = {\\color{green}{D}}\n$$\n\nWe can also think of the midline as representing the average of the maximum and minimum values. Thus, an alternative form for the midline equation is\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{y_{\\textrm{max}} + y_{\\textrm{min}}}{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can apply the same methods to determine the properties of a transformed cosine function of the form\n\n$$\ny={\\color{red}A}\\cos\\left({\\color{blue}B} x + C\\right) +{\\color{green}D}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186130491 1064 "We can determine the range of a transformed sine or cosine function by observing the effect of each transformation on the maximum and minimum values.\n\nFor example, let's determine the range of the following function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\color{red}2}\\sin\\left( {\\color{blue}3}x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)+{\\color{green}1}\n$$\n\nWe start by writing down the range of $\\sin x{:}$\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\sin x \\leq 1\n$$\n\nThe idea is to transform this inequality using addition, multiplication, and other operations until we arrive at the following:\n\n$$\ny_{\\textrm{min}} \\leq f(x) \\leq y_{\\textrm{max}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we note that *horizontal* shifts and *horizontal* stretches do not affect the range. Therefore, we can immediately state that\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\sin \\left( {\\color{blue}3}x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\leq 1.\n$$\n\nNotice that the middle part is starting to look like $f(x).$ However, we must also consider the effects of the amplitude $({\\color{red}2})$ and vertical shift $({\\color{green}1}).$\n\nTo take care of the amplitude effects, we multiply our inequality by ${\\color{red}2}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}(3x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\le 1 \\\\ 2\\cdot (-1)\\le 2\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}(3x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\le 2\\cdot 1 \\\\ -2\\le 2\\operatorname{sin}(3x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\le 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to deal with the vertical shift, we increase all the quantities in our inequality by $+{\\color{green}1}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\le 2\\operatorname{sin}(3x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\le 2 \\\\ -2+1\\le 2\\operatorname{sin}(3x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+1\\le 2+1 \\\\ -1\\le 2\\operatorname{sin}(3x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+1\\le 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the middle part of the inequality is now identical to $f(x),$ we can write\n\n$$\n-1  \\leq f(x)  \\leq 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range is $f(x)\\in [-1,3].$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186130491 1064 "We can determine the range of a transformed sine or cosine function by observing the effect of each transformation on the maximum and minimum values.\n\nFor example, let's determine the range of the following function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\color{red}2}\\sin\\left({\\color{blue}3}x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)+{\\color{green}1}\n$$\n\nWe start by writing down the range of $\\sin x{:}$\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\sin x \\leq 1\n$$\n\nThe idea is to transform this inequality using addition, multiplication, and other operations until we arrive at the following:\n\n$$\ny_{\\textrm{min}} \\leq f(x) \\leq y_{\\textrm{max}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we note that *horizontal* shifts and *horizontal* stretches do not affect the range. Therefore, we can immediately state that\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\sin \\left({\\color{blue}3}x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\leq 1\n$$\n\nNotice that the middle part is starting to look like $f(x)$. However, we must also consider the effects of the amplitude $({\\color{red}2})$ and vertical shift $({\\color{green}1})$.\n\nTo take care of the amplitude effects, we multiply our inequality by ${\\color{red}2}{:}$\n\n$$\n-1 \\le \\sin (3x - \\frac{π}{2}) \\le 1; 2 \\cdot (-1) \\le 2 \\cdot \\sin (3x - \\frac{π}{2}) \\le 2 \\cdot 1; - 2 \\le 2\\sin (3x - \\frac{π}{2}) \\le 2\n$$\n\nFinally, to deal with the vertical shift, we increase all the quantities in our inequality by $+{\\color{green}1}{:}$\n\n$$\n-2 \\le 2\\sin (3x - \\frac{π}{2}) \\le 2; - 2 + 1 \\le 2\\sin (3x - \\frac{π}{2}) + 1 \\le 2 + 1; - 1 \\le 2\\sin (3x - \\frac{π}{2}) + 1 \\le 3\n$$\n\nSince the middle part of the inequality is now identical to $f(x)$, we can write\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq f(x) \\leq 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range is $f(x)\\in [-1,3]$." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186139619 1064 "Suppose that Katie took her dog to the park, and they entered the park walking at a speed of ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) meter per second. However, the dog then saw a squirrel across the field and began sprinting toward it. After ![](images/0c/0cf2eee0cdec31b7acdc244a255aaee7d6d520cfeee67b64ac9cf215f5377477.svg) seconds, the dog had reached a speed of ![](images/c7/c700188055bde6c02303571bfb6b737fddda4a14c08edf1cdeeaf684530eea88.svg) meters per second.\n\nWe can represent this information visually using a **speed-time** graph, where speed is plotted as a function of time. To construct the speed-time graph, we use the given information to find two points on the graph.\n\n- At time ![](images/de/de0f75fef492b8c35ff6827201a03e0d0578ef7ca712eb7ec3c8fcfa7ac927b3.svg) the dog was walking at a speed of ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) meter per second. So, our graph will start at the point ![](images/fd/fd83db97b0675d3527a9f2a7a68d38a98f2d05ab5b1771d961c0d687e44c191b.svg)\n\n- We also know that it took the dog ![](images/0c/0cf2eee0cdec31b7acdc244a255aaee7d6d520cfeee67b64ac9cf215f5377477.svg) seconds to reach a speed of ![](images/c7/c700188055bde6c02303571bfb6b737fddda4a14c08edf1cdeeaf684530eea88.svg) meters per second. So, our graph will pass through the point ![](images/c9/c9abfe29c0fac723fba903ca383c02e093421e903f6474c97ffc23be28165673.svg)\n\nNow, we plot these two points and connect them to form a line.\n\n![](images/da/da19f0e96ab0a3857cba7be308c633ae43599706d6b8d521b2cf2e1de0f60596.png)\n\nThis is our speed-time graph! Its slope represents the **acceleration** of the dog, in other words, the rate of change of the speed of the dog. Using the slope formula, we compute:\n\n![](images/52/52ff8ed3eabe0f76acb51343777c8ec4f45a717ab6218a36942a8f04933df2a2.svg)\n\nSo, the acceleration of the dog is ![](images/93/93786d321f2fb2fcb47c569c1611d4ec04f668a8828d3bddfb611403855a7a4f.svg)\n\n**Note:** At first sight, the units of acceleration might look a bit weird. In this case, the units were \n![](images/1a/1a1fc64521379a6c4080c4880c70bc4596b0f24a1254c6860f49a407af6cd09e.svg)\nor, \"meters per square second.\" However, remember that this is just the simplified form of \n![](images/21/21312c7b2a13ffab89ba3729a6e4cffa647748b5ea87366ec2e349f44516863c.svg)\nor \"meters per second per second,\" which is the rate of change of speed." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186139619 1064 "Suppose that Katie took her dog to the park, and they entered the park walking at a speed of $1$ meter per second. However, the dog then saw a squirrel across the field and began sprinting toward it. After $5$ seconds, the dog had reached a speed of $8$ meters per second.\n\nWe can represent this information visually using a **speed-time** graph, where speed is plotted as a function of time. To construct the speed-time graph, we use the given information to find two points on the graph.\n\n- At time $t=0$, the dog was walking at a speed of $1$ meter per second. So, our graph will start at the point $(0,1)$.\n- We also know that it took the dog $5$ seconds to reach a speed of $8$ meters per second. So, our graph will pass through the point $(5,8)$.\n\nNow, we plot these two points and connect them to form a line.\n\n![](images/93/930f2df1aa41c994dfc03c4349541327fac3554a487878c57514252c693800ad.png)\n\nThis is our speed-time graph! Its slope represents the **acceleration** of the dog, in other words, the rate of change of the speed of the dog. Using the slope formula, we compute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{8m/s - 1m/s}{5s - 0s} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7m/s}{5s} \\\\\n&= 1.4\\frac{m/s}{s} \\\\\n&= 1.4\\frac{m}{s} \\cdot \\frac{1}{s} \\\\\n&= 1.4\\frac{m}{s^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the acceleration of the dog is\n\n$$\n1.4\\, \\dfrac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}^2}\n$$\n\n**Note:** At first sight, the units of acceleration might look a bit weird. In this case, the units were\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}^2}\n$$\n\nor, \"meters per square second.\" However, remember that this is just the simplified form of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\text{m/s}}{\\text{s}}\n$$\n\nor \"meters per second per second,\" which is the rate of change of speed." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186139620 1064 "When an object has negative acceleration, we say that it is **decelerating.** Likewise, when an object has zero acceleration, we say that it is **traveling at constant speed.**\n\nFor example, suppose a car traveling at ![](images/11/114b8ccdbfbf4aabddadfa777b5e21d210e181c6b52a2c3b0848a9bab7ac7e0c.svg) brakes and slows to a speed of ![](images/5a/5a51ab43845a8c340b642441443c8cf251637310d384bf8e1a3c95eb0b476176.svg) in a duration of ![](images/6c/6cb44f25e489afb06ec62d2e95c4d235ece77eb36e2327b856264d2fb8b22c21.svg) seconds. Then, it continues traveling at the same speed. The speed-time graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/7f/7f28203c4ebec342d5ed6896e6e37ab822b90e13ede0e8e2c02497a65a5857c8.png)\n\nThe acceleration of the car is given by the slope of the speed-time graph.\n\n- In the first ![](images/6c/6cb44f25e489afb06ec62d2e95c4d235ece77eb36e2327b856264d2fb8b22c21.svg) seconds, between the points ![](images/ba/baaf16d6a27b71b3bd168bba307a1bcdad527dc77961c78b4754398d84df9597.svg) and ![](images/21/214df2c0eae7db46b0723b042f20843d1fe9ca40b8b8a1a6ba9b6b963077f4dd.svg) the slope of the line is:\n![](images/51/51c9f674b80880d03e77b6a4d2dc2b8446c7eb35b504e295b4f1dc8ba0b49ff5.svg)\nBecause the acceleration of the car is negative, we say that the car is **decelerating** at a rate of ![](images/15/152a7623dba5cc67b196148dc787539e70ba9ebd3d3de3ce1d3149d6f49837a5.svg) In other words, it is slowing down.\n\n- In the next ![](images/6c/6cb44f25e489afb06ec62d2e95c4d235ece77eb36e2327b856264d2fb8b22c21.svg) seconds, between the points ![](images/e9/e97a34fe9cc0e368dcbc85fc293ec500a9c9ed54bebd2c0332abbeea60768dc6.svg) and ![](images/bb/bba8903a2b81c739502238b461b03d3289a4548929c3a8d35e4c747e4a4060d2.svg) the slope of the line is:\n![](images/fc/fc0cddf0523b41ebf30346f6b91ea469ce09ceaa4c84a7a7d3caddecc7e1acb9.svg)\nBecause the acceleration of the car is zero, we say that the car is **traveling at constant speed.** In other words, it is neither speeding up nor slowing down." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186139620 1064 "When an object has negative acceleration, we say that it is **decelerating.** Likewise, when an object has zero acceleration, we say that it is **traveling at constant speed.**\n\nFor example, suppose a car traveling at $15 \\textrm{m/s}$ brakes and slows to a speed of $9 \\textrm{m/s}$ in a duration of $3$ seconds. Then, it continues traveling at the same speed. The speed-time graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/5a/5a912e1018109960c2d41576f89720a5250b97c19d597276c8d7ee98b1d5df99.png)\n\nThe acceleration of the car is given by the slope of the speed-time graph.\n\n- In the first $3$ seconds, between the points $(0,15)$ and $(3,9)$, the slope of the line is:\n$m = \\dfrac{9 \\text{m/s}-15 \\text{m/s}}{3 \\text{s}-0 \\text{s}} = \\dfrac{-6 \\text{m/s}}{3 \\text{s}} = -2 \\, \\dfrac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}^2}$\nBecause the acceleration of the car is negative, we say that the car is **decelerating** at a rate of $2 \\, \\textrm{m/s}^2$. In other words, it is slowing down.\n- In the next $3$ seconds, between the points $(3,9)$ and $(6,9)$, the slope of the line is:\n$m = \\dfrac{9 \\text{m/s}-9 \\text{m/s}}{6 \\text{s}-3 \\text{s}} = \\dfrac{0 \\text{m/s}}{3 \\text{s}} = 0 \\, \\dfrac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}^2}$\nBecause the acceleration of the car is zero, we say that the car is **traveling at constant speed.** In other words, it is neither speeding up nor slowing down." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186153426 1064 "Suppose a company manager compiles the tables below, showing the years of work experience and corresponding monthly salary, in thousands of dollars, among a particular group of employees.\n\n| **Work Experience (years)** | **$2$** | **$4$** | **$5$** | **$7$** | **$9$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Salary (thousands of $\\[math]$)** | $2.5$ | $3$ | $4$ | $4.5$ | $4.5$ |\n\nTo visualize this kind of data, we can use **scatter plots**.  Let's draw a scatter plot representing the information in our table.\n\nThe first step is to set up the axes in the coordinate plane. We draw\n\n- the *independent variable* (years of work experience) along the horizontal axis, and\n\n- the *dependent variable* (salary) along the vertical axis.\n\n![](images/88/88935768ddec6e92c9e3758a5e71dcffb1812e60535f4a53ebba79d6fc1f28fe.png)\n\nThen, we plot the points that correspond to the pairs from the data:\n\n$$\n(2,2.5), \\quad\n(4,3), \\quad\n(5,4), \\quad\n(7,4.5), \\quad\n(9,4.5)\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following scatter plot.\n\n![](images/f3/f3dd4bcb349b1b71fcd15264d6d97808bf1b7f4ca37bcbd3799c1d00eb80e8eb.png)" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186153426 1064 "Suppose a company manager compiles the tables below, showing the years of work experience and corresponding monthly salary, in thousands of dollars, among a particular group of employees.\n\n| **Work Experience (years)** | **$2$** | **$4$** | **$5$** | **$7$** | **$9$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **Salary (thousands of $\\[math]$)** | $2.5$ | $3$ | $4$ | $4.5$ | $4.5$ |\n\nTo visualize this kind of data, we can use **scatter plots**. Let's draw a scatter plot representing the information in our table.\n\nThe first step is to set up the axes in the coordinate plane. We draw\n\n- the *independent variable* (years of work experience) along the horizontal axis, and\n- the *dependent variable* (salary) along the vertical axis.\n\n![](images/88/88935768ddec6e92c9e3758a5e71dcffb1812e60535f4a53ebba79d6fc1f28fe.png)\n\nThen, we plot the points that correspond to the pairs from the data:\n$(2,2.5), \\quad (4,3), \\quad (5,4), \\quad (7,4.5), \\quad (9,4.5)$\n\nThis gives us the following scatter plot.\n\n![](images/f3/f3dd4bcb349b1b71fcd15264d6d97808bf1b7f4ca37bcbd3799c1d00eb80e8eb.png)" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186153427 1064 "When constructing a scatter plot, choosing an appropriate scale on one (or both) coordinate axes is often impossible unless we distort or enlarge our plot considerably.\n\nTo demonstrate the issue and how to deal with it, consider the data in the following table, showing the ages and corresponding weights of five players on a baseball team.\n\n| **Age (years)** | **$10$** | **$11$** | **$12$** | **$13$** | **$14$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **Weight (kg)** | $30$ | $40$ | $30$ | $50$ | $70$ |\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- the data for the independent variable (age) starts at $10$ and increases in increments of one, while\n- the data for the dependent variable (weight) starts at $30$ and increases in increments of ten.\n\nIn this case, the scales for the coordinate axes of our scatter plot could be as follows:\n\n![](images/3d/3d78768a0bd5a78e3c9d7141202e64ef3bca57437c9e54a413706e59bf611bdc.png)\n\nThe \"*lightning bolt symbol*\" (highlighted) at the beginning of the $x$-axis indicates a jump or **axis discontinuity**. This symbol means we omit a whole interval on the $x$-axis unrelated to the given data to set a more convenient scale.\n\nFinally, we complete the construction of our scatter plot by plotting the points representing the data, as usual.\n\n![](images/2f/2fb453f752d2fe9f20895c68ff9de283e49a7baed569c5ae3654714d0e39efc8.png)" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186153427 1064 "When constructing a scatter plot, choosing an appropriate scale on one (or both) coordinate axes is often impossible unless we distort or enlarge our plot considerably.\n\nTo demonstrate the issue and how to deal with it, consider the data in the following table, showing the ages and corresponding weights of five players on a baseball team.    \n\n| **Age (years)** | **$10$** | **$11$** | **$12$** | **$13$** | **$14$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Weight (kg)** | $30$ | $40$ | $30$ | $50$ | $70$ |\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- the data for the independent variable (age) starts at $10$ and increases in increments of one, while\n\n- the data for the dependent variable (weight) starts at $30$ and increases in increments of ten.\n\nIn this case, the scales for the coordinate axes of our scatter plot could be as follows: \n\n![](images/3d/3d78768a0bd5a78e3c9d7141202e64ef3bca57437c9e54a413706e59bf611bdc.png)\n\nThe \"*lightning bolt symbol*\" (highlighted) at the beginning of the $x$-axis indicates a jump or **axis discontinuity**. This symbol means we omit a whole interval on the $x$-axis unrelated to the given data to set a more convenient scale.\n\nFinally, we complete the construction of our scatter plot by plotting the points representing the data, as usual. \n\n![](images/2f/2fb453f752d2fe9f20895c68ff9de283e49a7baed569c5ae3654714d0e39efc8.png)" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186153428 1064 "Suppose we want to determine if the relationship between two variables is *linear*. How would we do this?\nOne way is to draw a scatter plot and try to find a straight line that fits the data.\n\nConsider the scatter plot below that shows the correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y$.\n\n![](images/db/db07023aea39829e5567002225639ca9cc8cdcea01eb9681814eb27fe35f8eb1.png)\n\nNotice that the data points are scattered along a straight line, as shown below. This line is called the **trend line**, **regression line,** or **line of best fit.** We also say that the data has a **linear trend**.\n\n![](images/73/73a1609767711f53605a3ea8804be412596e7ff5a6295a92358ad75e3ac84631.png)\n\nOn the other hand, some data sets do *not* show a linear trend. For example, consider the following scatter plot that shows the correspondence between the variables $a$ and $b$.\n\n![](images/66/66f80436628f69486da4cf32904a53c43f54cfcb31bfd4cba501f3cd2ab200c7.png)\n\nFrom the plot, it seems that the data points are scattered randomly. Therefore, there is no linear trend." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186153428 1064 "Suppose we want to determine if the relationship between two variables is *linear*. How would we do this?\nOne way is to draw a scatter plot and try to find a straight line that fits the data. \n\nConsider the scatter plot below that shows the correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y.$ \n\n![](images/db/db07023aea39829e5567002225639ca9cc8cdcea01eb9681814eb27fe35f8eb1.png)\n\nNotice that the data points are scattered along a straight line, as shown below. This line is called the **trend line**, **regression line,** or **line of best fit.** We also say that the data has a **linear trend**.\n\n![](images/73/73a1609767711f53605a3ea8804be412596e7ff5a6295a92358ad75e3ac84631.png)\n\nOn the other hand, some data sets do *not* show a linear trend. For example, consider the following scatter plot that shows the correspondence between the variables $a$ and $b.$ \n\n![](images/66/66f80436628f69486da4cf32904a53c43f54cfcb31bfd4cba501f3cd2ab200c7.png)\n\nFrom the plot, it seems that the data points are scattered randomly. Therefore, there is no linear trend." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186153484 1064 "Just as we can find the least common multiple ($\\textrm{LCM}$) of two monomials, we can also find the least common multiple of two polynomials.\n\nTo find the least common multiple ($\\textrm{LCM}$) of two polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of the variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nFor example, let's find the least common multiple of $6(x+3)^2$ and $8(x+3)$.\n\nFirst, we find the least common multiple of the constants, $\\textrm{LCM}(6,8)$:\n\n- Multiples of $6$: $\\quad 6, \\, 12, \\, 18, \\, {\\color{blue}24}, \\, 30, \\ldots$\n- Multiples of $8$: $\\quad 8, \\, 16, \\, {\\color{blue}24}, \\, 32, \\ldots$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(6,8) = {\\color{blue}{24}}\n$$\n\nTo find the $\\textrm{LCM}$ of $(x+3)^2$ and $(x+3)$, we raise $(x+3)$ to the largest power that appears in the expressions, which is $2$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}\\left((x+3)^2, \\, (x+3) \\right) = {\\color{red}{(x+3)^2}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}\\left(6(x+3)^2, \\, 8(x+3) \\right) = {\\color{blue}{24}}\\cdot {\\color{red}{(x+3)^2}} = 24(x+3)^2\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186153484 1064 "Just as we can find the least common multiple ($\\textrm{LCM}$) of two monomials, we can also find the least common multiple of two polynomials.\n\nTo find the least common multiple ($\\textrm{LCM}$) of two polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of the variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nFor example, let's find the least common multiple of $6(x+3)^2$ and $8(x+3).$\n\nFirst, we find the least common multiple of the constants, $\\textrm{LCM}(6,8)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- Multiples of $6 \\mathbin{:}$ $\\quad 6, \\, 12, \\, 18, \\, {\\color{blue}24}, \\, 30, \\ldots$\n\n- Multiples of $8 \\mathbin{:}$ $\\quad 8, \\, 16, \\, {\\color{blue}24}, \\, 32, \\ldots$\n\nSo, $\\textrm{LCM}(6,8) =  {\\color{blue}{24}}.$\n\nTo find the $\\textrm{LCM}$ of $(x+3)^2$ and $(x+3),$ we raise $(x+3)$ to the largest power that appears in the expressions, which is $2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}\\left((x+3)^2, \\, (x+3) \\right) = {\\color{red}{(x+3)^2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}\\left(6(x+3)^2, \\, 8(x+3) \\right) = {\\color{blue}{24}}\\cdot {\\color{red}{(x+3)^2}} = 24(x+3)^2.\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186158536 1064 "Suppose we want to find all of the solutions to the equation\n\n$$\nx \\cdot 3^x - 2 \\cdot 3^x = 0\n$$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx \\cdot 3^{x} - 2 \\cdot 3^{x} &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 2) \\cdot 3^{x} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0$. This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\nx-2 = 0 \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad 3^x=0\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $x-2=0$ has the solution $x=2$.\n- The equation $3^x=0$ has no real solution because $3^x$ is positive for every real number $x$.\n\nTherefore, the original equation has a single solution, $x=2$." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186158536 1064 "Suppose we want to find all of the solutions to the equation\n\n$$\nx \\cdot 3^x - 2 \\cdot 3^x = 0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot {3}^{x}-2\\cdot {3}^{x} & =0 \\\\ (x-2)\\cdot {3}^{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\nx-2  = 0 \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad 3^x=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $x-2=0$ has the solution $x=2.$\n\n- The equation $3^x=0$ has no real solution because $3^x$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has a single solution, $x=2.$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186176956 1064 "Given two finite data sets of equal length, say,\n\n$$\nx=\\big\\{ x_1,x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\big\\}, \\qquad \ny=\\big\\{ y_1,y_2, \\ldots, y_n \\big\\},\n$$\n\nthe **covariance** between $x$ and $y,$ denoted by $\\textrm{Cov}(x,y),$ is a measure that evaluates the extent to which $x$ and $y$ change together.\n\n- A positive covariance means that if $x$ increases, then $y$ is also likely to increase:\n\n![](images/bb/bbfae3f0fb38bc8652cd26716e4b0b128cf1f2039350267034d97161d7f5dac4.png)\n\n- A negative covariance means that if $x$ increases, then $y$ is likely to decrease:\n\n![](images/fd/fd45053fdc39f070e3438c3b8c61fe01613201595cfe00bd4cc6c6e481bf73ab.png)\n\n- A covariance of zero (or close to zero) indicates that there is no *linear* relationship between $x$ and $y.$\n\n![](images/a5/a58fa4d40d77af23df86b5bd8eaca3119b93bc9ea97cc435eb4e53ba2777b071.png)\n\n**Watch Out!** A covariance of zero does *not* mean that $x$ and $y$ are not related. It simply means the relationship between them is not linear. To illustrate, consider the image below.\n\n![](images/a4/a4bdbf92e691174644dda9b9a84acb1b896c0436afb221501dc10b4635841539.png)\n\nThe (nonlinear) relationship shown above is $y=x^2.$ However, it can be shown that $\\textrm{Cov}(x,y) = 0$ for this data set.\n\nTo calculate $\\textrm{Cov}(x,y),$ we use the following formula: \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}(x,y) =  \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})(y_i - \\overline{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{x}$  and $\\overline{y}$ denote the means of $x$ and $y$ respectively.\n\n**Note**: This formula for the covariance assumes that $x$ and $y$ are drawn from a *population*. There is a slightly different formula for covariance when $x$ and $y$ are sample data. However, we won't worry about the difference for now." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186176956 1064 "Given two finite data sets of equal length, say,\n\n$$\nx=\\big\\{x_1,x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\big\\}, \\qquad y=\\big\\{y_1,y_2, \\ldots, y_n \\big\\}\n$$\n\nthe **covariance** between $x$ and $y$, denoted by $\\textrm{Cov}(x,y)$, is a measure that evaluates the extent to which $x$ and $y$ change together.\n\n- A positive covariance means that if $x$ increases, then $y$ is also likely to increase:![](images/bb/bbfae3f0fb38bc8652cd26716e4b0b128cf1f2039350267034d97161d7f5dac4.png)\n- A negative covariance means that if $x$ increases, then $y$ is likely to decrease:![](images/fd/fd45053fdc39f070e3438c3b8c61fe01613201595cfe00bd4cc6c6e481bf73ab.png)\n- A covariance of zero (or close to zero) indicates that there is no *linear* relationship between $x$ and $y$.![](images/a5/a58fa4d40d77af23df86b5bd8eaca3119b93bc9ea97cc435eb4e53ba2777b071.png)**Watch Out!** A covariance of zero does *not* mean that $x$ and $y$ are not related. It simply means the relationship between them is not linear. To illustrate, consider the image below.![](images/a4/a4bdbf92e691174644dda9b9a84acb1b896c0436afb221501dc10b4635841539.png)The (nonlinear) relationship shown above is $y=x^2$. However, it can be shown that $\\textrm{Cov}(x,y) = 0$ for this data set.\n\nTo calculate $\\textrm{Cov}(x,y)$, we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}(x,y) = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})(y_i - \\overline{y})\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{x}$ and $\\overline{y}$ denote the means of $x$ and $y$ respectively.\n\n**Note**: This formula for the covariance assumes that $x$ and $y$ are drawn from a *population*. There is a slightly different formula for covariance when $x$ and $y$ are sample data. However, we won't worry about the difference for now." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186176957 1064 "Variance measures the spread of a *single* data set $x$ around its mean value $\\overline{x}$. On the other hand, covariance measures the relationship between *two* data sets $x$ and $y$.\n\nThe (population) covariance of the data sets\n\n$$\nx=\\big\\{x_1,x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\big\\}, \\qquad y=\\big\\{y_1,y_2, \\ldots, y_n \\big\\}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}(x,y) = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})(y_i - \\overline{y})\n$$\n\nIf we compare a single data set, say $x$, against itself using our formula for covariance, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nCov(x, x) &= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))(x_{i} - x^(―)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))^{2} \\\\\n&= σ^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\sigma^2$ denotes the variance of $x$." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186176957 1064 "Variance measures the spread of a *single* data set $x$ around its mean value $\\overline{x}.$ On the other hand, covariance measures the relationship between *two* data sets $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe (population) covariance of the data sets\n\n$$\nx=\\big\\{ x_1,x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\big\\}, \\qquad  \ny=\\big\\{ y_1,y_2, \\ldots, y_n \\big\\}\n$$\n\n \n\nis given by \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}(x,y) =  \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})(y_i - \\overline{y}).\n$$\n\nIf we compare a single data set, say $x$, against itself using our formula for covariance, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,x) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & ={\\sigma }^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\sigma^2$ denotes the variance of $x.$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186177050 1064 "**Speed** is a special unit rate applied to moving objects. It is defined as *the distance traveled per unit of time*.\n\nWe can calculate the speed of a moving object using the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\text{speed} = \\dfrac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{time}}\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose that a car travels $80$ miles in $2$ hours at a constant rate. What is the speed of the car?\n\nTo find the speed (in miles per hour), we divide the number of miles, $80$, by the number of hours, $2$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{80}{2} = 40\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the car is $40$ miles per hour." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186177050 1064 "**Speed** is a special unit rate applied to moving objects. It is defined as *the distance traveled per unit of time*.\n\nWe can calculate the speed of a moving object using the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\text{speed} = \\dfrac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{time}}\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose that a car travels $80$ miles in $2$ hours at a constant rate. What is the speed of the car? \n\nTo find the speed (in miles per hour), we divide the number of miles, $80,$ by the number of hours, $2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{80}{2} = 40.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the car is $40$ miles per hour." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186177051 1064 "Let's return to the example of a car traveling at ${\\color{red}{40}}$ miles per hour.\n\nIf the car travels at this speed, it covers a distance of ${\\color{red}{40}}$ miles *every hour*. Therefore, we can deduce the following:\n\n- after ${\\color{blue}{1}}$ hour, the car covers a distance of ${\\color{red}{40}}\\times{\\color{blue}{1}} = 40$ miles\n- after ${\\color{blue}{2}}$ hours, the car covers a distance of ${\\color{red}{40}}\\times{\\color{blue}{2}} = 80$ miles\n- after ${\\color{blue}{3}}$ hours, the car covers a distance of ${\\color{red}{40}}\\times{\\color{blue}{3}} = 120$ miles\n- after ${\\color{blue}{4}}$ hours, the car covers a distance of ${\\color{red}{40}}\\times{\\color{blue}{4}} = 160$ miles\n- $\\cdots$\n\nThis suggests the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{distance} = \\text{speed} \\times \\text{time}\n$$\n\nWe can use this formula to calculate the distance traveled by an object moving at a constant speed.\n\nFor instance, suppose an elevator traveled for $1.5$ minutes at a constant speed of $120$ feet per minute. What distance is covered by the elevator?\n\nThe speed is $120$ feet per minute. To find the distance covered in $1.5$ minutes, we multiply the speed by $1.5$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndistance &= speed \\cdot time \\\\\n&= 120 \\cdot 1.5 \\\\\n&= 180\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elevator covered a distance of $180$ feet." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186177051 1064 "Let's return to the example of a car traveling at ${\\color{red}{40}}$ miles per hour. \n\nIf the car travels at this speed, it covers  a distance of ${\\color{red}{40}}$ miles *every hour*. Therefore, we can deduce the following:\n\n- after ${\\color{blue}{1}}$ hour, the car covers a distance of ${\\color{red}{40}}\\times{\\color{blue}{1}} = 40$ miles\n\n- after ${\\color{blue}{2}}$ hours, the car covers a distance of ${\\color{red}{40}}\\times{\\color{blue}{2}} = 80$ miles\n\n- after ${\\color{blue}{3}}$ hours, the car covers a distance of ${\\color{red}{40}}\\times{\\color{blue}{3}} = 120$ miles\n\n- after ${\\color{blue}{4}}$ hours, the car covers a distance of ${\\color{red}{40}}\\times{\\color{blue}{4}} = 160$ miles\n\n- $\\cdots$\n\nThis suggests the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{distance} = \\text{speed} \\times \\text{time}\n$$\n\nWe can use this formula to calculate the distance traveled by an object moving at a constant speed.\n\nFor instance, suppose an elevator traveled for $1.5$ minutes at a constant speed of $120$ feet per minute. What distance is covered by the elevator?\n\nThe speed is $120$ feet per minute. To find the distance covered in $1.5$ minutes, we multiply the speed by $1.5.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{distance} & =\\text{speed}\\times \\text{time} \\\\  & =120\\times 1.5 \\\\  & =180.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elevator covered a distance of $180$ feet." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186177052 1064 "Let's recall the formula relating distance to speed and time:\n\n$$\n\\text{speed} \\times \\text{time} = \\text{distance}\n$$\n\nUsing the relationship between multiplication and division, we can write this equation as\n\n$$\n\\text{time} = \\text{distance} \\div \\text{speed}\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{time} = \\dfrac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{speed}}\n$$\n\nWe can use this formula to calculate the time taken for an object moving at a particular speed to cover a certain distance.\n\nFor example, suppose a plane flew for $3\\,120$ kilometers at a constant speed of $520$ kilometers per hour. For how long was the plane in motion?\n\nTo determine the time the plane was in motion, we divide the distance, $3\\,120$ kilometers, by the speed, $520$ kilometers per hour. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ntime &= \\frac{distance}{speed} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3120}{520} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3120 \\div 10}{520 \\div 10} \\\\\n&= \\frac{312}{52} \\\\\n&= 312 \\div 52 \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the plane was in motion for $6$ hours." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186177052 1064 "Let's recall the formula relating distance to speed and time:\n\n$$\n\\text{speed} \\times \\text{time} = \\text{distance}\n$$\n\nUsing the relationship between multiplication and division, we can write this equation as\n\n$$\n\\text{time} = \\text{distance} \\div \\text{speed}\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{time} = \\dfrac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{speed}}.\n$$\n\nWe can use this formula to calculate the time taken for an object moving at a particular speed to cover a certain distance. \n\nFor example, suppose a plane flew for $3\\,120$ kilometers at a constant speed of $520$ kilometers per hour. For how long was the plane in motion?\n\nTo determine the time the plane was in motion, we divide the distance, $3\\,120$ kilometers, by the speed, $520$ kilometers per hour. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{time} & =\\frac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{speed}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\,120}{520} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\,120÷10}{520÷10} \\\\  & =\\frac{312}{52} \\\\  & =312÷52 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the plane was in motion for $6$ hours." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186177070 1064 "Let's look again at the graph of the function $y=\\cos x$.\n\n![](images/88/88378ece5b1721cdc533d07d69c004901539c7f8fda9a5a3e5c5648ed32905c5.png)\n\nThe cosine function has infinitely many zeros and an infinite number of points where the function reaches its maximum and minimum value. Let's write general expressions for these properties, assuming $n$ is an integer.\n\n**The Zeros of the Function**\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the zeros of the function are\n\n$$\nx=\\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\pm \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\dots\n$$\n\nA general expression for the zeros is\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + n\\pi\n$$\n\n**The Maxima of the Function**\n\nThe function has a maximum when $x=0$ and is periodic with period $2\\pi$. Therefore, a general expression for the values of $x$ that maximize cosine is\n\n$$\nx = 2n\\pi\n$$\n\n**The Minima of the Function**\n\nThe function has a minimum when $x=\\pi$ and is periodic with period $2\\pi$. Therefore, a general expression for the values of $x$ that minimize cosine is\n\n$$\nx = \\pi + 2n\\pi\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186177070 1064 "Let's look again at the graph of the function $y=\\cos x.$ \n\n![](images/88/88378ece5b1721cdc533d07d69c004901539c7f8fda9a5a3e5c5648ed32905c5.png)\n\nThe cosine function has infinitely many zeros and an infinite number of points where the function reaches its maximum and minimum value. Let's write general expressions for these properties, assuming $n$ is an integer.\n\n**The Zeros of the Function**\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the zeros of the function are $x=\\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\pm \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\dots$ \n\nA general expression for the zeros is\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + n\\pi.\n$$\n\n**The Maxima of the Function**\n\nThe function has a maximum when $x=0$ and is periodic with period $2\\pi.$ Therefore, a general expression for the values of $x$ that maximize cosine is\n\n$$\nx = 2n\\pi.\n$$\n\n**The Minima of the Function**\n\nThe function has a minimum when $x=\\pi$ and is periodic with period $2\\pi.$ Therefore, a general expression for the values of $x$ that minimize cosine is\n\n$$\nx = \\pi + 2n\\pi.\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186177071 1064 "Let's look at the graph of the cosine function again.\n\n![](images/04/04f13f2dec860874174f51990f4260615e7be893a6be5f86b3b13857c4e8953f.png)\n\nThe periodicity of the function is another characteristic of its graph. The period of the cosine function is $2\\pi$, shown below:\n\n![](images/78/789fa06939a3e3350a18c4e9cdf078bf6baf3f025709703e608cb3b7ba89e698.png)\n\nThis means that\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(x+2n\\pi\\right) = \\cos \\left(x\\right)\n$$\n\nfor any number $x$ and any integer $n$." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186177071 1064 "Let's look at the graph of the cosine function again.\n\n![](images/04/04f13f2dec860874174f51990f4260615e7be893a6be5f86b3b13857c4e8953f.png)\n\nThe periodicity of the function is another characteristic of its graph. The period of the cosine function is $2\\pi,$ shown below:\n\n![](images/78/789fa06939a3e3350a18c4e9cdf078bf6baf3f025709703e608cb3b7ba89e698.png)\n\nThis means that $\\cos\\left( x+2n\\pi\\right) = \\cos \\left( x\\right)$ for any number $x$ and any integer $n.$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186177072 1064 "Let's look at the graph of $y=\\tan x$.\n\n![](images/31/31b430f003171dcc04a8f17402d6035fbbc305c244d3676bbf5da83fa447bd50.png)\n\nThis graph also has infinitely many zeros, and there is an infinite number of asymptotes of the function. We can w