 "Recall that a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(-x) = -f(x)\n$$\n\nAs it turns out, cosecant is an odd function! So, for any value of $x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\csc(-x) = -\\csc(x)\n$$\n\nOdd functions always have rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This is indeed true of the cosecant function, as we can see from the diagram below.\n\n![](images/cb/cb63f7c0930524e9f54a65aad6d74784c403a6255f723b879d76586ad8ea598b.png)\n\nWe can use the oddness property of cosecant in conjunction with its periodicity property to simplify trigonometric expressions. Let's see an example." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186177092 1064 "Recall that a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(-x) = -f(x).\n$$\n\nAs it turns out, cosecant is an odd function! So, for any value of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\csc(-x) = -\\csc(x).\n$$\n\nOdd functions always have rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This is indeed true of the cosecant function, as we can see from the diagram below. \n\n![](images/cb/cb63f7c0930524e9f54a65aad6d74784c403a6255f723b879d76586ad8ea598b.png)\n\nWe can use the oddness property of cosecant in conjunction with its periodicity property to simplify trigonometric expressions. Let's see an example." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186177101 1064 "To find the slope of a line that is perpendicular to a given line\n\n$$\ny=mx+b\n$$\n\nwe can just take the **negative reciprocal** of the given slope $m$. So, the slope of the perpendicular line would be\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{1}{m}\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the line\n\n$$\ny = 3x + 1\n$$\n\nwhich has a slope of $m=3$. The negative reciprocal of $3$ is\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nSo, any line with a slope of\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nwill be perpendicular to the given line, because\n\n$$\n3 \\cdot \\left(-\\dfrac{1}{3} \\right) = -1\n$$\n\nTo illustrate, some lines with a slope of\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nare plotted below. We can see that they are all perpendicular to the given line\n\n$$\ny=3x+1\n$$\n\n![](images/e2/e2772aa0cbe3e736e1129b52c59d3a7c36759a0ccf7b7a6b409cce09b2b22497.png)" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186177101 1064 "To find the slope of a line that is perpendicular to a given line $y=mx+b,$ we can just take the **negative reciprocal** of the given slope $m.$ So, the slope of the perpendicular line would be $-\\dfrac{1}{m}.$\n\nFor example, consider the line $y = 3x + 1,$ which has a slope of $m=3.$ The negative reciprocal of $3$ is $-\\dfrac{1}{3}.$ So, any line with a slope of $-\\dfrac{1}{3}$ will be perpendicular to the given line, because\n\n$$\n3 \\cdot \\left( -\\dfrac{1}{3} \\right) = -1.\n$$\n\nTo illustrate, some lines with a slope of $-\\dfrac{1}{3}$ are plotted below. We can see that they are all perpendicular to the given line $y=3x+1.$\n\n![](images/e2/e2772aa0cbe3e736e1129b52c59d3a7c36759a0ccf7b7a6b409cce09b2b22497.png)" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186180666 1064 "Sometimes, we can use ratios to find quantities that aren't explicitly given in the ratio.\n\nFor instance, suppose that the ratio of boys to girls in the fifth grade is $3: 4$. If there are $15$ boys, how many students are there in total?\n\nNotice that the \"total number of students\" doesn't appear in the ratio. However, we can find the number of students by adding the total number of boys and the number of girls.\n\nWe start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}3}:{\\color{red}4}$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}3}}{{\\color{red}4}}\n$$\n\nIn this fraction, the numerator represents the number of boys and the denominator represents the number of girls.\n\nWe know that there are $15$ boys, so we want to make a numerator of $15$. We can do this by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $5$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}3}\\times 5}{{\\color{red}4}\\times 5} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}15}}{{\\color{red}20}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since there are $15$ boys in the fifth grade, there must also be $20$ girls. So the total number of students is\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}15} + {\\color{red}20} = 35\\,\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186180666 1064 "Sometimes, we can use ratios to find quantities that aren't explicitly given in the ratio.\n\nFor instance, suppose that the ratio of boys to girls in the fifth grade is $3: 4.$ If there are $15$ boys, how many students are there in total?\n\nNotice that the \"total number of students\" doesn't appear in the ratio. However, we can find the number of students by adding the total number of boys and the number of girls.\n\nWe start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}3}:{\\color{red}4}$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}3}}{{\\color{red}4}}\n$$\n\nIn this fraction, the numerator represents the number of boys and the denominator represents the number of girls.\n\nWe know that there are $15$ boys, so we want to make a numerator of $15.$ We can do this by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $5\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}3}\\times 5}{{\\color{red}4}\\times 5} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}15}}{{\\color{red}20}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since there are $15$ boys in the fifth grade, there must also be $20$ girls. So the total number of students is \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}15} + {\\color{red}20} = 35\\,.\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186180672 1064 "The most general way of solving exponential equations with different bases is to use logarithms on both sides of the equation.\nFor example, given the equation\n\n$$\n3^{x+1} = 5^{x}\n$$\n\nwe could apply $\\log_3$ on both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\log_3(3^{x+1}) = \\log_3(5^{x})\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that\n\n$$\n\\log_b \\left(b^c \\right) = c\n$$\n\nto simplify the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 1 &= \\log_{3} (5^{x})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law for logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 1 &= x\\log_{3} (5)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 1 &= x\\log_{3} (5) \\\\\nx - x\\log_{3} (5) &= -1 \\\\\nx(1 - \\log_{3} (5)) &= -1 \\\\\nx &= - (1)/(1 - \\log_{3} (5)) \\\\\nx &= (1)/(\\log_{3} (5) - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** in the first step, we also could have applied $\\log_5$ on both sides of our equation, instead of $\\log_3$. Then, the answer would be numerically the same but expressed using $\\log_5$ instead of $\\log_3$." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186180672 1064 "The most general way of solving exponential equations with different bases is to use logarithms on both sides of the equation. \nFor example, given the equation\n\n$$\n3^{x+1} = 5^{x},\n$$\n\nwe could apply $\\log_3$ on both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\log_3(3^{x+1}) = \\log_3(5^{x}).\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\log_b \\left( b^c \\right) = c$ to simplify the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & ={log}_{3}({5}^{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law for logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =x{log}_{3}(5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =x{log}_{3}(5) \\\\ x-x{log}_{3}(5) & =-1 \\\\ x(1-{log}_{3}(5)) & =-1 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{1-{log}_{3}(5)} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{{log}_{3}(5)-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** in the first step, we also could have applied $\\log_5$ on both sides of our equation, instead of $\\log_3.$ Then, the answer would be numerically the same but expressed using $\\log_5$ instead of $\\log_3.$\n\n### General Case\n\nIn general, let's consider an equation of the form\n\n\\[\na^{f(x)} = b^{g(x)},\n\\]\n\nwhere [math]a[/math] and [math]b[/math] are different constants that can't be presented as powers of the same base, and [math]f(x)[/math], [math]g(x)[/math] are some functions.\nWe now can apply \n\\[\n\\log_a\n\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad\n\\log_b\n\\]\nas well as the common logarithm ([math]\\log[/math]) or the natural logarithm ([math]\\ln[/math]) on both sides of our equation to get rid of the exponents." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186180682 1064 "Factorials can be applied to variables, too. To manipulate a factorial expression involving a variable, we just need to remember that the variable represents a number, and the previous number can be obtained by subtracting $1$ from the variable.\n\nSo, in the same way that we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5! &= 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1^(⏞)^(4!) \\\\\n&= 5 \\cdot 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe also have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn! &= n \\cdot (n - 1) \\cdot (n - 2) \\cdot ⋯ \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1^(⏞)^((n - 1)!) \\\\\n&= n \\cdot (n - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes some positive integer.\n\nLikewise, in the same way that we have\n\n$$\n5! = 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3\n$$\n\nwe also have\n\n$$\nx! = x \\cdot (x-1) \\cdot (x-2)\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186180682 1064 "Factorials can be applied to variables, too. To manipulate a factorial expression involving a variable, we just need to remember that the variable represents a number, and the previous number can be obtained by subtracting $1$ from the variable.\n\nSo, in the same way that we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5! & =5\\cdot \\overset{4!}{\\overset{⏞}{4\\cdot 3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1}} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 4!\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe also have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n! & =n\\cdot \\overset{(n-1)!}{\\overset{⏞}{(n-1)\\cdot (n-2)\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1}} \\\\  & =n\\cdot (n-1)!\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes some positive integer.\n\nLikewise, in the same way that we have\n\n$$\n5! = 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3!\n$$\n\nwe also have\n\n$$\nx! = x \\cdot (x-1) \\cdot (x-2)!\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186180743 1064 "We can use trend lines to predict values for data that follows a linear trend.\n\nConsider the scatter plot below that gives the correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y$. Let's predict the value of $y$ when $x = 5$.\n\n![](images/8a/8ae392764c110d85f913353ddd932fad9d9c7fedde6275c2705cc7f3e92d3844.png)\n\nFirst, we sketch the trend line. If we don't know the trend line's equation, then we simply approximate it.\n\n![](images/2e/2e001c3f1e9dbdc2574913116a0fb73ac70396e97777c56b9d36b683d10d9814.png)\n\nTo make our prediction, we proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we start at $x = 5$ on the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/fb/fb4f4ac52e4e2190b54cf9cf272e720729635c48387e6ac439ffcaf1282a2d7d.png)\n\n- Then, we move up until we intersect the trend line.\n\n![](images/46/46a2e566d3810362756e6061e3120b7e7140a8a71591a6a1b90fd059b0505ae0.png)\n\n- Finally, we move left until we intersect the $y$-axis.\n\n![](images/8d/8db3717a601f2e5b22c507385a04c60531d2ed057adc5372c5dddba174cf703f.png)\n\nTherefore, when $x = 5$, we obtain\n\n$$\ny \\approx 10\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186180743 1064 "We can use trend lines to predict values for data that follows a linear trend.\n\nConsider the scatter plot below that gives the correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y.$ Let's predict the value of $y$ when $x = 5.$\n\n![](images/8a/8ae392764c110d85f913353ddd932fad9d9c7fedde6275c2705cc7f3e92d3844.png)\n\nFirst, we sketch the trend line. If we don't know the trend line's equation, then we simply approximate it.\n\n![](images/2e/2e001c3f1e9dbdc2574913116a0fb73ac70396e97777c56b9d36b683d10d9814.png)\n\nTo make our prediction, we proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we start at $x = 5$ on the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/fb/fb4f4ac52e4e2190b54cf9cf272e720729635c48387e6ac439ffcaf1282a2d7d.png)\n\n- Then, we move up until we intersect the trend line.\n\n![](images/46/46a2e566d3810362756e6061e3120b7e7140a8a71591a6a1b90fd059b0505ae0.png)\n\n- Finally, we move left until we intersect the $y$-axis.\n\n![](images/8d/8db3717a601f2e5b22c507385a04c60531d2ed057adc5372c5dddba174cf703f.png)\n\nTherefore, when $x = 5,$ we obtain $y \\approx 10.$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186180744 1064 "If we know the equation of a trend line, we can make more accurate predictions about a given data set. Let's consider an example.\n\nThe pediatric unit of a hospital wants to know the relationship between the age $x$ (in years) and weight $y$ (in kilograms) of their patients. They collected data over several weeks and plotted the diagram given below.\n\n![](images/b6/b63e7db30ad2533058b9e2191f699043784a42b2b3f73525783f720cf84e0fc5.png)\n\nThe hospital finds that the trend line equation is\n\n$$\ny = 2x+8\n$$\n\nNow, suppose the hospital wants to estimate the weight of a $3$-year-old child. To do this, we substitute $x=3$ into the equation of the trend line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 2x + 8 \\\\\n&= 2(3) + 8 \\\\\n&= 6 + 8 \\\\\n&= 14\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our model predicts that a typical $3$-year-old patient weighs approximately $14\\,\\textrm{kg}$.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- There is a point on our trend line where $x=3$. Therefore, the result $y=14$ should be reliable since we can see from the graph that the data follows a linear trend for values of $x$ close to $3$.\n- Making predictions inside the range of values from which the data was collected is known as **interpolation**." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186180744 1064 "If we know the equation of a trend line, we can make more accurate predictions about a given data set. Let's consider an example.\n\nThe pediatric unit of a hospital wants to know the relationship between the age $x$ (in years) and weight $y$ (in kilograms) of their patients. They collected data over several weeks and plotted the diagram given below. \n\n![](images/b6/b63e7db30ad2533058b9e2191f699043784a42b2b3f73525783f720cf84e0fc5.png)\n\nThe hospital finds that the trend line equation is $y = 2x+8.$\n\nNow, suppose the hospital wants to estimate the weight of a $3$-year-old child.  To do this, we substitute $x=3$ into the equation of the trend line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x+8 \\\\  & =2(3)+8 \\\\  & =6+8 \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, our model predicts that a typical $3$-year-old patient weighs approximately $14\\,\\textrm{kg}.$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- There is a point on our trend line where $x=3.$ Therefore, the result $y=14$ should be reliable since we can see from the graph that the data follows a linear trend for values of $x$ close to $3.$\n\n- Making predictions inside the range of values from which the data was collected is known as **interpolation**." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186180757 1064 "![](images/9c/9cac9d494a95fab39dc7fd71f2d0daf9d474157d453dac61b25ab71da4cc580f.png)\n\nNow suppose that the hospital wants to estimate the weight of a $7$-year-old child. They have no data for this age. However, if they assume that the trend continues, they can find the corresponding weight by substituting $x=7$ into the trend line equation. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 2x + 8 \\\\\n&= 2(7) + 8 \\\\\n&= 14 + 8 \\\\\n&= 22\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our model predicts that a typical $7$-year-old child weighs approximately $22 \\, \\textrm{kg}$.\n\n**Warning:** The point where $x=7$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, the result $y=22$ *may be unreliable* since we don't know whether values of $x$ close to $7$ follow the same linear trend. The diagram below shows some trends the data *might* follow beyond our initial data range.\n\n![](images/9e/9ea0d5bde6ac6854ea9992cac38f3bf06acb54b0065fff8174d75c89992203b0.png)\n\nMaking predictions outside the range of values from which the data was collected is known as **extrapolation**.\n\nWe must be very cautious when extrapolating in real-world situations." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186180757 1064 "![](images/9c/9cac9d494a95fab39dc7fd71f2d0daf9d474157d453dac61b25ab71da4cc580f.png)\n\nNow suppose that the hospital wants to estimate the weight of a $7$-year-old child. They have no data for this age. However, if they assume that the trend continues, they can find the corresponding weight by substituting $x=7$ into the trend line equation. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x+8 \\\\  & =2(7)+8 \\\\  & =14+8 \\\\  & =22.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, our model predicts that a typical $7$-year-old child weighs approximately $22 \\, \\textrm{kg}.$\n\n**Warning:** The point where $x=7$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, the result $y=22$ *may be unreliable* since we don't know whether values of $x$ close to $7$ follow the same linear trend. The diagram below shows some trends the data *might* follow beyond our initial data range.\n\n![](images/9e/9ea0d5bde6ac6854ea9992cac38f3bf06acb54b0065fff8174d75c89992203b0.png)\n\nMaking predictions outside the range of values from which the data was collected is known as **extrapolation**.\n\nWe must be very cautious when extrapolating in real-world situations." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186185306 1064 "Consider the quadratic equation\n\n$$\nax^2+bx+c = 0\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\na\\neq 0\n$$\n\nWe can derive a general solution to this equation by completing the square.\n\nFirst, let's subtract $c$ from both sides to isolate the variable terms:\n\n$$\nax^2+bx = -c\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\na\\neq 0\n$$\n\ndividing by $a$ gives\n\n$$\nx^2 + \\dfrac{b}{a}x = -\\dfrac{c}{a}\n$$\n\nNext, we complete the square on the left-hand side and move the constant terms to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 2 \\cdot \\frac{b}{2a}x =-\\frac{c}{a} \\\\\n(x + \\frac{b}{2a})^{2} - (\\frac{b}{2a})^{2} &= -\\frac{c}{a} \\\\\n(x + \\frac{b}{2a})^{2} - \\frac{b^{2}}{4a^{2}} &= -\\frac{c}{a} \\\\\n(x + \\frac{b}{2a})^{2} &= \\frac{b^{2}}{4a^{2}} - \\frac{c}{a}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the terms on the right-hand side over a common factor of $4a^2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x + \\dfrac{b}{2a}\\right)^2 = \\dfrac{b^2 - 4ac}{4a^2}\n$$\n\nTaking the square root of both sides yields\n\n$$\nx + \\dfrac{b}{2a} = \\pm \\sqrt{\\dfrac{b^2 - 4ac}{4a^2}}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + \\frac{b}{2a} &= ± \\frac{\\sqrt{b^{2} - 4ac}}{2a}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we isolate $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= -\\frac{b}{2a} ± \\frac{\\sqrt{b^{2} - 4ac}}{2a}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\n$$\n\nThis formula is called the **quadratic formula**. You'll learn more about how to apply the quadratic formula in future lessons." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186185306 1064 "Consider the quadratic equation\n\n$$\nax^2+bx+c = 0,\n$$\n\nwhere $a\\neq 0.$ We can derive a general solution to this equation by completing the square.\n\nFirst, let's subtract $c$ from both sides to isolate the variable terms:\n\n$$\nax^2+bx = -c\n$$\n\nSince $a\\neq 0,$ dividing by $a$ gives\n\n$$\nx^2 + \\dfrac{b}{a}x = -\\dfrac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nNext, we complete the square on the left-hand side and move the constant terms to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{b}{2a}x=-\\frac{c}{a} \\\\ {(x+\\frac{b}{2a})}^{2}-{(\\frac{b}{2a})}^{2} & =-\\frac{c}{a} \\\\ {(x+\\frac{b}{2a})}^{2}-\\frac{{b}^{2}}{4{a}^{2}} & =-\\frac{c}{a} \\\\ {(x+\\frac{b}{2a})}^{2} & =\\frac{{b}^{2}}{4{a}^{2}}-\\frac{c}{a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the terms on the right-hand side over a common factor of $4a^2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x + \\dfrac{b}{2a}\\right)^2  = \\dfrac{b^2 - 4ac}{4a^2}.\n$$\n\nTaking the square root of both sides yields\n\n$$\nx + \\dfrac{b}{2a}  = \\pm \\sqrt{\\dfrac{b^2 - 4ac}{4a^2}}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{b}{2a} & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we isolate $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{b}{2a}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}.\n$$\n\nThis formula is called the **quadratic formula**. You'll learn more about how to apply the quadratic formula in future lessons." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186185343 1064 "Let's think about how to complete the square on the following quadratic expression:\n\n$$\n- \\left(x^2 + 6x+4\\right)\n$$\n\nWe begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\nx^2 + 6x + {\\color{purple}{4}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{3}}x  + {\\color{purple}{4}}\n$$\n\nThen, we add and then subtract ${\\color{blue}{3}}^2,$ writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}{4}}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{3}}x + {\\color{blue}{3}}^2} - {\\color{blue}{3}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{4}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+{\\color{blue}{3}})^2 - {\\color{blue}{3}}^2+ {\\color{purple}{4}}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+{\\color{blue}{3}})^2 - 5\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}+6x+4) & =-((x+3)^{2}-5) \\\\  & =-(x+3)^{2}+5 \\\\  & =5-(x+3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186185343 1064 "Let's think about how to complete the square on the following quadratic expression:\n\n$$\n-\\left(x^2 + 6x+4\\right)\n$$\n\nWe begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\nx^2 + 6x + {\\color{purple}{4}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{3}}x + {\\color{purple}{4}}\n$$\n\nThen, we add and then subtract ${\\color{blue}{3}}^2$, writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}{4}}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{3}}x + {\\color{blue}{3}}^2} - {\\color{blue}{3}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{4}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+{\\color{blue}{3}})^2 - {\\color{blue}{3}}^2+ {\\color{purple}{4}}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+{\\color{blue}{3}})^2 - 5\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n- (x^{2} + 6x + 4) &= - ((x + 3)^{2} - 5) \\\\\n&= - (x + 3)^{2} + 5 \\\\\n&= 5 - (x + 3)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186185358 1064 "We can use the method of completing the square to solve quadratic equations with leading coefficients.\n\nAs an example, let's solve the following quadratic equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n4x^2-8x+3 = 0\n$$\n\nFirst, we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x^{2} - 8x + 3 &= 0 \\\\\n4x^{2} - 8x &= -3 \\\\\n4(x^{2} - 2x) &= -3 \\\\\n4(x^{2} - 2 \\cdot 1 \\cdot x + 1^{2} - 1^{2}) &= -3 \\\\\n4[(x - 1)^{2} - 1] &= -3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, expanding the brackets, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4[(x - 1)^{2} - 1] &= -3 \\\\\n4(x - 1)^{2} - 4 &= -3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we isolate the perfect square by adding $4$ to both sides and then dividing by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4(x - 1)^{2} - 4 &= -3 \\\\\n4(x - 1)^{2} - 4 + 4 &= -3 + 4 \\\\\n4(x - 1)^{2} &= 1 \\\\\n(x - 1)^{2} &= \\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - 1)^{2} &= \\frac{1}{4} \\\\\n\\sqrt{(x - 1)^{2}} &= \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\\n\\mid x - 1 \\mid &= \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\nx - 1 &= ± \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\nx &= 1 ± \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= 1 - \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\nx_{2} &= 1 + \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac32\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186185358 1064 "We can use the method of completing the square to solve quadratic equations with leading coefficients. \n\nAs an example, let's solve the following quadratic equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n4x^2-8x+3 = 0\n$$\n\nFirst, we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-8x+3 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-8x & =-3 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}-2x) & =-3 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1\\cdot x+{1}^{2}-{1}^{2}) & =-3 \\\\ 4[(x-1)^{2}-1] & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, expanding the brackets, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4[(x-1)^{2}-1] & =-3 \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2}-4 & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we isolate the perfect square by adding  $4$ to both sides and then dividing by $4\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-1)^{2}-4 & =-3 \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2}-4+4 & =-3+4 \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{(x-1)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\ |x-1| & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =1\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =1-\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} & =1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\dfrac12$ and $x=\\dfrac32.$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186185359 1064 "We can apply combinations of vertical and horizontal shifts and stretches to tangent and cotangent curves.\n\nFor example, let's consider the function\n\n$$\ny = \\tan(Bx) + D\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ and $D$ are constants. The graph of this function is shown below.\n\n![](images/58/58c3a7647037c409235c0bf0c532794e0bc8eec47daf379c23488b0a0c8bac42.png)\n\nLet's compute the values of $B$ and $D$ using our knowledge of graph transformations.\n\nFirstly, recall that we can calculate the period $T$ of a tangent or cotangent function by considering the difference in the $x$-values between two consecutive asymptotes. Therefore,\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} - \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right) = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function\n\n$$\ny = A\\tan(Bx + C) + D\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{\\pi}{B}\n$$\n\nBy applying this formula, we can calculate the value of $B$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{3} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{B} \\quad\\Longrightarrow \\quad B = \\dfrac{\\pi}{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)} = 3\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the tangent function. The graph shows that the function has been translated by $1$ unit down. Hence, $D = -1$.\n\nTherefore, the equation of our curve is\n\n$$\ny = \\tan(3x) - 1\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186185359 1064 "We can apply combinations of vertical and horizontal shifts and stretches to tangent and cotangent curves.\n\nFor example, let's consider the function\n\n$$\ny = \\tan(Bx) + D,\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ and $D$ are constants. The graph of this function is shown below.\n\n![](images/58/58c3a7647037c409235c0bf0c532794e0bc8eec47daf379c23488b0a0c8bac42.png)\n\nLet's compute the values of $B$ and $D$ using our knowledge of graph transformations.\n\nFirstly, recall that we can calculate the period $T$ of a tangent or cotangent function by considering the difference in the $x$-values between two consecutive asymptotes. Therefore, \n\n$$\nT =  \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} - \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right) = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y = A\\tan(Bx + C) + D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{\\pi}{B}.\n$$\n\nBy applying this formula, we can calculate the value of $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{3} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{B} \\quad\\Longrightarrow \\quad B = \\dfrac{\\pi}{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)} = 3\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the tangent function. The graph shows that the function has been translated by $1$ unit down. Hence, $D = -1.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of our curve is\n\n$$\ny = \\tan(3x) - 1.\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186185389 1064 "Let's recall the formula for factoring a perfect square:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}}x + a^2 = (x + {\\color{blue}{a}})^2\n$$\n\nAs we've seen, **completing the square** on a quadratic expression means expressing it as the sum of a perfect square and a constant.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn how to complete the square on a quadratic expression when the coefficient of the linear term is odd. In these cases, we get a fraction in the final expression.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the following quadratic expression:\n\n$$\n\\left(x^2 + 5x + \\left(\\dfrac 52\\right)^2\\right) + 1\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the large parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 5x + (\\frac{5}{2})^{2} &=  \\\\\nx^{2} + 2 \\cdot \\frac{5}{2}x + (\\frac{5}{2})^{2} &=  \\\\\n&= (x + \\frac{5}{2})^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression can be written as\n\n$$\n(x + \\frac{5}{2})^{2} + 1\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186185389 1064 "Let's recall the formula for factoring a perfect square:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}}x + a^2 = (x + {\\color{blue}{a}})^2\n$$\n\nAs we've seen, **completing the square** on a quadratic expression means expressing it as the sum of a perfect square and a constant.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn how to complete the square on a quadratic expression when the coefficient of the linear term is odd. In these cases, we get a fraction in the final expression.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the following quadratic expression:\n\n$$\n\\left(x^2 + 5x + \\left(\\dfrac 52\\right)^2\\right) + 1\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the large parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+5x+{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}x+{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} & = \\\\ {(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186185390 1064 "Let's complete the square on the following expression:\n\n$$\nx^2 + 3x + 4\n$$\n\nThe procedure is very similar to the one we encountered when the coefficient of the linear term was even.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}x + {\\color{purple}{4}}\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract*\n\n$$\n\\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}\\right)^2\n$$\n\nwriting the constant term ${\\color{purple}{4}}$ last:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}x + \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}\\right)^2 - \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}\\right)^2 + {\\color{purple}{4}}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first three terms now form a perfect square!\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}x + \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}\\right)^2} - \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}\\right)^2 + {\\color{purple}{4}}\n$$\n\nFactoring the perfect square, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x + {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}\\right)^2 - \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}\\right)^2 + {\\color{purple}{4}}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + \\frac{3}{2})^{2} - (\\frac{3}{2})^{2} + 4 &=  \\\\\n(x + \\frac{3}{2})^{2} - \\frac{9}{4} + 4 &=  \\\\\n(x + \\frac{3}{2})^{2} - \\frac{9}{4} + \\frac{16}{4} &=  \\\\\n&= (x + \\frac{3}{2})^{2} + \\frac{7}{4} \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186185390 1064 "Let's complete the square on the following expression: \n\n$$\nx^2 + 3x + 4\n$$\n\nThe procedure is very similar to the one we encountered when the coefficient of the linear term was even.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}x + {\\color{purple}{4}}\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* $\\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}\\right)^2,$ writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}{4}}$ last:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}x  + \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}\\right)^2 - \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}\\right)^2 + {\\color{purple}{4}}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first three terms now form a perfect square!\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}x  + \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}\\right)^2} - \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}\\right)^2 + {\\color{purple}{4}}\n$$\n\nFactoring the perfect square, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x + {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}\\right)^2  - \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}\\right)^2 + {\\color{purple}{4}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+4 & = \\\\ {(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}+4 & = \\\\ {(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{16}{4} & = \\\\ {(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{7}{4} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186205180 1064 "Some linear equations can have **no solutions.** This means there is no value of $x$ that can be substituted into the equation to give a true statement.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following equation:\n\n$$\nx+1 = x\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to solve this equation. We start by subtracting $x$ from both sides.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 1 &= x \\\\\nx + 1- x &= x- x \\\\\n(x - x) + 1 &= (x - x) \\\\\n0 + 1 &= 0 \\\\\n1 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the final equation, $1=0$, is always **false**. In other words, there is no value of $x$ that we can substitute to make the equation true.\n\nTherefore, our equation has no solutions." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186205180 1064 "Some linear equations can have **no solutions.** This means there is no value of $x$ that can be substituted into the equation to give a true statement.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following equation:\n\n$$\nx+1 = x\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to solve this equation. We start by subtracting $x$ from both sides.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =x \\\\ x+1\\,-\\,x & =x\\,-\\,x \\\\ (x-x)+1 & =(x-x) \\\\ 0+1 & =0 \\\\ 1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the final equation, $1=0,$ is always **false**. In other words, there is no value of $x$ that we can substitute to make the equation true. \n\nTherefore, our equation has no solutions." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186205213 1064 "Consider the equation in the variable $x$, given by\n\n$$\n7qx = -5\n$$\n\nwhere $q$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solutions, what is the value of $q$?\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax = b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{7q}_{\\large a} \\ x = \\underbrace{-5}_{\\large b}\n$$\n\nIn this case,\n\n$$\na = 7q, \\qquad b = -5\n$$\n\nWe know that an equation of the form $ax=b$ with\n\n$$\nb \\neq 0\n$$\n\nhas no solutions when $a = 0$.\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n7q = 0\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7q &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{7q}{7} &= \\frac{0}{7} \\\\\nq &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the equation $7qx = -5$ has no solutions if $q = 0$.\n\n**Note:** The constant $q$ in this problem is sometimes called a **parameter.**" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186205213 1064 "Consider the equation in the variable $x,$ given by\n\n$$\n7qx = -5,\n$$\n\nwhere $q$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solutions, what is the value of $q?$\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax = b{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{7q}_{\\large a} \\ x = \\underbrace{-5}_{\\large b}\n$$\n\nIn this case,\n\n$$\na = 7q, \\qquad b = -5.\n$$\n\nWe know that an equation of the form $ax=b$ with $b \\neq 0$ has no solutions when $a = 0.$\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n7q = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7q & =0 \\\\ \\frac{7q}{7} & =\\frac{0}{7} \\\\ q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the equation $7qx = -5$ has no solutions if $q = 0.$\n\n**Note:** The constant $q$ in this problem is sometimes called a **parameter.**\n\nSimilarly, the equation\n\n\\[\n(p + 3)x = -9,\n\\]\n\nwhere [math]p[/math] is a constant, is an equation in the form [math]ax = b{:}[/math] \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(p + 3)}_{\\large a} \\ x = \\underbrace{-9}_{\\large b},\n\\]\n\nIn this case,\n\n\\[\na = p + 3, \\qquad b = -9.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\\[\np + 3 = 0.\n\\]\n\nSolving this for [math]p,[/math] we get\n\\begin{align*}\np + 3 & = 0 \\\\[5pt]\np & = -3.\n\\end{align*}\n\nSo, the equation has no solution when [math]p=-3.[/math]" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186205214 1064 "In general, suppose we have an equation in the variable $x$ of the form\n\n$$\nax = b\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a$ and $b$ are constants, and\n- $b$ is *not* equal to zero, which we write as $b \\neq 0$.\n\nThen, if $a = 0$, the equation has **no solutions.**\n\nIndeed, by substituting $a = 0$ into $ax=b$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nax &= b \\\\\n(0)x &= b \\\\\n0 &= b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a *contradiction* (i.e., a statement that's always false) because we were told that\n\n$$\nb \\neq 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186205214 1064 "In general, suppose we have an equation in the variable $x$ of the form \n\n$$\nax = b,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $a$ and $b$ are constants, and\n\n- $b$ is *not* equal to zero, which we write as $b \\neq 0.$\n\nThen, if $a = 0,$ the equation has **no solutions.**\n\nIndeed, by substituting $a = 0$ into $ax=b,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax & =b \\\\ (0)x & =b \\\\ 0 & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a *contradiction* (i.e., a statement that's always false) because we were told that $b \\neq 0.$ Therefore, the equation has no solution." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186205310 1064 "In this lesson, we'll learn how to construct expressions that describe the zeros, maxima, and minima of transformed sine and cosine functions.\n\nFirst, let's recall the following facts:\n\n- The zeros of the sine function $y = \\sin x$ are given by\n$x = n\\pi$\nwhere $n$ is any integer.![](images/1d/1dfe4444da1a8f30db08c90de8efecbe1214125b1cfbf49c2ae8681edf3d10db.png)\n- The zeros of the cosine function $y = \\cos x$ are given by\n$x = \\dfrac\\pi2 + n\\pi$\nwhere $n$ is any integer.![](images/88/88378ece5b1721cdc533d07d69c004901539c7f8fda9a5a3e5c5648ed32905c5.png)\n\nLet's use these facts to find the zeros of a transformed sine or cosine function." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186205310 1064 "In this lesson, we'll learn how to construct expressions that describe the zeros, maxima, and minima of transformed sine and cosine functions.\n\nFirst, let's recall the following facts:\n\n- The zeros of the sine function $y = \\sin x$ are given by \n\n$$\nx = n\\pi\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer.\n\n![](images/1d/1dfe4444da1a8f30db08c90de8efecbe1214125b1cfbf49c2ae8681edf3d10db.png)\n\n- The zeros of the cosine function $y = \\cos x$ are given by \n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac\\pi2 + n\\pi\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer.\n\n![](images/88/88378ece5b1721cdc533d07d69c004901539c7f8fda9a5a3e5c5648ed32905c5.png)\n\nLet's use these facts to find the zeros of a transformed sine or cosine function." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186205311 1064 "Let's find a general expression that describes all of the zeros of\n\n$$\ny = 5\\cos \\left(2x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} \\right)\n$$\n\nFirst, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX = 2x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nSo, we now need to calculate the zeros of\n\n$$\ny = 5\\cos X\n$$\n\nAs we saw previously, the zeros of this function are given by the expression\n\n$$\nX = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + n\\pi\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer.\n\nSo, to find the zeros of our original function\n\n$$\ny = 5\\cos \\left(2x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} \\right)\n$$\n\nwe rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nX &= \\frac{π}{2} + nπ \\\\\n2x - \\frac{π}{3} &= \\frac{π}{2} + nπ \\\\\n2x &= \\frac{π}{2} + nπ + \\frac{π}{3} \\\\\n2x &= \\frac{5π}{6} + nπ \\\\\nx &= \\frac{5π}{12} + \\frac{nπ}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the zeros of\n\n$$\ny = 5\\cos \\left(2x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} \\right)\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{12} + \\dfrac{n\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer.\n\nLet's look at an example involving the sine function." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186205311 1064 "Let's find a general expression that describes all of the zeros of \n\n$$\ny = 5\\cos \\left( 2x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} \\right).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX = 2x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now need to calculate the zeros of \n\n$$\ny = 5\\cos X.\n$$\n\nAs we saw previously, the zeros of this function are given by the expression\n\n$$\nX = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + n\\pi,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer.\n\nSo, to find the zeros of our original function $y = 5\\cos \\left( 2x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} \\right),$ we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ 2x-\\frac{\\pi }{3} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{5\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the zeros of $y = 5\\cos \\left( 2x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} \\right)$ are given by \n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{12} + \\dfrac{n\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\n where $n$ is any integer.\n\nLet's look at an example involving the sine function." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186205312 1064 "We'll now turn our attention to the minima and maxima of transformed sine and cosine functions.\n\nFirst, recall the graph of $y=\\sin{x}$ once again.\n\n![](images/1d/1dfe4444da1a8f30db08c90de8efecbe1214125b1cfbf49c2ae8681edf3d10db.png)\n\nRecall that:\n\n- The function has a maximum when $x=\\dfrac\\pi 2$ and the function is periodic with period $2\\pi$. Therefore, a general expression for the $x$-values that maximize sine is\n$x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + 2n\\pi$.\n- The function has a minimum when $x=-\\dfrac\\pi 2$ and the function is periodic with period $2\\pi$. Therefore, a general expression for the $x$-values that minimize sine is\n\n$$\nx = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + 2n\\pi\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186205312 1064 "We'll now turn our attention to the minima and maxima of transformed sine and cosine functions. \n\nFirst, recall the graph of $y=\\sin{x}$ once again.\n\n![](images/1d/1dfe4444da1a8f30db08c90de8efecbe1214125b1cfbf49c2ae8681edf3d10db.png)\n\nRecall that:\n\n- The function has a maximum when $x=\\dfrac\\pi 2$ and the function is periodic with period $2\\pi.$ Therefore, a general expression for the $x$-values that maximize sine is\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + 2n\\pi.\n$$\n\n- The function has a minimum when $x=-\\dfrac\\pi 2$ and the function is periodic with period $2\\pi.$ Therefore, a general expression for the $x$-values that minimize sine is\n\n$$\nx = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + 2n\\pi.\n$$" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186205313 1064 "Now, recall the graph of $y=\\cos x{:}$\n\n![](images/88/88378ece5b1721cdc533d07d69c004901539c7f8fda9a5a3e5c5648ed32905c5.png)\n\n- The function has a maximum when $x=0$ and is periodic with period $2\\pi$. Therefore, a general expression for the $x$-values that maximize cosine is\n$x = 2n\\pi$.\n- The function has a minimum when $x=\\pi$ and is periodic with period $2\\pi$. Therefore, a general expression for the $x$-values that minimize cosine is\n\n$$\nx = \\pi + 2n\\pi\n$$\n\nLet's now apply these results to transformed sine and cosine functions." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186205313 1064 "Now, recall the graph of $y=\\cos x{:}$\n\n![](images/88/88378ece5b1721cdc533d07d69c004901539c7f8fda9a5a3e5c5648ed32905c5.png)\n\n- The function has a maximum when $x=0$ and is periodic with period $2\\pi.$ Therefore, a general expression for the $x$-values that maximize cosine is\n\n$$\nx = 2n\\pi.\n$$\n\n- The function has a minimum when $x=\\pi$ and is periodic with period $2\\pi.$ Therefore, a general expression for the $x$-values that minimize cosine is\n\n$$\nx = \\pi + 2n\\pi.\n$$\n\nLet's now apply these results to transformed sine and cosine functions." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186214219 1064 "Let's recall what we learned in a previous lesson about linear equations with infinitely many solutions.\n\nSuppose we have the linear equation\n\n$$\n2x + 5 = 2x + 5\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n- the coefficient of $x$ on both sides of the equation is $2$, and\n- the constant term on both sides of the equation is $5$.\n\nSince the coefficients of $x$ on both sides of the equation are equal, and the constants are also equal, it follows that this equation has *infinitely many solutions.* In fact, this equation is true for *any* value of $x$.\n\nMore generally, suppose we have the equation\n\n$$\nax + b = cx + d\n$$\n\nIf $a = c$ and $b = d$ are *both* true, then the equation is true for any value of $x$.\n\nThe goal of this lesson is to extend this idea to quadratic polynomials." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186214219 1064 "Let's recall what we learned in a previous lesson about linear equations with infinitely many solutions. \n\nSuppose we have the linear equation\n\n$$\n2x + 5 = 2x + 5.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n- the coefficient of $x$ on both sides of the equation is $2,$ and\n\n- the constant term on both sides of the equation is $5.$\n\nSince the coefficients of $x$ on both sides of the equation are equal, and the constants are also equal, it follows that this equation has *infinitely many solutions.* In fact, this equation is true for *any* value of $x.$\n\nMore generally, suppose we have the equation\n\n$$\nax + b = cx + d.\n$$\n\nIf $a = c$ and $b = d$ are *both* true, then the equation is true for any value of $x.$\n\nThe goal of this lesson is to extend this idea to quadratic polynomials." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186214229 1064 "Suppose we have two quadratic polynomials $P$ and $Q$, given by\n\n$$\nP(x) = ax^2 + bx + c \\quad \\text{and} \\quad Q(x) = dx^2 + ex + f\n$$\n\nIf these polynomials are equal for *all* values of $x$, we say that they're **identical.** This means their matching terms must be the same. In other words, we must have\n\n$$\na = d, \\qquad b = e, \\qquad c = f\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose we are given the equation\n\n$$\nax^2 + bx + c = x^2 + 5x + 4\n$$\n\nwhere $a, b$, and $c$ are fixed numbers, and we're told that this equation is true for all values of $x$.\n\nSince this polynomial equation is true for all values of $x$, we can match the coefficients.\n\n- The coefficient of $x^2$ on the left-hand side is $a$, and on the right-hand side it is $1$.\nSo, we have\n$a = 1$.\n- The coefficient of $x$ on the left-hand side is $b$, and on the right-hand side it is $5$.\nSo, we have\n$b = 5$.\n- The constant on the left-hand side is $c$, and on the right-hand side it is $4$.\nSo, we have\n$c = 4$.\n\nThis process is known as **equating coefficients.** It’s a useful tool whenever we know two polynomials are equal for all values of the variable.\n\nThis idea works for higher-degree polynomials too: when two polynomials are equal for all $x$, we can compare the coefficients of each power of $x$ and set them equal to each other." 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186214229 1064 "Suppose we have two quadratic polynomials $P$ and $Q,$ given by\n\n$$\nP(x) = ax^2 + bx + c \\quad \\text{and} \\quad Q(x) = dx^2 + ex + f.\n$$\n\nIf these polynomials are equal for *all* values of $x,$ we say that they're **identical.** This means their matching terms must be the same. In other words, we must have\n\n$$\na = d, \\qquad b = e, \\qquad c = f.\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose we are given the equation\n\n$$\nax^2 + bx + c = x^2 + 5x + 4\n$$\n\nwhere $a, b,$ and $c$ are fixed numbers, and we're told that this equation is true for all values of $x.$\n\nSince this polynomial equation is true for all values of $x$, we can match the coefficients.\n\n- The coefficient of $x^2$ on the left-hand side is $a$, and on the right-hand side it is $1.$\nSo, we have\n\n$$\na = 1.\n$$\n\n- The coefficient of $x$ on the left-hand side is $b$, and on the right-hand side it is $5.$\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nb = 5.\n$$\n\n- The constant on the left-hand side is $c$, and on the right-hand side it is $4.$\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nc = 4.\n$$\n\nThis process is known as **equating coefficients. **  It’s a useful tool whenever we know two polynomials are equal for all values of the variable.\n\nThis idea works for higher-degree polynomials too: when two polynomials are equal for all $x$, we can compare the coefficients of each power of $x$ and set them equal to each other." 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186218341 1064 "Recall that **Euler's number,** denoted $e$, is an irrational number whose decimal approximation is\n\n$$\ne\\approx2.718\\,281\\,828\n$$\n\nAs we'll discover, the exponential growth curve $y=e^x$ is one of the most important curves in mathematics. Its graph is given below.\n\n![](images/46/46ee2c3dbb420baffef373088d3dceeb340848538f5e5ecb0a62b29b7f5e7197.png)\n\nNow, suppose we want to sketch the graph of\n\n$$\ny=e^{x-3} + 1\n$$\n\nFirst, we shift it $3$ units to the right to get $y=e^{x-3}$. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to the point $(3,1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0$.\n\n![](images/e7/e7bc9047e665d024e7427fcf03515090d417917d9cd6a0ce8b2bc0305a0489de.png)\n\nThen, we vertically shift it by $1$ unit up to give $e^{x-3} + 1$. The point $(3,1)$ moves to $(3,2)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=1$.\n\n![](images/44/4481040a752ead29f53b4a7937a23dccfc85880ac0a4335a0619894b98cea0f4.png)" 13194139533435 17592186045585 false] [17592186218341 1064 "Recall that **Euler's number,** denoted $e,$ is an irrational number whose decimal approximation is\n\n$$\ne\\approx2.718\\,281\\,828.\n$$\n\nAs we'll discover, the exponential growth curve $y=e^x$ is one of the most important curves in mathematics. Its graph is given below.\n\n![](images/46/46ee2c3dbb420baffef373088d3dceeb340848538f5e5ecb0a62b29b7f5e7197.png)\n\nNow, suppose we want to sketch the graph of $y=e^{x-3} + 1.$ \n\nFirst, we shift it $3$ units to the right to get $y=e^{x-3}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to the point $(3,1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/e7/e7bc9047e665d024e7427fcf03515090d417917d9cd6a0ce8b2bc0305a0489de.png)\n\nThen, we vertically shift it by $1$ unit up to give $e^{x-3} + 1.$ The point $(3,1)$ moves to $(3,2)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=1.$\n\n![](images/44/4481040a752ead29f53b4a7937a23dccfc85880ac0a4335a0619894b98cea0f4.png)" 13194139533435 17592186045585 true] [17592186362409 1064 "We can still find a position vector by tracing a path from the origin to the point and adding the vectors along that path.\n\nHowever, if one part of the path points in the opposite direction to a known vector, then we write it as a **negative** multiple of that vector.\n\nFor example, consider the picture below. \n\n![](images/d5/d5366a68ed98e7f614ff998f69098613ea8e12d05bc27274ea66e8ce5a908cb7.png)\n\nWe are given that ![](images/80/80eb0b356da6e6a2315ea1466d34b914c6cad5e59ff07c4d061ea82b69ed9838.svg) and we are asked to find ![](images/44/44db815dcaf0a9d405026460a655495e055df7e55a785f